kb:BestPractice_BP_X1_ConformanceClaimDisclosureDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-X1: '0 violations' claims must name shape version AND measurement method in the same sentence"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-X1"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal_v1_0_0 (a domain ecosystem session); a domain ecosystem Assessment Baseline reported 0 violations under v3.2.0/per-file/stripped — canonical v2_0_13_3/merged/fixtures-excluded surfaced 8."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Any 'N violations' claim in a README, blueprint, assessment, or release note must name, in the SAME SENTENCE: (a) the shape file version it was measured under, (b) the imports policy (stripped / merged), (c) the corpus scope (per-file / merged), and (d) any exclusions (e.g., fixtures excluded).

The same corpus can produce wildly different conformance numbers under different methods. The difference is CONCEALED if the wording doesn't disclose it.

A reviewer must reject any conformance number that omits any of these four attributes. Detection signal: a conformance number stated without its method ('0 violations under redo_shacl' — which version? what corpus? fixtures included?). Remedy: the four-attribute disclosure is non-negotiable in any sentence asserting conformance.

Distinct from L-66 (governs SYNTHESIS extending a declared framework) and L-67 (governs SHAPE DESIGN for merged corpus); BP-X1 governs the REPORTING discipline — the wording of conformance claims themselves."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_66_DeclaredMeasurementFrameworksMustNotSilentlyExtend,
        kb:Lesson_L_67_ShapeTargetScopingMustConsiderMergedCorpus .

kb:BestPractice_D10_RiceDepFactorDecisionDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Use RICE+DepFactor scoring for prioritization decisions among multiple options"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D10"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session decision-discipline conversation. Frames adapted from RICE (Intercom product team) and SAFe WSJF (Reinertsen). DepFactor correction added for OE Pack's typical multi-option dependency structure. First applied: ranking 5 candidate options at session end (D > E > A > B > C)."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D10_RiceDepFactorDecisionDiscipline (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When facing 3+ candidate options for next work, score each option using RICE+DepFactor instead of vibes-based prioritization or raw probability×impact. The formula:

  Total = (Reach × Impact × Confidence / Effort) × DepFactor
  DepFactor = 1 + 0.5 × (number of other candidate options this enables)

Inputs (each grounded in observable session state, not guessed):
  Reach: countable downstream artifacts/sessions/scenarios this affects (integer)
  Impact: 0.25 (minimal) / 0.5 (low) / 1 (medium) / 2 (high) / 3 (massive)
  Confidence: 0.50 / 0.80 / 0.95 / 1.00 — explicit P(success); calibrated, not optimistic
  Effort: tool turns or person-hours (integer or half-step)

Honest discipline:
  1. Justify each input in 1-2 sentences citing observable session state. No bare numbers.
  2. After computing scores, run sensitivity analysis on the dominant input (usually Reach
     for new-capability options, Confidence for novel-implementation options). If the ranking
     flips under reasonable variation, the formula isn't decisive — escalate to human judgment.
  3. If two options score within 20% of each other, treat them as tied and break with secondary
     criteria (regression risk, reversibility, learning value).
  4. If the top option's score is dominated by a single unverifiable input (e.g., Reach=100
     'every future session'), document the asymmetry: small-downside-large-upside bets are
     defensible even when uncertain.
  5. Record the scoring run in the closure document so future sessions can audit and adjust.

Provenance: derived from RICE (Intercom, peer-reviewed in product management literature) plus an additive DepFactor correction for topological compounding when options have enabling dependencies. Documented to address the gap that raw P×I formulations cannot express dependencies, costs, or calibration of P explicitly.

Anti-pattern this prevents: 'I think Option X feels most impactful, let's do it.' Such reasoning hides the cost dimension, conflates probabilities, and is not auditable."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D11_RescoreAfterCompletion a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Re-score the option set after each completion; rankings are snapshots, not roadmaps"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D11"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session user dialogue. User asked: 'is D, E, A, B, C the steps of the next phases or are they just the current alternatives and we will decide after the completion of each one?' BP-D11 encodes the discipline that the latter is correct."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D11_RescoreAfterCompletion (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A RICE+DepFactor ranking (per BP-D10) is a snapshot of expected value-per-cost with current information at one point in time. It is NOT a committed roadmap. After each option completes, re-run the scoring on the remaining options before auto-executing the next item on the prior list.

Three reasons re-scoring is required:

(1) COMPLETING ONE OPTION CHANGES THE INPUTS FOR OTHERS. Reach, Impact, Confidence, Effort, and DepFactor can all shift. Example: if Option D ships a HOWTO that already covers most of Option E's scope, E's Effort drops from 3 to 1.5, and its score may rerank above options that previously beat it.

(2) NEW OPTIONS MAY EMERGE FROM COMPLETED WORK. The act of doing Option X often surfaces a previously-invisible Option F that wasn't in the original ranking. F may outscore everything previously listed.

(3) UNCERTAIN INPUTS MAY BE RESOLVED BY EVIDENCE. The original ranking estimated values like 'Reach=100 future sessions' that are unverifiable. After a completion, we may have empirical signal (e.g., the bootstrap kit was downloaded once and never referenced again, suggesting Reach was over-estimated). Update the inputs and re-rank.

Operational form (mandatory after every option completion):
  1. Update the option set: remove just-completed, add any newly-surfaced.
  2. Re-justify each remaining option's R/I/C/E values with current information.
  3. Re-compute DepFactor based on the updated option graph.
  4. Compute new Total scores; produce a ranking.
  5. Append a new section to the decision record (Decision_Record_Options_*.md)      with the date, the prior ranking, the updated ranking, and what changed.
  6. Decide based on the NEW ranking, not the prior one.

Anti-pattern this prevents: 'We ranked D > E > A > B > C in the original session, so after D completes we automatically do E next.' This treats stale information as current and bypasses the discipline that made the original ranking defensible.

Provenance: surfaced by user feedback during the BP-D10 application, when the user asked whether D > E > A > B > C represented committed phases or current alternatives. The honest answer was 'current alternatives, not committed phases', but BP-D10 alone does not enforce this. BP-D11 closes the gap by making re-scoring a mandatory step."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D10_RiceDepFactorDecisionDiscipline> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D10_RiceDepFactorDecisionDiscipline .

kb:BestPractice_D12_RederiveOptionsFromLatestClosure a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "When re-scoring per BP-D11, also re-derive the option set from the latest closure document with adversarial attention"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D12"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session honest status analysis (Honest_Status_Analysis_v1_0_0.md §5). Self-derived: BP-D12 is the discipline-correction the v1.0.2 re-evaluation needed in order to be procedurally complete. Encoding it ensures the same failure mode does not recur in future sessions."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D12_RederiveOptionsFromLatestClosure (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """BP-D11 says 'recompute scores for remaining options after each completion.' But re-scoring an INCOMPLETE option set is procedurally correct yet substantively wrong. The option-discovery step is not mechanical — it requires fresh, adversarial reading of the most recent closure document or status artifact, specifically scrutinizing honest disclosures and open items for implied options.

Operational form (mandatory after every option completion, paired with BP-D11):
  1. Read the latest closure document or status artifact RAW (not from memory).
  2. For each honest disclosure, ask: 'does this disclosure imply an option that
     could be executed?' If yes, that's a candidate option.
  3. For each open-items entry, ask: 'is this option's scope still unchanged?
     Has the just-completed work changed any of its inputs?'
  4. Add newly-derived candidate options to the option set BEFORE running BP-D11
     re-scoring. The set must be re-derived first; the formula is applied second.
  5. If the prior re-evaluation claimed 'no new options surfaced' without doing
     this re-derivation, that prior re-evaluation is procedurally incomplete.

Anti-pattern this prevents: Mechanically re-scoring the same option set across
successive iterations while honest disclosures in closure documents quietly
imply higher-scoring options that never get visible to the formula. The formula
selects from candidates; it cannot select what isn't a candidate.

Concrete example from this session: After Option E (Session closure) shipped,
the v1.0.2 BP-D11 re-evaluation re-scored A, B, C with 'no new options' claim,
recommending STOP. A subsequent honest re-reading of Session_Closure §7
disclosures revealed three new options:
  - §7.1 (4 non-conformant zips) → Option G (AMOD v1.4.2 amendment)
  - §7.5 ('pre-loader, not enforcer') → Option H (automate enforcement)
  - §2 listing 4 zips when reality has 5 → Option I (closure §2 update)
Plus the meta-option K — encode BP-D12 itself, scoring 213.75 (Reach=100 because
every future BP-D11 application benefits). H scored 45.00 — both far higher than
B's 4.05 which v1.0.2 had recommended. Without BP-D12, the session would have
stopped without doing the highest-value work. With BP-D12 encoded, future sessions
are protected from the same failure mode.

Provenance: surfaced when user asked for an honest status analysis after the
v1.0.2 re-evaluation. The user's request itself triggered the adversarial re-read
that exposed the missed options. BP-D12 makes that adversarial re-read mandatory
in BP-D11's procedure rather than dependent on user intervention."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D11_RescoreAfterCompletion>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D11_RescoreAfterCompletion .

kb:BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "When shipping a release bundle, distinguish per-bundle SemVer from ecosystem SemVer; consolidated releases inherit the ecosystem version lineage"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D13"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session honest version-management investigation. The user asked 'is the version naming correct?' which triggered raw-file inspection of Phase closure documents and revealed oepack-final-v1_0_0.zip violated the established v17.X.Y lineage. BP-D13 makes the lineage discipline explicit so the same failure mode does not recur."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """BP-D6 mandates AMOD-conformant naming on every produced file. BP-D7 mandates strict BP-3 version semantics with versionInfo + versionIRI alignment. Together they govern how each artifact is versioned. But neither addresses the question: *which* SemVer should this release use — a fresh per-bundle version (starting at v1.0.0) or a continuation of an ecosystem version lineage (e.g., v17.X.Y)?

TWO LEGITIMATE PATTERNS exist in OE Pack practice:

  1. PER-BUNDLE SemVer — fresh version starting at v1.0.0. Used for new artifact      types with no prior version: audit zips, decision records, tooling bundles,      ad-hoc deliverables. Examples: oepack-bootstrap-v1_0_0.zip,      oepack-decision-record-v1_0_3.zip, oepack-discipline-and-tooling-v1_0_0.zip.

  2. ECOSYSTEM SemVer — continues an established X.Y.Z lineage. Used for      consolidated releases that succeed a previously-shipped ecosystem release.      Examples: oepack-family-v17.0.0.zip → oepack-family-v17_1_0.zip (MINOR bump      because Phases 1+2+3 added deliverables without breaking v17.0.0 baseline).

BOTH patterns are AMOD-conformant — configuration:ProjectArchiveConvention's regex (^oepack-{scope}-v\\d+_\\d+_\\d+\\.zip$) accepts v1_0_0 and v17_1_0 equally. The regex is version-AGNOSTIC; the choice of semantics depends on whether the bundle continues an existing lineage.

OPERATIONAL FORM (mandatory before naming any release zip):
  1. Ask: 'Does a predecessor release of this scope exist?'
     - If a predecessor with matching scope-label exists in the ecosystem, this        is a CONTINUATION → use ecosystem SemVer (bump from predecessor's version).
     - If this is a brand-new artifact type with no prior version, this is an        ORIGINATION → use per-bundle SemVer (start at v1_0_0).
  2. For continuations, identify the predecessor by SHA-256 (from prior      release manifests) and declare it in the bundle's README and CHANGELOG.
  3. The bump magnitude (PATCH/MINOR/MAJOR) follows BP-D7 / BP-3:
     - PATCH: bug fixes, no vocabulary or structural changes
     - MINOR: additive changes (new files, new vocabulary, no breaking changes)
     - MAJOR: breaking changes (removed axioms, changed semantics, deleted files)
  4. The scope label MUST match the predecessor exactly.      'oepack-family-v17.0.0' → 'oepack-family-v17_1_0'  ✅      'oepack-family-v17.0.0' → 'oepack-final-v1_0_0'    ❌ (changed scope label      AND restarted version). Both errors visible only via lineage check, not regex.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: Conflating 'first time I'm building this kind of bundle' (justifies v1_0_0) with 'first time anyone's built a consolidated release' (does NOT justify v1_0_0 if a predecessor in the lineage exists).

CONCRETE EXAMPLE from this session: After Phases 1+2+3 + this session's work, the consolidated release was first shipped as oepack-final-v1_0_0.zip. That name was AMOD-conformant by regex but BROKE the v17.X.Y ecosystem lineage that Phase 1/2/3 closure documents had explicitly declared as the target output ('Compliance frame: OE Pack v17.0.0 → v17.1.0'). Honest remediation: rename to oepack-family-v17_1_0.zip with explicit predecessor declaration. The mistake was procedurally complete (all gates passed) but substantively wrong — exactly the failure mode BP-D13 prevents.

VERIFICATION: A new release_check Gate G can detect this by parsing the README's Predecessor declaration and comparing against the bundle's filename version-stem. Where no predecessor is declared, the bundle is asserted as ORIGINATION; this should be cross-checked against any existing prior bundles with the same scope."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D6_AMODNamingOnCreation>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D7_StrictVersionSemantics> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:OEPACK_BP3_PatchSemantics .

kb:BestPractice_D14_DerivativeArchiveDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "When shipping a derivative of a source release (subset, superset, or focused-use bundle), name it with its own ORIGINATION lineage and declare its source via configuration:derivedFrom — do not reuse the source bundle's scope label or version"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D14"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session honest convention question: 'should sub-packages reuse family-v17.1.1 in their names?' which triggered raw inspection of BP-D13 and AMOD v1.4.1 conventions. Verified that derivation as a third category was implicitly handled but not encoded. BP-D14 closes that gap."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D14_DerivativeArchiveDiscipline (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """BP-D13 introduced two release-naming patterns: CONTINUATION (same scope, next version) and ORIGINATION (new scope, fresh v1_0_0). A third real-world case fits neither cleanly: DERIVATION — a bundle whose contents are a subset or recombination of a source release, packaged separately for a focused use case (e.g., 'engineering-kit' from 'family', 'methodology' from 'family').

DERIVATIONS are NOT continuations: they don't add the next version of the source's contents — they re-package a subset. Their scope label MUST differ from the source (else Gate G correctly fails them as scope-mismatch continuations). Their version starts at v1_0_0 in their own scope's lineage.

DERIVATIONS are NOT pure originations either: they have a real semantic relationship to the source release. Documenting only via 'this is a new artifact, no predecessor' loses the source citation that academic and audit users need.

OPERATIONAL FORM (mandatory before naming any derivative bundle):
  1. Ask: 'Are the contents a subset/recombination of an existing source bundle?'
     - If yes → DERIVATION. Use a new scope label (e.g., 'engineering-kit', 'reference', 'methodology').
     - If no → CONTINUATION (BP-D13) or ORIGINATION (BP-D13).
  2. The derivative's scope label MUST be different from the source's scope label.
     'oepack-family-v17_1_1' (source) → 'oepack-engineering-kit-v1_0_0' (derivative) ✅
     'oepack-family-v17_1_1' (source) → 'oepack-family-engineering-kit-v17_1_1' (compound scope) ❌ — implies family-lineage continuation that is not the case.
  3. The derivative's version starts at v1_0_0 (per BP-D13 ORIGINATION semantics).
  4. The README MUST declare the source via 'Derived from:' (NOT 'Predecessor:'). Predecessor is reserved for same-scope continuations.
  5. The TTL inside the derivative SHOULD reference the source's IRI via configuration:derivedFrom (vocabulary-grounded, not just prose).
  6. Future re-derivations from a newer source (e.g., from family v17.2.0) bump the derivative's version per its OWN lineage (engineering-kit v1_0_0 → v1_1_0 if the underlying contents change in additive way).

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: Naming derivatives with the source's scope+version (e.g., 'oepack-family-engineering-kit-v17_1_1.zip'). This implies family-lineage membership, which is wrong: derivatives are not continuations of the family release. Gate G would mis-classify such a name as either CONTINUATION (failing scope match) or ORIGINATION-with-confusing-name.

CONCRETE EXAMPLE from this session: After family v17.1.1 shipped, sub-packages were created for engineering, reference, methodology, phases, prompt-guide use cases. First draft used 'Predecessor:' to reference family v17.1.1 — Gate G correctly flagged scope mismatch. Honest fix: use 'Derived from:' and start fresh ORIGINATION lineages. BP-D14 makes this discipline explicit and adds vocabulary (configuration:derivedFrom) so the relationship is machine-checkable, not just prose.

VERIFICATION: A future Gate G extension (Gate G2 or H) can read configuration:derivedFrom from the bundle's manifest TTL and verify (a) the source bundle is named in a known release, (b) the derivative's scope label is different from the source's, (c) the derivative's version starts at v1_0_0 in its own lineage. Until then, README 'Derived from:' prose serves as the human-readable convention."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D6_AMODNamingOnCreation> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage .

kb:BestPractice_D15_StagingArchiveDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "When shipping a bundle whose contents are intended for future integration into an ecosystem release, declare the integration target in the README and (where possible) via configuration:integratesInto in the manifest"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D15"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session honest convention question: 'oepack-discipline-and-tooling-v1_1_1 doesn't give any information about oepack-family-v17_1_1 — is this a rule bypass?' Confirmed via raw inspection that the bundle is a correct CONTINUATION (not a rule bypass) but reveals a real gap: the convention has no rule for declaring integration targets. BP-D15 closes that gap."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D15_StagingArchiveDiscipline (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """BP-D13 covered CONTINUATION (same scope, next version) and ORIGINATION (new scope, fresh v1_0_0). BP-D14 covered DERIVATION (subset/recombination of a source release, separate lineage). A fourth real-world case fits none of these cleanly: STAGING — a bundle authored as work-in-progress for a future ecosystem release, technically a CONTINUATION of its own scope but informationally incomplete because its filename gives no hint of its eventual integration target.

STAGING bundles ARE CONTINUATIONS of their own scope. Their predecessor is the prior version of that scope, NOT the future ecosystem release. They satisfy BP-D13 correctly. But a reader looking at the filename alone cannot tell that the contents are queued for integration into (e.g.) family v17.x.y.

OPERATIONAL FORM (mandatory before naming any bundle whose contents are integration-targeted):
  1. Ask: 'Will the contents of this bundle be merged into a future ecosystem release?'
     - If yes → STAGING. Continue using the bundle's own scope lineage (per BP-D13), AND declare the integration target.
     - If no → plain CONTINUATION (BP-D13) or ORIGINATION (BP-D13) or DERIVATION (BP-D14).
  2. Declare the integration target in the README under 'Integrates into:' (NOT 'Predecessor:' or 'Derived from:').
     Example: 'Integrates into: oepack-family-v17_1_2.zip (planned)'.
  3. Where the bundle ships an OWL ontology or manifest, encode the target via configuration:integratesInto (introduced in AMOD v1.4.3).
  4. When the integration ships (e.g., family v17.1.2 absorbs the staging contents), the staging bundle is SUPERSEDED. Its own lineage may continue independently for future staging cycles.
  5. Staging bundles SHOULD NOT be the canonical form for production use — once integrated, the integrated release is canonical.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: Shipping a staging bundle whose name gives no hint of its integration target, then having a future reader (or future Claude session) wonder where the contents fit. Filename-only inspection should at least suggest the bundle's role in the broader release pipeline.

CONCRETE EXAMPLE from this session: oepack-discipline-and-tooling-v1_1_1.zip ships BP-D14 + AMOD v1.4.2 — additive vocabulary that will become part of a future family v17.1.2 release. Per BP-D13, the v1_1_1 bundle is a correct CONTINUATION of the discipline-and-tooling lineage. But its README does not declare 'Integrates into: family v17.1.2'. A reader encountering only the filename has no way to know the contents are integration-targeted. BP-D15 makes this declaration mandatory.

VERIFICATION: A future Gate G3 (or extension to Gate G) can read configuration:integratesInto from the bundle's manifest and (a) verify the target is named, (b) cross-check whether the integrated release has shipped, (c) flag the staging bundle as SUPERSEDED once the integration ships. Until then, README 'Integrates into:' prose serves as the human-readable convention.

RELATIONSHIP TO BP-D14: BP-D14 covers the inverse direction (derivative → source). BP-D15 covers staging → target. configuration:integratesInto is the inverse of an implicit 'is-integrated-from' relation; in practice, the integrated release does NOT need to enumerate which staging bundles it absorbed (that's documented in CHANGELOG), so configuration:integratesInto is one-directional from staging to target."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D14_DerivativeArchiveDiscipline> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
