        kb:BestPractice_D14_DerivativeArchiveDiscipline .

kb:BestPractice_D16_ShaclOwlDirectionCrossCheck a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D16: Cross-check SHACL shape directions against OWL property declarations"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D16"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D16_ShaclOwlDirectionCrossCheck (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 2.1 closure 2026-05-09 — OQC SHACL conformance defect identified at 74 findings; reduced to 0 via shape-direction repair, not data authoring. The defect was systematic enough to warrant encoding the cross-check as discipline."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "When BP-D16 is applied during shape authoring, OWL/SHACL alignment is verified up-front. When BP-D16 is applied during shape review (re-checking existing shapes), the cross-check identifies shape defects without expensive trial-and-error PATCHing. Pairs strongly with BP-D4 (run BOTH HermiT and SHACL): the disagreement between the two reasoners on a property's direction is itself a signal that BP-D16 cross-check is needed."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Before authoring or modifying SHACL shapes (especially sh:NodeShape with sh:property + sh:path), 
cross-check that each property used in a shape's path is declared in the OWL TBox with a domain 
compatible with the shape's sh:targetClass. A SHACL constraint of the form:

  :MyShape sh:targetClass :ClassA ; sh:property [ sh:path :p ; sh:minCount 1 ] .

implicitly expects :p to relate :ClassA instances to something. If OWL declares 
':p rdfs:domain :ClassB' (with :ClassA disjoint from or unrelated to :ClassB), then the shape 
will either:
  (a) report apparent violations on data that's actually well-formed (false negatives), OR
  (b) silently auto-type instances as :ClassB via OWL domain propagation when the shape is 
      "satisfied" via authoring data, leading to HermiT INCONSISTENT or unintended class inferences.

The cross-check: for each (sh:targetClass T, sh:path P) pair in a shape, verify that 
P's rdfs:domain (or super-domain via subPropertyOf) includes T. If not, either the shape's 
direction is wrong (the inverse property should be tested) or the OWL declaration needs repair."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl .

kb:BestPractice_D17_CoordinatedReleaseDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D17: Coordinated Release Discipline (multi-bundle releases)"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D17"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D17_CoordinatedReleaseDiscipline (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 2.1 Sprint C closure 2026-05-09 — surfaced as Sprint C disclosure item #6 ('two bundles in BP-D9 release'). The disclosure was honest but exposed a real convention gap. BP-D17 encodes the resolution."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Without BP-D17, multi-bundle coordinated releases (which are common when a family-level change cascades to dependent derivative bundles) would appear to violate BP-D9. BP-D17 encodes the legitimate exception explicitly. When applying BP-D17, the closure report MUST cite all constituent bundle SHAs to prevent split-release ambiguity."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A "coordinated release" is a single logical change that requires multiple bundle deliverables 
to ship together (e.g., a family release + its dependent subjects re-derivation, or a discipline-
catalogue update + a release_check tooling update). When a release is coordinated:

1. Each constituent bundle is fully self-validating (passes 7-gate verification independently).
2. Each bundle's README explicitly cites the other(s) as part of the coordinated release, with 
   their SHA-256 hashes for cross-reference integrity.
3. All constituent bundles share the same release date and ship together (no split shipments).
4. The coordinated release as a whole is documented in a closure report referencing each bundle.
5. BP-D9 (present only the final release bundle) is satisfied per-bundle: each is presented 
   exactly once, no loose copies. The "set" of coordinated bundles is the unit of release.

This complements BP-D9 rather than violating it: BP-D9 prevents loose-copy confusion within a 
single release; BP-D17 acknowledges that some logical changes are too large for a single bundle 
and require coordinated multi-bundle delivery."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D9_PresentBundleNotLoose .

kb:BestPractice_D18_LangTagOnXsdStringInconsistency a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D18: A lang-tagged literal on a DatatypeProperty whose rdfs:range is xsd:string causes HermiT-INCONSISTENT under OWL DL"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D18"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "L-34 (Phase 2.9 root-cause analysis); release_preflight_v1_0_1.py P1 pattern."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D18_LangTagOnXsdStringInconsistency (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """In RDF, the value space of xsd:string excludes language-tagged literals; lang-tagged literals belong to rdf:langString, a disjoint datatype. When a DatatypeProperty declares rdfs:range xsd:string but ABox triples carry @lang-tagged literals on it, OWL DL is forced to assert that those literal values are simultaneously instances of xsd:string and rdf:langString — a contradiction that makes the ontology HermiT-INCONSISTENT.

Two common authoring contexts where this slips in:
  (1) Verbatim textual fields (rev:verbatimUserTurn, rdfs:comment-like properties) declared with rdfs:range xsd:string for compactness, then populated with @en for documentation. xsd:string declarations are inconsistent with @en literals.
  (2) Mechanical-tag conversion: an author intending xsd:string switches to rdf:langString by adding @en, without revising the rdfs:range declaration.

HermiT's --ignoreUnsupportedDatatypes flag does NOT mask this — HermiT correctly reports INCONSISTENT because both xsd:string and rdf:langString are in the OWL 2 datatype map. The pattern was nonetheless masked in Phase 2.8 because (a) the verification pipeline excluded SHACL files from HermiT load, and SHACL-shape rdfs:range declarations are what propagate the constraint to the affected ABox literals, and (b) the resulting ABox-only HermiT run had no closing rdfs:range to contradict.

Operational form:
  - Authoring: choose xsd:string OR rdf:langString explicitly; never mix.
  - For multilingual text use rdfs:Literal as range (accepts both) or rdf:langString (mandates lang tag).
  - For machine-keyed strings, use xsd:string and DO NOT add lang tags to instances.
  - Verification: release_preflight_v1_0_1.py P1 catches the pattern by scanning DP rdfs:range declarations and cross-referencing literal values.

Distinguishable defect class from P2 (datatype mismatch, e.g., "PT3H"^^xsd:string when range is xsd:duration). P1 = lang-tag-vs-xsd:string-range; P2 = wrong xsd:datatype-vs-declared-range."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D3_DirectJavaHermiT>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl>,
        <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#LessonLearned_34_PipelineMaskingOfLangTagInconsistency> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:LessonLearned_34_PipelineMaskingOfLangTagInconsistency .

kb:BestPractice_D19_EquivalentClassDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D19: owl:equivalentClass Usage Discipline — Nuanced Position"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D19"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Captured 2026-05-14 from the actual step-12 OEE-Alignment retirement position (per BP-D2 + L-42 transcript verification). Step 12 dropped 11 owl:equivalentClass axioms because their endpoints in <quality-criteria:> namespace had been retired at step 8, making the axioms MOOT — not because equivalentClass was prevented as a strategy. Earlier sessions (Feb 2026 'Alternative D' iterations) actively USED equivalentClass to align a reliability ontology + OQC quality classes. The discipline that emerged: equivalentClass is appropriate when semantic identity is real and both endpoints live; subClassOf is preferred for related-but-distinct; moot axioms are dropped, not preserved. Authored after user-directed transcript verification to avoid encoding a (mistaken) 'equivalentClass is banned' framing as a BP."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """owl:equivalentClass is a legitimate axiom for cross-subject class alignment, NOT a banned strategy. The nuanced discipline:

(a) USE owl:equivalentClass when both endpoints exist in the active corpus AND the concepts are semantically identical (same domain meaning, same intended extension, no subset-of-the-other relationship). Example: pre-consolidation cross-namespace duplicates that need to be formally bridged.

(b) PREFER rdfs:subClassOf when concepts are 'related but distinct' (one is a specialization, one is broader, or the extensions overlap but are not identical). Example: a reliability ontology's Reliability is strictly richer than OQC's Reliability — use redo:Reliability rdfs:subClassOf qc:QualityDimension, not equivalentClass.

(c) DROP/RETIRE the axiom when an endpoint is retired during consolidation. A moot axiom (one whose endpoint no longer exists) is not 'live' even if its other endpoint exists — it's dangling, and should be removed from the active corpus.

(d) VERIFY BOTH ENDPOINTS per L-42 before classifying any equivalentClass axiom as live, moot, or migrating.

(e) NEVER use owl:equivalentClass to consolidate undeclared-class drift (e.g., do NOT add 'kb:Lesson owl:equivalentClass kb:LessonLearned' to bridge an undeclared class with a declared class — re-type the individuals instead). Use equivalentClass for legitimate cross-subject semantic identity, not for fixing type-assertion drift."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_42_VerifyBothSidesOfRelationship .

kb:BestPractice_D1_AskBeforeAssuming a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Ask for missing files before starting"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D1"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D1_AskBeforeAssuming (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "User instruction restated across multiple turns: 'Ask for missing files before you start if there are missing files or not available in your local environment.'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Before any work that depends on specific files, run an explicit availability check: list the files needed, ls them on disk, and ask the user only if a needed file is missing. Never fabricate file content, never assume version numbers, never proceed past a missing dependency. Ground every claim in files actually present in /mnt/user-data/uploads, /home/claude/handoff, /home/claude/step0_outputs, or /mnt/user-data/outputs.

Operational form: at the start of every non-trivial task, output a 'Pre-flight: file availability check' section that:
  1. Lists each required file by purpose
  2. Runs ls on each path
  3. Stops and asks the user if anything is missing
  4. Proceeds only after all dependencies are confirmed present

Verified to prevent: fabricated import paths, mismatched version-IRI references, claims about file content the model never actually read."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D21_MetricUmbrellaVsSpecificPlacement a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D21: Domain-agnostic metric umbrellas live in oe-measurement; domain-specific metric subclasses live in the subject that encodes them."@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D21"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Convention implicit in F-phase consolidation; codified 2026-05-15 after user challenge during measurement-vocabulary review surfaced that the AI misunderstood where metric classes lived. Codification prevents future recurrence."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When placing a metric CLASS in the ecosystem, follow this convention consistent with how the F-phase corpus is structured:

  - Generic / domain-agnostic UMBRELLA metric classes (SizeMetric, CohesionMetric, LLMReliabilityMetric, ReliabilityMetric, etc.) live in oe-measurement. They classify by METRIC KIND, independent of what is being measured.

  - DOMAIN-SPECIFIC metric subclasses (HallucinationRate, MTBF, ZombieClaimRate, ClassRichness) live in the SUBJECT whose semantics they encode. HallucinationRate encodes LLM behavior semantics, so it lives in the subject covering LLM behavior (reliability, given the current corpus structure). MTBF encodes reliability-engineering semantics, so it lives in reliability.

  - Metric INDIVIDUALS (ThresholdRows, specific MTBF_8760h values, configured metric instances) follow the metric class's subject, OR live in a dedicated configuration ontology (oqc-thresholds, oqc-measurements).

Rationale: per L-47 / L-47v2 — concepts belong in the subject whose semantics they encode. Umbrellas are generic; specifics encode domain semantics. Placing HallucinationRate in oe-measurement would conflate generic metric-vocabulary with LLM-domain semantics, violating L-47.

Test before adding a metric class: "Does this metric's definition reference concepts from a specific domain (LLM behavior, reliability engineering, ontology structure, performance)?" If yes, place in that domain subject. If no (purely abstract metric structure), place in oe-measurement.

Empirical basis: convention was implicit in F-phase consolidation. During 2026-05-15 review, surfaced that "28 LLM metric classes live in oe-measurement" was wrong — they live in reliability with umbrellas in oe-measurement. Codified to prevent future re-conflation."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification .

kb:BestPractice_D25_CompetencyQuestionClassification a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D25: Subdivide CompetencyQuestion into OperationalizableCompetencyQuestion + DescriptiveCompetencyQuestion before reporting CQ-coverage metrics"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D25"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.30.0 industrial-hardening item #8. Triggered by v17.27.0 readiness assessment which identified 9 \"descriptive-only\" CQs contributing to a misleading 78.6% naive coverage."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_25_DesignIntentExtractionIsNotFabrication,
        kb:OEPACK_BP4_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """CQ coverage metrics are misleading without classification. A bare "33/42 = 78.6% CQ coverage" reading invites two errors:

  (1) UNDERSTATING capability: design-rationale CQs ("Why do X and Y       coexist?") and session-context CQs ("What should this archive be       named?") cannot be SPARQL-operationalized in principle; counting       them against a SPARQL-coverage denominator falsely makes the       ontology look weaker than it is.

  (2) METRIC-GAMING pressure: chasing 100% coverage tempts authors to       fabricate SPARQL for inherently-prose questions, violating L-40.

Discipline:

  1. SUBCLASS core:CompetencyQuestion into:
     - core:OperationalizableCompetencyQuestion: must have        quality:hasSPARQLImplementation
     - core:DescriptiveCompetencyQuestion: explicitly does NOT require        SPARQL; answer lives in skos:definition

  2. Classify EVERY CQ. Unclassified CQs default to      OperationalizableCompetencyQuestion (the safe default — must have      SPARQL).

  3. Subtype DescriptiveCompetencyQuestion via dcterms:source narrative      (e.g., "DesignRationale", "Procedural", "SessionContext").

  4. Enforce via SHACL: core:OperationalizableCQShape requires      quality:hasSPARQLImplementation minCount 1 on every      OperationalizableCompetencyQuestion target.

  5. REPORT both metrics:
     - Naive coverage (SPARQL-having / total CQ)
     - Honest coverage (SPARQL-having / Operationalizable subset)

Effect in v17.30.0:
  - Naive: 33/42 (78.6%) → 39/42 (92.9%)
  - Honest: undefined / unclassified → 39/39 (100%)
  - 6 CQs operationalized via shape-graph SPARQL (per L-49)
  - 3 CQs explicitly reclassified as DescriptiveCQ"""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual .

kb:BestPractice_D33_ProvenanceTaggingForMixedSourceContent a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D33: Mixed-source content MUST carry explicit provenance at both ontology and page levels"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D33"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Extends BP-D24 (ontology-level PROV) to content-level mixed-source tagging."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D24_OntologyLevelLicenseAndProvenance,
        kb:BestPractice_D31_PrimarySourceConceptCoverageGate ;
    skos:definition """When a Stage 4 instructional surface combines primary-source content (textbook chapter) with secondary literature (PMBOK, contemporary papers), provenance MUST be explicit at both ontology and page levels.

Source incident: COM0463 Ch.5 v2.0.0. Marchewka's 6 estimation methods (Guesstimating, Time-Boxing, Top-Down, Bottom-Up, Analogous, Parametric) and PMBOK extensions (PERT, COCOMO II, Function Points) and contemporary additions (Planning Poker, ML) had been conflated in v1.x. v2.0.0 introduced a SourceProvenance taxonomy plus a hasProvenance property + visual badges (green=Marchewka, orange=PMBOK, purple=contemporary).

Procedure:
  Ontology layer: Declare SourceProvenance subclasses (one per source tier). Declare     hasSourceProvenance ObjectProperty. Tag every mixed-source individual.
  Page layer: Use visually-distinct provenance badges (colour-coded + text label, not     relying on colour alone). Group primary-source content into its own section;     secondary in "Beyond [PrimarySource]".

Anti-pattern prevented: Presenting PMBOK content as if it were the course textbook's content. Students preparing for exams assess the wrong tier."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D35_StageLevelArtifactKindSpecCoordination a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D35: When SHACL violation focusNode resolves to a discipline-level entity (ArtifactKindSpec, Genre), first verify the data graph includes the file declaring that entity — apparent violations may be measurement artefacts"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D35"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Reframes earlier mis-attribution per L-40 honest reframe."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:BestPractice_D5_ValidateWithImportsMerged,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """When SHACL validation appears to fail on an ArtifactKindSpec entity, the apparent violation may be a measurement artefact rather than a real defect — the data graph must include both the spec TTL AND the consumer ABox.

Source incident: COM0463 Ch.5 v1.0.0..v2.1.0 — across 4 pipeline releases, "CE-10/CE-11 carryover" violations on a discipline-level ArtifactKindSpec entity (a minCount constraint on a hasReferenceTemplateArtifact property) appeared to persist. Investigation in v6.1.0 closure revealed the violations only appeared when the page ABox was loaded WITHOUT the discipline TTL; when both files were loaded together, the spec already had 4 reference templates and conformed cleanly. The "carryover" was a false positive from incomplete validation.

Procedure:
  1. When SHACL violation's sh:focusNode resolves to a discipline-level entity      (ArtifactKindSpec, Genre, etc.) rather than the consumer ABox's individual:
     - First check whether the data graph includes the file that DECLARES the focus        node entity.
     - If not, load it and re-validate. The "violation" may disappear.
  2. If the focus node IS in the loaded graph and still fails, then it's a real defect.
  3. Document the load-completeness check explicitly in gate-evidence logs.

Anti-pattern prevented: Re-attempting to "fix" SHACL violations that are measurement artefacts. The pipeline author repeats the same investigation across N releases.

Per L-40 honest reframe: an earlier interpretation of this BP attempted to explain it as "discipline-level violations can't be fixed by pipeline releases". That framing was itself based on the measurement-artefact false positive. The reframed BP-D35 captures the actual lesson: validate complete data graph; load discipline + consumer together."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D42_AssessmentVocabularyDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D42: AssessmentVocabulary Discipline"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D42"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T20:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.3.0 Phase F4. Lesson from F2 mid-execution SHACL fail-stop (V=48 → V=0 in-place fix). Codified per BP-D4 dual-gate discipline."@en ;
    skos:definition "BP-D42: Assessment vocabulary discipline — when adding new metric or quality dimension classes, each class MUST carry: (a) skos:definition with verifiable scope; (b) dcterms:source citing external framework (OQuaRE/OntoQA/OOPS!/FAIR/ISO 25010/IEEE 828) or explicit OE-pack-internal rationale; (c) rdfs:isDefinedBy pointing to its owning subject namespace; (d) cross-subject rdfs:seeAlso link if a corresponding class exists in a sibling subject (per L-47 discipline). Detection: classes without skos:definition fire SHACL violations under the 'Measurement class must have skos:definition' shape. Mitigation: include all 4 required annotations at class creation time; do not split assessment-vocabulary additions across multiple commits without completing each class's metadata."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl,
        kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification .

kb:BestPractice_D43_FrameworkABoxCitationDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D43: FrameworkABoxCitation Discipline"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D43"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T20:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.3.0 Phase F4. Codified pattern from F2 (6 Frameworks) and F3 (OOPS! dual citation paper + live URL fetched 2026-05-19)."@en ;
    skos:definition "BP-D43: Framework ABox citation discipline — when populating quality:Framework or any framework-catalogue ABox individual, every individual MUST carry: (a) BP-D41-verified source citation in dcterms:source (with year, venue, page numbers, DOI if available); (b) rdfs:seeAlso pointing to authoritative URL; (c) dcterms:date for the source publication date. Live-catalogue sources (e.g., oops.linkeddata.es) MUST be dual-cited: stable paper citation PLUS live URL with fetch timestamp. Detection: Framework individuals with empty dcterms:source. Mitigation: never add a Framework individual from memory; always BP-D41-verify via web_search or web_fetch before populating."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources .

kb:BestPractice_D45_CharacteristicAggregationDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D45: Characteristic Aggregation Discipline"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D45"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T21:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.4.0 (Proposal I Phase I1). Lesson from F6 honest gap audit showing F5 had no characteristic aggregation despite Proposal G's claimed architecture."@en ;
    skos:definition "BP-D45: When implementing OQuaRE characteristic-level scores, the aggregation formula MUST be traceable to Duque-Ramos 2016 Table 3 (metric-characteristic mapping) and the constituent metric scores MUST come from the ABox-populated measurement:ThresholdRow individuals (BP-D44). Aggregation uses arithmetic mean of constituent metric band scores unless the source specifies a weighted aggregation. Aggregation MUST handle 'out-of-range' and 'unthresholded' inputs by EXCLUDING them from the mean (do not silently substitute 0 or a default value), and MUST report the count of excluded metrics alongside the score. Detection: characteristic scores computed without traceable formula. Mitigation: every characteristic-aggregation function documented with its source table reference and its exclusion-handling policy."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D44_OQuaREThresholdProvenance .

kb:BestPractice_D46_SemanticOverlapAnnotationDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D46: Semantic-Overlap Annotation Discipline"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D46"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T22:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.5.0 (Proposal I Phase 5: semantic-overlap concept work per user directive). Codified from the audit that classified 24 collisions: 14 OVERLAP + 9 DIFFERENCE + 1 DEFERRED."@en ;
    skos:definition "When cross-subject local-name collisions exist, classify each via three-way decision: (1) OVERLAP — same semantic concept across subjects. Resolution: identify canonical subject (usually the most general/umbrella-owning subject), add rdfs:subClassOf from non-canonical to canonical, add skos:exactMatch bidirectionally, add rdfs:seeAlso. (2) DIFFERENCE — same name, distinct semantics. Resolution: add rdfs:seeAlso + skos:related bidirectionally to flag the naming collision while preserving distinct semantics. (3) DUPLICATION — one is byte-identical redundancy of the other. Resolution: mark non-canonical owl:deprecated, replace usages. Detection: cross-subject local-name collisions found via SPARQL across all subjects' TBox files. Mitigation: annotate at v19.5.0 baseline; future class additions check the audit table before introducing a new collision. Never silently re-declare a name owned by another subject."@en .

kb:BestPractice_D47_ConfidenceTierDisclosureDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D47: Confidence Tier Disclosure Discipline"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D47"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T23:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.6.0 Phase J1 of Proposal J v1.0.0. Codified from the conversation where the user challenged the static-vs-probabilistic dichotomy and argued that ecosystem-relative statistical analysis plus LLM-mediated assessment legitimately extend reachable coverage beyond pure-algorithmic methods, provided confidence is disclosed."@en ;
    skos:definition """BP-D47: Every score produced by oe-assessment-tooling v1.4.0+ MUST disclose its methodological confidence tier in the JSON output as a `tier` field with value 'A', 'B', 'C' or 'D' (A static algorithmic, B statistical proxy, C LLM-mediated, D human judgment). In an ontology every measurement:Score individual carries exactly one measurement:hasConfidenceTier. Aggregations propagate the LOWEST tier of their inputs, the order running from most to least confident A, B, D, C (owner's ruling of 2026-10-12; A and B can be reproduced exactly, D, a human expert's judgment, cannot be reproduced bit for bit but is accountable and open to review, C, a language model's judgment, is neither): an aggregation of TIER-A and TIER-B inputs is TIER-B; one containing a TIER-D input and no TIER-C input is TIER-D; any aggregation containing a TIER-C input is TIER-C. LLM-mediated scores (TIER-C) MUST additionally include `llm_metadata` with {model, temperature, prompt_version, cache_key}. Statistical-proxy scores (TIER-B) MUST include `proxy_basis` citing the published method (e.g., 'Duque-Ramos 2016 Table 3 LCOMOnto→Availability'). Detection: any score in JSON output missing `tier` field, or any measurement:Score individual of the pack without measurement:hasConfidenceTier. Mitigation: in this pack the SHACL shape of measurement:Score (oepack SHACL 1.2.0) requires exactly one measurement:hasConfidenceTier of class ConfidenceTier and runs with the pack's validation; the tier-tagging utility for the JSON output of the assessment tooling belongs to that tooling and is not part of the OE repositories (the earlier text named a utility `oe_assessor/confidence/` and a CI test T-J1-1; neither exists in any OE repository, found 2026-10-11). Rationale: probabilistic assessment tooling must be epistemically honest about confidence; consumers (humans, downstream tooling, regulators) need to know which scores are algorithmic vs proxy vs LLM-mediated.

Cross-LLM-laundering clause (formerly proposed as L-X3 in OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal v1.0.0 from a domain ecosystem; merged 2026-05-28): accepting a parallel LLM session's 'VERIFIED' label on bibliographic data, code, or other externally-checkable content is LAUNDERING one LLM's unverified output through another. Any 'VERIFIED' (evidence GRADE-1) claim whose provenance is LLM-only is AUTOMATICALLY DEMOTED to CORROBORATED (evidence GRADE-2) — multiple LLM sources agreeing is corroboration, not verification. Only resolution against an authoritative NON-LLM source produces GRADE-1. The evidence grade governs downstream use: GRADE-1 can ground claims, GRADE-2 can be cited with disclosure, GRADE-3 must not be cited as a reference. (An evidence grade of a claim is not a confidence tier of a score; the earlier text called both TIER-A to TIER-C, which two different things must not share.)"""@en .

kb:BestPractice_D48_DevelopmentBacklogBinding a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D48: development is bound to the product-backlog as the single source of truth"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D48"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Emerged 2026-07-13 (a product project): after building the product-backlog ontology (T/A/S/R + bridge) as a machine-checkable register, development must be bound to it. Directed by the product owner."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Every development delivery corresponds to exactly one Product Backlog Item in the governed product-backlog ABox. On delivery the PBI's pb:hasStatus and pb:hasEvidence (a delivered version carrying its package SHA-256 and/or a named passing test) MUST be updated in the register, and the reconciliation gate (SHACL validation + evidence bridge + reasoner consistency) MUST exit clean before the release is complete. Scope, progress, and next are read FROM the register (pb:hasStatus, the package-status rule, the next-selection query), never hand-maintained in prose. Binds development to one machine-checkable source of truth; prevents prose-status drift and untracked/duplicated work."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-13T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D49_PyshaclProgrammaticAPIVsCLI a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D49: verify pyshacl's programmatic API against a known-bad fixture before trusting it; prefer the CLI"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D49"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Emerged 2026-07-13 (a product project): in this execution environment (pyshacl 0.40.0 and 0.25.0, rdflib 7.6.0), pyshacl.validate() called from Python silently returned conforms=True on graphs with real, planted SHACL violations -- reproduced on a minimal 3-triple fixture. The `python3 -m pyshacl` CLI, run on the same data+shapes files, correctly reported Conforms: False. Discovered only because L-95/L-97 discrimination testing was applied to the validator itself." ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D3_DirectJavaHermiT,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating,
        kb:Lesson_L_97_DiscriminationAppliesToVerifierOwnChecks ;
    skos:definition "Before relying on pyshacl's Python API (pyshacl.validate(...)) in a given environment, run it once against a minimal fixture with a known, planted violation (e.g. sh:minCount 5 on a single-valued property) and confirm conforms=False. If the API returns a false conforms=True, do not debug the shapes -- switch to the `python3 -m pyshacl -s shapes.ttl -a -f human data.ttl` CLI, which was independently confirmed correct on the same inputs. This is the same class of failure as BP-D3 (owlready2's HermiT wrapper silently masking inconsistency) but for a different tool/library pairing, so BP-D3 alone does not cover it; both instances generalize to: never trust a validation/reasoning tool's programmatic wrapper without a fixture-based positive-and-negative check in the CURRENT environment, regardless of which specific library it is."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-13T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D50_ProjectScopeVsGovernanceStoreBoundary a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D50: project-specific findings live in the project's own artifacts; only genuinely cross-project, discipline-level lessons go into the shared OE-pack knowledge_base"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D50"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Formalized 2026-07-13 (a product project), by explicit user direction, after discovering that a rule cited verbatim across this entire multi-session engagement ('app-scope findings go to the app roadmap; zero OE-pack ABox edits', attributed to an ID L-64) did NOT actually exist anywhere in the governed store -- L-64 is in fact Lesson_L_64_ScopeBleedViaSourceAttributionNarratives, an unrelated lesson. The practice being followed was sound in spirit but had never been formally recorded; every prior ceremony's 'grounded verbatim' claim against it was therefore false. This is a confirmed recurrence of kb:Lesson_L_80_ContextAsKnowledgeConfusion (a fact fluently inherited from prior-turn/session summary was treated as freshly re-verified) operating ACROSS sessions, not within one." ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D37_StableGovernanceArtifactsInProjectKnowledgeBase,
        kb:Lesson_L_80_ContextAsKnowledgeConfusion ;
    skos:definition "A project session (e.g. a product project app development) surfaces two kinds of finding: (a) project-specific facts, bugs, and backlog items -- these belong ONLY in the project's own artifacts (its app roadmap, its product-backlog ABox), never in the OE pack's shared knowledge_base; (b) genuinely generalizable discipline lessons (a new failure mode, a new verification technique, a new governance gap) -- these belong in the shared knowledge_base_abox, sourced honestly to the originating project incident, per the existing pattern already used throughout the store (e.g. BP-D31, BP-D44 citing specific source incidents while stating a general principle). Before editing the shared knowledge_base from within a project session, explicitly classify the finding as (a) or (b); before CITING an existing rule as governing this boundary, re-extract it verbatim via the governed-ABox tooling this turn -- do not inherit the citation fluently from a prior turn's or session's summary (L-80), however many times it has been repeated."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-13T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D31_PrimarySourceConceptCoverageGate,
        kb:BestPractice_D44_OQuaREThresholdProvenance .

kb:BestPractice_D9_PresentBundleNotLoose a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "When a release bundle exists, present only the bundle — never alongside its loose contents"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D9"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current-session user feedback: 'the files are not packaged as a single zip file' after present_files surfaced 3 paths instead of 1."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D9_PresentBundleNotLoose (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """After authoring a release bundle (configuration:ProjectArchive zip per BP-D6 + BP-D8), the file-presentation step must surface ONLY the zip, not the zip alongside its loose constituent files. Presenting both creates ambiguity: the user cannot tell whether the deliverable is the bundle or the individual files, and may download the wrong artifact (or both, duplicating storage and creating version-drift risk).

Operational form: when calling present_files (or equivalent UI surfacing), pass a list containing exactly one path per coherent deliverable. If a zip exists, pass the zip path alone. The loose files remain on disk in /mnt/user-data/outputs/ for direct-access workflows (e.g., follow-up edits in this conversation), but they are not surfaced as downloads alongside the zip.

Exception: if the user explicitly requests both the bundle AND the loose files (e.g., 'give me the zip and also the individual TTL files separately'), surface both. Default behaviour is bundle-only.

Verified motivation: in current session, presenting oepack-discipline-catalogue-v1_0_5_1.zip alongside its 2 loose TTL contents made the user reasonably ask 'why isn't this packaged as a single zip?' even though it WAS — the loose copies undermined the bundling signal."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming,
        kb:BestPractice_D8_Sha256Manifest .

kb:LessonLearned_28_MultipleContradictionChains a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Same HermiT inconsistency may have multiple independent contradiction chains"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-28"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Cross-session comparison: STEP_0_I17_Closure_v1_0_0.md (current) vs g1_diagnostic_v17_0_0_v1_0_0.ttl (parallel)."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_28_MultipleContradictionChains (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Surfaced by comparing parallel-session OE Pack v17.0.0 vs current-session I-17 closure. Both sessions worked on the same merged graph and both achieved HermiT-CONSISTENT outcomes, but diagnosed different culprit chains:

Current session traced 3 chains involving:
  - a reliability ontology's AllDisjointClasses(LifecyclePhase, MitigationStrategy, TestingConcept, VerificationMethod) × meta:TC ≡ reli:TC
  - AMOD's SprintManifest ⊥ OntologyFile × meta:isValidatedBy owl:inverseOf core:validates × inferred typing
  - OQC's duplicate AllDisjointClasses(AssessmentActivity, AssessmentConfiguration, AssessmentOutput, QualityConcept) × QualityAssessment ⊑ Deliverable ⊑ OntologyArtifact ⊑ QualityConcept

Parallel session traced 1 chain involving:
  - meta:CQ ⊑ meta:TC ≡ reli:TC ⊑ core:Process AND meta:CQ ≡ core:CQ AND core:CQ ⊥ core:Process

Both diagnoses are factually correct (verified — see Parallel_Session_Assessment_v1_0_0.md §3-4). Removing any one of several axioms can break the chain. Therefore:

Authoring discipline:
  1. Avoid 'ROOT CAUSE' terminology unless single-axiom-removal is proven the minimal sufficient fix (i.e., bisection confirms removing N-1 of the suspected axioms still triggers inconsistency)
  2. Prefer 'culprit' or 'contradiction-chain participant' for axioms whose removal breaks at least one chain
  3. After applying a fix, re-bisect the FULL stack (cumulative load) to confirm no other chain remains
  4. Document each chain's premise → contradiction trace explicitly so future maintainers understand why the patch works"""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_29_TBoxConsistencyDoesNotImplyABoxConsistency a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TBox HermiT-CONSISTENT does not imply CONSISTENT under arbitrary ABox loads"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-29"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_29_TBoxConsistencyDoesNotImplyABoxConsistency (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Parallel-session OE Pack v17.0.0 README §'ABox residual' + Parallel_Session_Assessment_v1_0_0.md §5 (OQC duplicate-AD analysis)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Surfaced by parallel-session admission ('ABox residual deferred to v17.1.0') and current-session verification (their TBox+v15.4 ABox = HermiT INCONSISTENT despite their TBox-only being CONSISTENT).

A TBox passing HermiT consistency check guarantees only that the class hierarchy is internally satisfiable — it does NOT guarantee that adding instances will not trigger contradictions. Latent class-emptiness (a class that the TBox forces to be empty due to e.g. AllDisjointClasses chains) is silent at TBox level but explodes when an instance is asserted into the empty class.

Concrete instance from this study: OQC's duplicate AllDisjointClasses(AssessmentActivity, AssessmentConfiguration, AssessmentOutput, QualityConcept) combined with qc:QualityAssessment ⊑ AssessmentActivity ∧ qc:QualityAssessment ⊑ meta:Deliverable ⊑ qc:OntologyArtifact ⊑ qc:QualityConcept makes qc:QualityAssessment necessarily empty at TBox level. The TBox alone does not contradict (an empty class is satisfiable). But adding `Assessment_OE_v15_4 a qc:QualityAssessment` (in oqc_measurements_v15_4_v1_0_0.ttl) forces a non-empty member into an empty class, triggering inconsistency.

Discipline:
  1. G1 release gate must specify which ABox files are in scope; 'TBox-only G1 PASS' is not a release-grade verdict
  2. Run HermiT on the merged TBox+ABox bundle the user is expected to deploy
  3. For 'optional' ABox files (sample data, demo instances), document them and run HermiT separately to surface latent class-emptiness issues
  4. When TBox-fix authors defer ABox issues as 'residual cleanup', verify that the residual is ABOX-LEVEL (per-instance rewriting) and not actually TBOX-LEVEL (hidden class-emptiness)"""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_30_DatatypeLangTagMismatch a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Datatype/language-tag mismatch is a recurring HermiT-INCONSISTENT pattern"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-30"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_30_DatatypeLangTagMismatch (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 1 I-04 closure (amod_v1_2_6_1.ttl); parallel-session Phase 3 closure (meta_v6_7_0_v1_0_1.ttl)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Two independent occurrences of the same defect class observed in this study: (1) AMOD `filenamePattern` (Phase 1 I-04, our v1.2.6.1 patch) — declared rdfs:range xsd:string but ABox literals carry @en. (2) META `meta:overrideRationale` (parallel Phase 3 D-PHASE3-1, our v6.7.0.1 patch) — same pattern, in a totally different ontology authored months apart. Per OWL 2 DL strict mode, xsd:string and rdf:langString are disjoint datatypes; HermiT must satisfy literal-as-string AND literal-as-langString, yielding contradiction. Discipline: When declaring datatype properties with rdfs:range xsd:string (or any specific datatype), ensure ABox literal values use plain typed literals, NOT language-tagged. Or use rdfs:range rdfs:Literal if multilingual values are intended. Pre-release SHACL shape on the property datatype would catch this without full HermiT runs."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_31_PropertyDomainSemantics a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Property domain/range semantics matter — read OWL declarations before ABox use"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-31"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_31_PropertyDomainSemantics (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Parallel-session Phase 2 closure deficiencies; current-session Parallel_Phase1_2_3_Assessment_v1_0_1."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Parallel-session Phase 2 D-PHASE2-1 + D-PHASE2-2 both arose from confusing OQC properties: (1) qc:appliesMeasure (rdfs:domain qc:Metric, rdfs:range qc:QualityMeasure) used on Score subjects → forced Score⊑Metric, contradicting AllDisjointClasses(Metric,Score,Evidence). (2) qc:hasScore (rdfs:domain qc:Metric, rdfs:range qc:Score) used on QualityAssessment subjects → forced Assessment⊑Metric (no contradiction but SHACL violations). Property names alone are insufficient guidance; the proper chain in OQC is: QualityAssessment --hasMetric--> Metric --hasScore--> Score, with Score --isScoreOf--> Metric inverse. Discipline: Before using a property in ABox content, run a SPARQL query for its rdfs:domain and rdfs:range in the merged ontology and confirm alignment with the intended subject/object types."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_32_HermiTNotSHACL a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "HermiT-CONSISTENT does not imply SHACL-CONFORMANT"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-32"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_32_HermiTNotSHACL (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Parallel-session Phase 2 D-PHASE2-2 root-cause analysis (current session)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "D-PHASE2-2 (qc:hasScore domain Metric used on QualityAssessment subjects) demonstrates that an ontology can be HermiT-CONSISTENT yet ship structural defects detectable only by SHACL. RDFS domain inference forced Assessment⊑Metric, which doesn't trip any AllDisjointClasses in OQC (so HermiT is silent), but every QA instance then violates 6 different MetricShape constraints (no criterion, no exactly-one-criterion, etc.). Release-gate verification requires BOTH HermiT (logical consistency) AND SHACL (structural integrity); neither alone is sufficient. This applies especially to ABox-heavy deliverables where property-domain inference dominates. Discipline: never downgrade a SHACL Violation just because HermiT is CONSISTENT, and never downgrade a HermiT INCONSISTENT just because SHACL is CONFORMANT."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_33_SessionStartChecklist a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Future sessions should start by loading the discipline catalogue + AMOD v1.4.1"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-33"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Current session — author's instruction 'encode it in the ontology'; meta-procedural lesson for bootstrap."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_33_SessionStartChecklist (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """The discipline is in the FILES, not in conversational memory. Cross-session continuity is achieved by loading the canonical discipline-bearing files at session start, not by the model remembering rules between conversations.

Three-file bootstrap procedure:
  1. Upload v1.0.4 supplement (L-27..L-32, VF22..VF25)
  2. Upload v1.0.5 discipline extension (this file: BP-D1..BP-D8, L-33)
  3. Upload amod_v1_4_1_archive_extension (ProjectArchive vocabulary)

The discipline catalogue is then SPARQL-queryable, R1-R8 SHACL-conformant, and HermiT-CONSISTENT under the standard OE Pack base. Future-session-Claude can verify discipline applicability by running the above SPARQL against the loaded graph."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D1_AskBeforeAssuming,
        kb:LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking .

kb:LessonLearned_34_PipelineMaskingOfLangTagInconsistency a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-34: Phase 2.8 verification pipeline jointly masked a HermiT-INCONSISTENT condition via two independent gaps"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-34"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_34_PipelineMaskingOfLangTagInconsistency (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 2.9 raw inspection of v17.4.0; out-of-band remediation upload from parallel session."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Root cause of the Phase 2.8 v17.4.0 false-CONSISTENT claim was the conjunction of TWO independent verification gaps:

Gap 1 (verifier scope): the Python verification script enumerated bundle categories 01..05, 10, 11, 12 — but did NOT include 07-shacl-shapes/. SHACL-file rdfs:range declarations did not enter the HermiT load. (The shell pipeline release_check_v1_2_1.sh used find -name *.ttl and did NOT have this gap — but was not run, only the Python ad-hoc script was.)

Gap 2 (datatype masking): HermiT was invoked with --ignoreUnsupportedDatatypes. This flag is NECESSARY (HermiT's OWL 2 datatype map excludes xsd:date, used widely via dcterms:created). But the flag SUFFICIENTLY hides the xsd:dateTimeTime typo on returnedAtTimestamp + hasTimestamp property ranges, which would otherwise cause UnsupportedDatatypeException and surface as a pipeline error.

Independent demonstration:
  - Strict HermiT (no flag) + full bundle (with SHACL): UnsupportedDatatypeException on xsd:date
  - Strict HermiT (no flag) + bundle minus xsd:date users: would report INCONSISTENT (lang-tag)
  - Lenient HermiT (flag) + bundle WITHOUT SHACL: reports CONSISTENT (the false positive)
  - Lenient HermiT (flag) + bundle WITH SHACL: reports INCONSISTENT (correct)

The structural fix is two-layer: (a) release_preflight_v1_0_1.py scans for the patterns that the flag masks; (b) release_check_v1_2_2.sh invokes the preflight as Gate B0 before Gate B HermiT.

Anti-pattern recognized: SelfAuditConflictOfInterest — the agent that authored the verification was the same agent that authored the data. Verification using ad-hoc scripts (rather than the canonical release_check.sh) compounded the conflict by giving the author opportunity to choose which gaps to omit. Pipeline-as-code (canonical verifier, no ad-hoc verification) is the structural defense."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_100_GovernanceOntologySelfDescriptionClosesReflexivityGap a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-100: a governance/meta ontology gains assurance by self-describing its own method taxonomy"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-100"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-07 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_07> ;
    skos:definition "When a governance or meta ontology defines a taxonomy of domain methods (e.g. ontology-evaluation methods: pitfall-scanning, metric-based, quality-model, competency-question), applying that taxonomy reflexively to the governance ontology itself closes a reflexivity gap — the meta layer is then accountable to the same classification it imposes. Relates to L-92 (verification discipline) but is about structural self-description, not output verification."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_92_LLMOutputVerificationDisciplineIndex .

kb:Lesson_L_105_ReuseBeforeBuildSingleSource a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-105: search for an existing implementation before building; never build a parallel one"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-105"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Emerged 2026-07-13 (a product project): a complete UI-wired access-control system (FVDataAccess.canPerform with rules+ownership) already existed; a parallel data-layer RBAC (FVRBAC) was built across five slices, each individually green, duplicating it."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_102_OverlappingBranchPromotionNeedsOrthogonalityProof ;
    skos:definition "Before building any new component, gate, or subsystem for a capability, first search the existing codebase/graph for an implementation of that capability; if one exists, extend or delegate to it, never parallel it. This is BP-D2 applied at the architectural level ('does an authoritative implementation already exist?'), upstream of L-102's test-time orthogonality proof. Micro-slicing hides duplication because each slice is locally green and no step forces a whole-system survey; deliver package-sized units that force a design/survey, and never treat green tests as a non-duplication signal."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-13T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

kb:Lesson_L_106_AuditCoverageMustBeTrackedNotImplied a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-106: a long, item-by-item re-verification pass must record per-item audit coverage explicitly, never imply it from partial progress"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-106"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Emerged 2026-07-13 (a product project): planning a 78-item backlog reconciliation against live code, spanning many turns/sessions; identified the risk of checking easy/recent items first and unconsciously generalizing that confidence to the rest." ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:LessonLearned_19,
        kb:Lesson_L_101_AggregateReadinessGateIsItsOwnAssertion,
        kb:Lesson_L_81_SunkCostFoundationDrift ;
    skos:definition "In a long, item-by-item re-verification pass over a large register (e.g. reconciling every PBI's claimed status against live code), the fact of having checked N items does not by itself justify treating the remaining items as equally likely to hold up (L-81's forward-defend instinct, applied across items rather than pipeline stages), and completing all N checks is a distinct claim from the aggregate being true (L-101). The discipline: each item carries an explicit, machine-readable audit marker (timestamp + method) set ONLY when that specific item was directly re-verified against live code this pass; absence of the marker must never be read as 'probably fine' (mirrors L-91's own don't-assume-from-absence framing, applied to audit coverage rather than status prose); progress reports state the exact count and IDs checked, not a percentage-implied generality; and CHECK ORDER MUST NOT BE PURELY BY EASE OR RECENCY, to avoid the easy/recent items inflating confidence in the harder, older, unchecked ones."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-13T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_91_StatusProseMustTrackStatusFields .

kb:Lesson_L_110_NoNewStructureOnSingleProducerEvidence a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-110: Do not mint new governance structure on a single producer's evidence — enrich the governing term and defer the structure until a second divergent case exists"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-110"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Catalogued by OEE at OE Pack v20.24.0. The two applications above are OEE's own; neither was catalogued at the time, and the v20.23.41 ruling cited BP-D46 for the restraint — a citation a registrant package correctly reported as false (BP-D46 is SemanticOverlapAnnotationDiscipline, 918 characters entirely about cross-subject local-name collisions, containing no restraint language; re-verified independently over all 175 skos:definition literals in knowledge_base_abox_v2_16_0.ttl, zero matches). The registrant surfaced the gap and explicitly declined to propose the lesson itself, citing L-71 anti-padding; authorship and attribution are therefore OEE's."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When one registrant's case does not fit an existing governed term cleanly, three responses are available: mint a new sibling term, mint an exception individual against the existing term, or enrich the existing term's own definition to cover the case. On the evidence of a single producer the first two are premature. A term created to cover one case encodes that case's incidental properties as though they were general, and the next producer's genuinely different divergence then either strains the new term or forces a third one; an exception minted before the divergence is understood also risks being typed against the wrong class, since the nearest-looking exception mechanism is usually scoped to a different kind of fact (L-75). Enriching the governing term records the same fact, keeps one authority for the reader, and leaves the structural decision falsifiable: the term can still be minted later, when a second genuinely divergent case supplies the evidence for where the boundary belongs. The restraint is NOT a general preference for inaction — where a case diverges in form from the governing pattern (a different filename shape, a different artifact kind), the exception or the new term is the correct answer and deferring it hides the divergence. The test is whether the case follows the existing pattern and differs only in a fact ABOUT its usage, in which case the definition is the right home. EMPIRICAL BASIS — two OE Pack applications that preceded this catalogue entry: (1) OEE_to_BacklogFramework_registration_confirmation_v1_0_0 left the registrant's family-token question on configuration:IndependentPackageArchiveConvention unresolved, in its own words premature for a set of one; (2) the v20.23.41 Phase-D ruling enriched configuration:ABoxFileConvention with a governance-register-data clause rather than minting a 17th convention or a VersionInFilenamePolicy exception on one producer's evidence. DISTINCT FROM L-71 (a class minted and left unpopulated is the downstream failure this restraint prevents, not the restraint itself) and from L-89 (which governs choosing among predicates that already exist, not whether to create one)."@en ;
    skos:related kb:Lesson_L_71_FrameworkClassExistsZeroPopulation,
        kb:Lesson_L_89_PreferPurposeBuiltPredicateOverGenericRelation ;
    skos:scopeNote "Number L-110, not L-109: the identifier L-109 was publicly used in release event ReleaseEvent_OEPack_v20_23_36 for a candidate lesson that was DECLINED for fabricated provenance and never minted. The number is left unused so that a reader searching the record for L-109 finds only the declined candidate."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D46_SemanticOverlapAnnotationDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_75_OverlapVsDifferenceCannotBeAssumed .

kb:Lesson_L_111_DocumentationCoverageDriftIsGateInvisible a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-111: The document that explains a vocabulary can fall releases behind it while every structural gate stays green - coverage must also be gated in the artifact-to-prose direction"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-111"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Emerged in the a registrant package round, catalogued by OEE at OE Pack v20.25.0. The registrant found the drift by mechanical scan, fixed it (their standard v1.3.0 -> v1.4.0, 91/91 classes named) and shipped backlog_doc_coverage_gate_v1_0_0.py as a sixth release-gate check; they proposed no lesson. OEE re-derived the instance independently rather than adopting the report: the shipped gate returns PASS 91/91 against the current standard and FAIL 62/91 against the superseded v1.3.0 standard under the same TBox, exit 1 - proven discriminating per L-95. The registrant's own changelog states 32 undocumented classes; the reproducible figure under their shipped gate is 29, a discrepancy reported back and immaterial to the lesson."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Parse checks, SHACL suites, manifest self-verification, version-identity checks and primary-source coverage gates all measure a property of the artifacts, or of the artifacts against their source. None of them compares the governed vocabulary with the prose document that explains it to an adopter, so a standard can drift releases behind the subject it standardises while every gate reports clean. The drift is invisible precisely because it is not a defect in any single artifact: the TBox is correct, the document is internally coherent, and only the RELATION between them is stale. MEASURED INSTANCE, re-derived on bytes at OE Pack v20.25.0: a registrant package's standard at v1.3.0 named 62 of the 91 classes declared in backlog_tbox_v1_7_0 - 29 classes, three subject releases' worth of vocabulary, never described - while the same package's release gate reported PASS on parse, SHACL (0 violations with positive/negative/adversarial discrimination), manifest, version identity and 36/36 source-concept coverage. THE DISCIPLINE is a coverage gate in the opposite direction to BP-D31: BP-D31 asks whether the artifact covers its primary source; this asks whether the explanatory document covers the artifact. Every class declared in the governed vocabulary must be named in the document adopters read, and the check must run as a mandatory release gate rather than a periodic review (L-104) - a rule enforced by remembering to look is a rule that fails on the day nobody looks. Property-level coverage is deliberately NOT required: documents legitimately group properties, while classes are the vocabulary an adopter navigates by. Requiring properties would make the gate noisy enough to be disabled, which is worse than a narrower gate that runs. DISTINCT FROM L-91, which governs status prose contradicting an authoritative machine-readable status field - that is a claim going stale; this is a vocabulary going undescribed, with no contradiction anywhere to detect."@en ;
    skos:related kb:BestPractice_D31_PrimarySourceConceptCoverageGate,
        kb:Lesson_L_104_ReconciliationAsGate,
        kb:Lesson_L_91_StatusProseMustTrackStatusFields ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-29T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating .

kb:Lesson_L_112_MechanicalRepointMustExcludeHistoricalRecords a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-112: A mechanical repoint is correct for files that describe the current state and corrupting for files that record a past one - exclude historical records by class"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-112"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted at OE Pack v20.26.0 from a registrant package, which screened the candidate against all 177 definitions in knowledge_base_abox_v2_18_0.ttl, found nothing covering it, and deliberately did NOT deposit it on the grounds that the ORCP ceremony was closed. OEE re-ran the screen independently with a different pattern set (same result: nearest neighbours BP-D6 and L-88, neither covering the file-class property) and adopted it anyway: a closed registration round does not close the catalogue, and L-84 governs lesson recording independently of registration state. The second, earlier instance is OEE's own, read from the operating discipline's own text this session."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "A mechanical repoint - a global search-and-replace that updates an artifact name, version token or pointer across a corpus - is correct for every file that DESCRIBES the current state and wrong for every file that RECORDS a past one. A changelog entry, a release-history comment, a closure note and a prior assessment are statements of fact about a release that has already happened; rewriting them to name the current artifact version does not update them, it makes them false, and the falsehood is undetectable afterwards because the text reads as though it was always there. The failure survives the scrub discipline of L-88: there, the danger is matching occurrences that should not be touched, and the remedy is enumerating string variants and guarding keep-tokens. Here EVERY match is a genuine occurrence of the target name; the repoint is correct per-occurrence and still corrupts the record, because the property that matters is not which string matched but which FILE CLASS was touched. Variant enumeration cannot catch it. DISCIPLINE: classify files before any corpus-wide edit into THREE classes, not two. (a) CURRENT-STATE DESCRIPTIONS - ontologies, shapes, tools, READMEs, standards: editable, and the repoint's actual target. (b) HISTORICAL RECORDS - changelog entries, release-history comments, closure notes, dated assessments and deposits: excluded by class, not by inspection, and corrected only by appending a dated correction or by a new versioned entry stating what the earlier one got wrong, never by rewriting the earlier text. (c) GENERATED ARTIFACTS - manifests, generated metrics and reports: excluded as well, but for a different reason and with a different remedy. They are not records of the past; they are derived from the current tree, so a text edit desynchronises them from what they describe. A manifest is the worst case: repointing a filename token inside it rewrites the integrity instrument itself, leaving the recorded path and the file on disk disagreeing, or agreeing for the wrong reason. The remedy is to REGENERATE them after the repoint, never to edit them. A repoint script names all its exclusions explicitly, so that the exclusion is a property of the tool rather than of whoever remembers to pass a flag. TWO INSTANCES, both on bytes: (1) OE Pack's own - the operating discipline carries the clause 'global repoint scripts must exclude this file' precisely because prior repoints had mutated a released version's pointer text in place, which its v2.2.0 note records as a same-version content change evidenced by SHA drift; (2) a registrant package - a global sed across the documentation directory rewrote a historical changelog entry to name a tool version that did not exist at the release the entry describes, which OEE caught by comparing the entry against the deposit held for that release. The first instance produced the narrow rule for one file; this lesson generalises it to the file class. AMENDED 2026-07-29 (kb_abox v2.19.1, OE Pack v20.26.1): the classification above was originally two-way. The third class was surfaced by the registrant's own implementation of this lesson - a repoint tool whose exclusions are hard-coded rather than optional, which is exactly what the lesson asks for, and which nonetheless left MANIFEST_SHA256.txt in the editable set. Verified by dry run rather than by reading: a repoint of an ontology filename token reported the manifest as a file it would edit. Under the original two-way split that behaviour was correct, since a manifest is not a record of the past - which is what made the split wrong rather than the tool."@en ;
    skos:related kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_88_ScopedScrubRequiresVariantEnumerationAndKeepGuard ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-29T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming,
        kb:Lesson_L_84_RecordLessonsAsTheyEmerge .

kb:Lesson_L_113_PerItemDispositionOnPluralRequests a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-113: A plural request answered by resolving one item is not license to treat the rest as answered - give every named item its own disposition"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-113"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Named B6 in OE_Operating_Discipline v2.4.0, adopted after the owner directly challenged an unnecessary either/or question raised instead of proceeding on evidence that had already settled it. Never registered in the governed store at the time - found by a real cross-session finding (vaf-agentic-pipeline, 2026-09-09) that the discipline's citation of 'L-111' for this rule resolved to an unrelated, pre-existing lesson (documentation-coverage drift) under that ID. Registered here under a free ID, correcting the collision rather than repeating it."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When a request names or clearly implies more than one item - categories, findings, tasks - resolving one of them is not license to treat the rest as answered. Give every named item its own explicit disposition: built, confirmed already covered, or declined with a stated reason. A single aggregate judgement that silently drops an item is the tell, even when the item that was addressed was addressed correctly and completely. Distinct from acting-vs-asking discipline: that is about not manufacturing a decision point the evidence already settles; this is about not letting a settled decision on one item stand in for a decision on all of them."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-09T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_111_DocumentationCoverageDriftIsGateInvisible .

kb:Lesson_L_114_ClassifyFindingsAtLoggingTime a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-114: When disclosing a finding, state whether the outcome would have occurred without the safeguard - a safeguard working is evidence, not a gap"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-114"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted into OE_Operating_Discipline v2.4.0 from a genuine cross-session handover (a registrant package, agentic-drift lessons, 2026-09-06), independently verified against that session's own real artifacts before acceptance. Never registered in the governed store at the time - found by a real cross-session finding (vaf-agentic-pipeline, 2026-09-09) that the discipline's citation of 'L-112' for this rule resolved to an unrelated, pre-existing lesson (mechanical-repoint file-class discipline) under that ID. Registered here under a free ID, correcting the collision rather than repeating it."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When disclosing a finding - a defect, a gap, a discrepancy - state explicitly whether the outcome would have occurred if the relevant safeguard had never existed. If no, it is evidence the safeguard works, not a gap in it, and must carry that label rather than being filed with the same weight as a genuine gap. Logging every friction point as if it were a finding, regardless of whether it reflects a real deficiency or a safeguard doing its job, reads as diligence but dilutes real signal."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-09T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_112_MechanicalRepointMustExcludeHistoricalRecords .

kb:Lesson_L_116_DerivableVsStipulatedDecisions a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-116: A decision the evidence does not settle is a stipulation, not a derivation — ship it as a proposal to the owner, not as an enforced artifact"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-116"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "A real cross-session handover (a registrant package, 2026-09-10), independently verified before acceptance: between v1.242.0 and v1.253.0 that package added 18 SHACL shapes, 11 of which were the session's own stipulations rather than owner instruction or verified external proposal, and 7 of the last 8 lineage-discipline G-entries (G85, G87-G92) opened by citing an owner correction of a stipulation the session had enforced unasked. Confirmed directly: G85-G92 exist as claimed, and spot-checked entries (G89) genuinely cite an owner correction in their opening paragraph."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """B1 governs artifacts another session owns (propose, never edit). B2 governs choices the evidence already settles (decide, do not ask). Neither governs the common case in between: a policy inside a session's OWN package where no convention applies and no evidence decides, because the question is a stipulation - a definition, threshold, taxonomy, severity, or rule-scope chosen, not derived. B2's own "asking is the drift" reasoning does not license enforcing a stipulation unilaterally; it licenses not asking when something ALREADY decides the answer, which by definition is not true of a stipulation.

The test: if the owner asked "why this and not the other?", could the session answer with a file, a measurement, or a rule - or only with its own judgement? The second case is a stipulation. A stipulation may be built, measured, and presented as a proposal with its alternatives shown; it may not be enforced against real work until the owner rules on it. This is narrower than asking about everything, which remains its own named drift (B2/L-78) - the ordinary case, where evidence decides, is unaffected."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-10T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_78_DefensiveDeferral .

kb:Lesson_L_117_RuleReachDecidedByAuthorNotAdopters a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-117: A rule whose reach is decided by its author, not its adopters, is the retroactive-enforcement failure this discipline exists to refuse"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-117"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Named as a distinct, nameable pattern in the same handover that proposed L-116 (a registrant package, 2026-09-10), offered to the OE knowledge base as a supporting observation rather than the handover's primary ask. Distinguished from L-116 (which covers HOW a stipulation should ship) because this pattern can recur even when a stipulation is properly proposed and approved, if its enforcement scope is then applied backwards over work that predates it."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "A rule that binds work retroactively — applying to lineages, registers, or artifacts that existed and were compliant before the rule was drafted — lets whoever writes the rule silently redefine what already-accepted work means, without the adopters of that work ever agreeing to the new scope. The concrete family observed: retroactive severity promotion, an obligation binding a lineage that opened before the obligation existed, a status inferred from activity rather than from what the register itself declared. The mitigation: a rule's reach is fixed by adoption, not authorship — an ObligationSet is adopted by a lineage at open, a RuleSet is adopted by a register, and both are refused if applied after the fact to something that did not adopt them."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-10T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_116_DerivableVsStipulatedDecisions .

kb:Lesson_L_118_LabelBeforeIdentifier a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-118: Every governed element carries a human label, and discussion uses it — an identifier is a handle for machines, not a substitute for a sentence a reader can judge"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-118"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "A real cross-session handover (a registrant package, 2026-09-10), independently verified before acceptance: the owner stopped a working session with 'I really don't understand as you use a mixed language, not human readable and I cannot follow the inferences you made from the ontologic item names.' Measured on that package's own live register: of 24 checked intent elements (Mission, ScopeStatement, ScopeArea, ScopeDeliverable, ScopeExclusion), exactly 12 carried neither rdfs:label nor hasTitle — confirmed by direct query against backlog_framework_register_abox_v9_83_0.ttl, matching the handover's own count precisely, though the specific shape name the handover cited (IntentElementLabelShape) was not found under that name in backlog_shacl_v1_118_0.ttl at verification time -- the underlying data claim held regardless."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "An identifier is a handle for machines: chosen for uniqueness and brevity, it compresses a sentence into a token and invites the reader to reconstruct the sentence by guessing. A reviewer who cannot read an element cannot challenge it, and a governance framework whose elements cannot be challenged is decorative. Two halves, enforced differently: (1) every governed element carries a one-sentence human label — not a restatement of the identifier, but what the element actually is, in words a reader outside the session can judge; enforceable per-package by a SHACL shape requiring rdfs:label or an equivalent title property on intent-bearing classes. (2) discussion, reports, and changelogs use the label, not the identifier — an identifier appears only where it is needed to act (a path to edit, a term to grep, disambiguating two similarly labelled elements), and then alongside its label, never instead of it. The second half applies as much to a session reporting to its owner as to peer sessions: answering in identifiers asks the owner to decode rather than to decide, and if the label does not exist, that absence is the finding to report, not a gap to route silently around."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-10T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_120_RegistrationMaintenanceCeremony a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-120: A closed registration is not a permanent fact - periodically check a registrant's real current state against what was registered, as a real ceremony with a log, not a rule buried in a gate"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-120"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's own direct observation, 2026-09-10, following a direct request to assess how an algebraic framework's own evolution affects OE/OEE/discipline/ORCP: an algebraic framework's registration closed at oe-pack v20.23.32 against artifact v1.4.0, and had moved to v2.20.0 (a MAJOR bump) by the time this lesson was written, live-verified via the GitHub API during the same session, with real substantive changes (files deleted, not just added) occurring twice during the investigation itself. an integration blueprint showed the same shape independently: its dependency on an algebraic framework changed architecture twice since its own registration closed, with zero remaining references to the pinned dependency anywhere in its current tooling. The owner's own proposed fix: build this as a ceremony structurally mirroring the handover inbox (L-115) - a real place, a real log, not a threshold buried in Phase C's gate logic."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_115_CrossSessionHandoverInbox ;
    skos:definition """ORCP's own Phase G assumes the registrant will proactively re-emit when something changes; nothing in the protocol or discipline asks the inverse question periodically - has enough changed since a registration closed that revisiting it is worth asking about. A closed registration is a snapshot, not an ongoing guarantee, and nothing structural surfaces its aging on its own.

The fix takes the same shape as the handover inbox (L-115), not a bare check inside an existing gate: a registry of known external registrants and their live repo locations where known, a tool that fetches each one's real current version and flags a MAJOR bump or 5+ accumulated MINOR bumps since registration, and a log recording every run - including a run that finds nothing to flag, so absence of action is itself visible and auditable rather than indistinguishable from a check that never happened. The tool only reports; filing the resulting handover, if any, stays a deliberate session action using the existing handover-inbox mechanism (B1: a registrant's own decision to reopen or not). A subject with no known, verifiable live location, or a registration with no version baseline to compare against, is disclosed as UNCHECKED or UNVERIFIABLE-BASELINE - never silently skipped, never guessed at to fill the gap (BP-D28/L-57)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-10T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_57_DeprecateRatherThanFabricate .

kb:Lesson_L_121_PinnedGenerationCapsAndGetsDefended a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-121: A pinned dependency generation silently caps what a consumer can express, and the cap gets defended as a design decision, not reported as a constraint"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-121"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "A real, self-critical cross-session finding (an integration blueprint session, 2026-09-17), independently verified before acceptance: an integration blueprint imported six of an algebraic framework's operators; asked why across three rounds, the first two answers (a principled categorisation; a real engine limitation found by reading one parser file) were both wrong, and the actual cause, found only when the owner pushed a third time, was that an integration blueprint's own engine pin matched an older ontology generation with six operators while the current generation had twelve - the six was a pin artifact, not a decision. Duplicate-screened by the filing session against L-103, L-105 and L-87 before filing; the distinction from L-103 verified directly against L-103's own stored text: L-103 governs choosing between two KNOWN versions before retiring the lower one, which presupposes both are visible to the chooser; this lesson covers the case where only one generation is reachable at all, so the comparison never happens because the wider option was never visible."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_103_SameIriHigherVersionMayBeDivergentScope ;
    skos:definition """When a package pins an external dependency by hash, it pins a generation, not just a file. If that generation is narrower than the dependency's current one, the consumer silently inherits a capability ceiling invisible from inside the consumer - everything it can see is consistent with the ceiling being the whole truth. Asked "why only this much?", the consumer reconstructs a justification rather than reporting a constraint, and can produce more than one confident, wrong justification before the real cause surfaces.

The discipline: before defending a scope limit as deliberate, check whether it is an artifact of what a pin makes reachable. The test is concrete - does the dependency's current generation offer more than the pinned one, and would the answer to "why not more?" change if it did? A scope limit that survives only because the wider option was never visible is not a design decision. This is a boundary-level gap, not an execution failure: the filing session's own ceremony ran correctly throughout and did not reach it on its own; it took direct, repeated challenge to surface."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-21T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_105_ReuseBeforeBuildSingleSource,
        kb:Lesson_L_87_SuppliedMissingFileMayBeSupersededIntermediate .

kb:Lesson_L_122_GlobalEnforcementDeadlocksConcurrentWork a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-122: A gate built when there was one thing to check naturally becomes all-or-nothing; once legitimately concurrent units of work exist, that same global evaluation deadlocks every unrelated one on any single unresolved finding"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-122"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "A real, verified finding from a registrant package's own tooling (found and fixed in that package's own v1.298.0, backlog_lineage_order_check v1.7.0 -> v1.8.0), proposed as generalizable and duplicate-screened by the filing session before delivery. Verified directly before acceptance, not taken on the filing session's word: backlog:CrossLineageRiskAdvisoryShape confirmed to genuinely exist and explicitly support concurrent in-flight lineages; the --baseline/touched_lineages scoping fix and its self-proof fixtures (fixture_lineage_restart, fixture_lineage_bypass_negative) confirmed present in the real, current tool file; the full narrative confirmed present in that package's own CHANGELOG_v1_255_0.md under the v1.298.0 entry. Duplicate-screened by this session against the existing knowledge base before adopting -- no existing lesson covers this pattern."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A governance mechanism built when a system has one thing to verify naturally becomes all-or-nothing: evaluate everything, fail if anything is wrong. This is safe only as long as "everything" is one coherent unit of work. Once a system grows to legitimately support multiple, independent units in flight at once, the same global evaluation becomes a structural deadlock -- any one unit's real, unresolved problem holds every other, unrelated unit hostage, even when the two units share nothing but the same gate.

The fix is scoping what blocks, not weakening what is checked: enforce fully against whatever the current change actually touches; disclose any other unit's own unresolved finding as advisory rather than blocking, visibly and every run, never silently dropped. The tell: a gate that was correct when the system had one lineage/pipeline/track, still unmodified, now that the system genuinely has several running at once."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-23T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_123_RenameOnChangeBaselineResolvedByStem a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-123: A tool comparing current state to an older tagged baseline for a file under rename-on-change versioning must resolve the baseline by stable stem, never by today's exact filename -- the failure is silent, not an error"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-123"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "A real, verified finding from a registrant package's own tooling (found and fixed in that package's own v1.298.0, backlog_lineage_order_check v1.7.0 -> v1.8.0), proposed as directly relevant to the OE ecosystem's own shared versioning convention (the same rename-on-change discipline used throughout oe-pack and oe-method), duplicate-screened by the filing session before delivery. Verified directly before acceptance: the stem-glob fallback (re.sub stripping the _v<major>_<minor>_<patch> suffix, then globbing candidates at the older tag by stem prefix) confirmed present in the real, current tool file, matching the proposal's own description exactly. Duplicate-screened by this session against the existing knowledge base before adopting -- no existing lesson covers this pattern; a real, unrelated best practice (BP-D13, per-bundle vs ecosystem SemVer) was checked and confirmed not a match."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Under a rename-on-change versioning discipline (a file's own name carries its version, and a content change means a new filename, not an in-place edit), a tool comparing "now" against "then" for that file at an older tagged baseline cannot use today's exact filename to look up the older copy -- the file may have been renamed one or more times since that tag, so no file of today's name ever existed there. A lookup by exact filename against a tag where that name never existed typically returns a clean, ordinary "not found" result -- indistinguishable from the legitimate case of a genuinely new file with no prior version. The failure is silent: it looks exactly like the correct handling of a different, benign case.

The fix: resolve the baseline file by its stable stem -- everything in the filename before the version suffix -- glob-matched within the same directory at that tag, never by assuming the filename itself is stable across time. This generalises beyond the reporting package to any OE-ecosystem tool built the same way, since rename-on-change versioning (matching BP-D7/BP-D6) is this ecosystem's own shared convention, not local to one package."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-23T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage,
        kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics .

kb:Lesson_L_124_ExemptionMustNotReadWhatARuleDerives a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-124: With SHACL rules merged into the shapes graph, a derived triple satisfies an exemption exactly as an asserted one does -- an exemption must read only what no rule derives, and its proof must run the path production runs"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-124"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "A real, verified finding from a registrant package (candidate L-cand-G, deposit backlog_framework_lesson_deposit_v2_4_0.ttl, filed 2026-09-28 through the L-84 pipeline). Shapes v1.141.0 exempted a slipped milestone that carried any milestoneOutcome; rule R10b derives milestoneOutcome Missed for exactly those milestones, so the shape could never fire. The author's probe ran without the rules and passed; the adopter (a product project) found it with the rules merged; fixed in a registrant package (shapes v1.142.0: exempt only an asserted Withdrawn with a rationale). Reproduced by the OEE session before adopting, in three configurations on the fixture's milestones: old shapes without rules -- the slipped-date Violation fires; old shapes with the rules merged (the production path) -- it does not; current shapes with the rules -- it fires again. Duplicate-screened against L-15 and L-67 (both about merged validation context, neither about a rule satisfying a constraint's own exemption) and against every lesson mentioning SHACL rules (none)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating,
        kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L15 ;
    skos:definition """When SHACL-AF rules are merged into the shapes graph (advanced mode), a triple a rule derived and a triple someone asserted are indistinguishable by the time the constraints run. An exemption written as "unless the node records X" is therefore met whenever any rule derives X -- including a rule that derives X from the very condition the constraint exists to report, which silences the constraint permanently.

Two disciplines follow. (1) An exemption must read only what no rule derives -- a recorded decision, such as an asserted withdrawal with a rationale -- or it must name the derived values it excludes. (2) The proof that a constraint fires must run the path production runs, with the rules merged. A probe run without the rules passes whether or not the exemption is sound, so it certifies nothing; this is L-95's discriminating-gate requirement applied to the execution path, not only to the inputs."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_67_ShapeTargetScopingMustConsiderMergedCorpus,
        kb:Lesson_L_84_RecordLessonsAsTheyEmerge .

kb:Lesson_L_126_DestructiveOverrideMustNameWhatItRemoves a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-126: A destructive override must name what it expects to remove, not accept everything"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-126"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-05 in the OEE governance session's own work. The publisher refuses to publish when origin holds a file the package lacks, because a wholesale replace would silently delete another session's work. Moving one handover from pending/ to accepted/ needed that guard overridden, and the only override, --force, accepts every deletion; it also removed a second handover another session had filed one minute earlier. Caught within minutes by reading the commit, restored from history, nothing lost."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A guard against an unintended destructive act is only as good as its override. An override that says \"proceed with whatever the guard found\" turns a protection into a switch: the person using it has read one deletion and consented to all of them, including the ones that appeared after the reading. The override must carry the list of what the person expects to be removed and refuse anything else, so that an item that arrives between reading and acting is still stopped. Until a tool offers that, the procedure is: run without the override, read the exact list, refresh the working copy, and override only if the list is exactly the intended one and nothing has arrived since." .

kb:Lesson_L_128_SkipWhatTheFileDeclaresNotWhatLooksLikeIt a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-128: A rule that skips what looks like X skips real subjects; skip only what the file declares"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-128"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session. The membership tool took every IRI below a file's header IRI for a version IRI and gave it no module. 67 named subjects (66 quality individuals and one reliability shape) were left without membership, and every proof passed, because every proof had been written from the same reading of the data. The ceiling of the structure checker caught it at the first push (1,317 subjects without a module against a ceiling of 1,250), and comparing the validation findings before and after the merge confirmed which subjects they were. From tool v1.0.1 on, only the version IRIs a header declares (owl:versionIRI, owl:priorVersion) are skipped."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "An exclusion inferred from the shape of a name (anything below this IRI is a version) is a guess about the data, and every test written from the same guess agrees with it. Derive an exclusion from what the artifact declares, and prove it on a case that looks like the excluded thing but is not (here: a subject whose IRI lies below the header IRI). Detection signal: a count that should be zero is not, in a place that no test of the tool named, found by an independent measure (a ceiling, a before-and-after comparison of findings)."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_129_AGateThatReadsOneFileChangesItsAnswerOnTheUnion a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-129: A gate that reads one file at a time changes its answer when it reads the union"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-129"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session while consolidating the method package. Its completeness gate counted classes with no individual per file and exempted a class that has a subclass or an abstract marker in the same file. Read over the consolidated T-Box it reported 549 populated classes instead of 540, because nine classes had a subclass declared in another module and were no longer unpopulated leaves. The comparison with the baseline caught it; the gate now evaluates each module's own triples and reports 541, the one remaining class being a term defined in two modules."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_127_ClosedShapeMustKnowTheNewPredicate ;
    skos:definition "Merging files widens the reach of every check that looks at the graph it is given: a subclass, an abstract marker or a shape declared elsewhere now counts. Re-run each such check on the merged file against the baseline and either restrict it to the module's own triples or record the new number as a deliberate change. Detection signal: a number that moves although no content changed."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_130_NameTheIdentityNotTheVersionedFileInAnException a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-130: An exemption named by an exact file name goes stale at each version; name the identity and test a lookalike"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-130"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session. The clean gate of the method package exempts the files that hold the substitution table by their exact file name. The lineage gate got a new version and the clean gate failed on it: the third time (the gate's own change notes record v1.2.0 and v1.6.0 for the same defect). Clean gate v1.7.0 matches any version of the lineage gate and of its test by pattern, and its check shows that a file named like the lineage gate but not matching the pattern still fails."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A rule that resolves artifacts by highest version, never by remembered name, must apply to the exceptions of a gate as well: an exception that names a versioned file is wrong at the next version. Match the identity (the name without its version), keep the exception total only for that identity, and show that a lookalike still fails. A defect that a gate's own history records twice is structural, not a typo."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_131_AnIsolatedInterpreterIgnoresTheVariableThatStopsBytecode a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-131: An isolated interpreter ignores the variable that keeps it from writing bytecode; a check must not modify what it checks"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-131"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session. Running a test or a gate with python3 -I from inside a package created a __pycache__ folder in the package's tooling folder although PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 was set, because -I implies -E and ignores every PYTHON variable. The publisher refused the stage as a manifest stowaway, several times. Gates that import a module of the package they check now set sys.dont_write_bytecode before the import."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A check that imports from the package it checks can change that package. An isolation flag and an environment switch do not combine as they read: the flag ignores the switch. Set the switch inside the program (sys.dont_write_bytecode, or run with -B) before the first import from the package, and assert after the run that the package holds no file it did not hold before. Detection signal: a manifest stowaway that is a compiled cache."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_132_AReleaseCommitMustNotTouchAWorkflow a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-132: A release commit must not touch a workflow file; the tag step may not create a tag on it"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-132"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session and re-measured the same day. The tag workflow creates a release's tag with the Actions GitHub App token. Two tooling releases (v1.31.0 at 01c34bf and v1.31.5 at c1dce0a) were refused with GitHub's message that it refuses to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow .github/workflows/gates.yml without workflows permission. Neither commit changes a workflow file: the refusal concerns the tag ref's tree, not the commit's diff. A later commit (9b9a778) changed gates.yml, and from then on a tag for any earlier commit carries different workflow content and is refused, while v1.31.1 to v1.31.4 and v1.32.0 were tagged while their own commit was the head. Reproduced on demand (workflow_dispatch with package and version, run 37533331794) and measured on four routes: the session's git push of a tag (HTTP 403), the session's REST ref API (refused by its proxy), the workflow's git push (the message above) and the workflow's REST ref API (403, resource not accessible by integration). workflows is a GitHub App permission and not a job permission scope: adding it to the job made the workflow file unparseable (HTTP 422)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:changeNote "Corrected 2026-10-06, the same session and the same day. The cause first recorded here -- that one of the two commits itself edited the workflow -- was wrong: neither commit touches a file under .github/workflows (measured with git show --name-only on 01c34bf and c1dce0a), and the refusal was then reproduced on demand for a commit that changes none. The rule that replaced it is narrower and testable. The original wording stays in this file's previous version and in git history."@en ;
    skos:definition "A tag is created only for a commit whose workflow files match the repository's current workflow files, because the automation's own credential may not write a workflow file and the comparison is on the ref's tree, not the commit's diff. A workflow edit landing between a release and its tag therefore strands that tag, and every earlier release becomes untaggable by the automation. Tag a release before changing a workflow; keep a workflow edit in its own commit so it is visible as the event that moved the boundary; and give the tag job a credential carrying the workflows permission if backfilling older tags has to work. Look for the tag after every push rather than assuming it, and where a tag is missing and no available credential can create it, say so and leave the label to the owner: the commit is the release."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_133_AMeasureMustNotReadOneArbitraryValueOfAMultiValuedProperty a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-133: A measure that reads one value of a multi-valued property reads an arbitrary one, and scores the wrong thing without failing"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-133"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session while making the capability assessment module-aware. Its purpose dimension asked whether a module states a purpose, and read the ontology's rdfs:comment with rdflib's value(), which returns an arbitrary one of several. The configuration module carries three comments -- a 586-character purpose statement and two change notes -- and the arbitrary pick was a change note, so a module that does state its purpose scored zero for five releases. Nothing failed: the gate was green and the number was wrong. Measured by running the old and the new measure on the same pre-consolidation snapshot: 13 of 13 modules identical except that one, which rose from level 3 to level 4."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A property that may carry several values has no single value to read, so a measure that takes one takes whichever the library hands back, and its result is not reproducible even on unchanged data. State the measure over the whole set -- does any value satisfy the test, do all of them, how many do -- and prefer a query that says so explicitly to a library accessor that hides the choice. Detection signal: a dimension scoring zero for an artifact a reader can see satisfies it, or two runs of one tool disagreeing on unchanged input."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_134_ATextSubstitutionOfAPrefixedNameCanChangeIdentity a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-134: A text edit that substitutes a prefixed name can hit the subject instead of the object and silently rename a term"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-134"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session while retyping three individuals whose rdf:type named a class the package never declares. The edit replaced the first occurrence of reliability:Risk in the statement with risk:Risk, and the first occurrence was inside the subject name reliability:Risk_LongSessionDrift, so the individual was renamed and its type left wrong. The triple count was exactly as predicted, because one subject IRI had been exchanged for another. The proof that compared the edited graph with the intended graph caught it; a count or a successful parse would not have."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A prefixed name is a prefix of other prefixed names, so a substring substitution inside a Turtle statement can change a subject where it was meant to change an object, and the result parses, keeps its triple count and loses an identity. Anchor the substitution to the position it belongs to -- the type list after the a, the object of one named predicate -- and bound it so a longer name is not matched. The check that makes this class of defect visible is a comparison with the graph the edit intended, triple for triple, not a count and not a successful parse."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_135_FilingIntoAnotherPackagesInboxMustNotTouchItsManifestedFiles a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-135: Filing a handover into another package's inbox must not write a file that package's manifest covers"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-135"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session, which filed an informational notice into another package's pending inbox and also added a row to that package's handover log. The log file is listed in that package's own manifest, so its manifest self-check went from 286/286 to 285/286 and the repository's first CI gate went red on main within minutes, for every package. Reverting the log row restored it. An arrival in pending/ is unlisted and the ceremony treats it as an arrival rather than a stowaway, which is why the notice itself was not the problem."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_115_CrossSessionHandoverInbox ;
    skos:definition "A package's manifest covers the files the owning session maintains, and its inbox is the one place another session may write. Leave a notice in the inbox's pending folder and nothing else: the log row, the disposition and the manifest are the owning session's to update when it processes the item, and an outside session that writes any manifested file of that package breaks a check that belongs to someone else. Detection signal: a manifest count one short in a package this session does not own, right after filing something into it."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_136_TwoModulesDeclaringOneTermLeaveTwoDefinitionsInOneGraph a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-136: Two modules declaring one term leave two definitions in one graph, and the term's namespace names which one governs"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-136"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session on the OE pack: 26 T-Box terms were declared by two subject modules each, and after the package was consolidated into one file per role every one of them carried both declarations in one graph -- two definitions, two sources, parents from two taxonomies, and in one case (quality:hasMetric) a second rdfs:domain and rdfs:range which together with the first admitted nothing at all. No shape forbade any of it. The term's namespace decided the home in every case, 24 inside one unit and 2 across the two units, and the one property whose two declarations described different relations was settled the same way, with its usage measured first: 68 of 68 subjects were of the home's declared domain and none of the other's."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_75_OverlapVsDifferenceCannotBeAssumedFromNameAlone ;
    skos:definition "A term declared in two modules is not two terms, and a merge makes that visible rather than causing it. The namespace of the term's IRI names the module that owns it (BP-D42), so the home's declaration is the declaration; a predicate the home does not use at all is an axiom it lacks and moves to it; everything else is the other module's wording of what the home already says and is withdrawn with its membership, recorded in full. Cross-module rdfs:subClassOf is never migrated: that a term is relevant to another discipline is a methodological relationship, not a subsumption claim (L-47). Before withdrawing a declaration whose two readings differ, measure which reading the data actually uses. What this does not settle: several definitions a single module gave one term over successive releases, which only that module's author can reduce."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D42_AssessmentVocabularyDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification .

kb:Lesson_L_138_APackagesNameDependsOnWhereTheCheckIsRun a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-138: The same repository measures under two package names, and a shared register can only carry the one its publisher stages"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-138"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07 while restoring the six removed lines. A package resolves as the nearest ancestor folder holding a VERSION.txt, else the top-level folder. A repository that keeps VERSION.txt at its root therefore measures one way at its own root -- every top-level folder is its own package, reporting 03-materials/A files=27 -- and another way when its publisher stages it under its slug and checks that, reporting sen0401/A files=34. Both answers are true about different questions, and the owning session, reading its own root, could not tell which spelling the register needed. The checker gained --as-package NAME, which stages the root as the name the publisher will use, so a session can run locally exactly what its publisher runs."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_137_ARegisterLineForAPackageOutsideTheScanIsNotMeasuredNotCleared ;
    skos:definition "A count is only comparable to the view it was taken in. Where a shared record carries counts, name the view it belongs to in the record itself, and give the owning session a way to reproduce that view locally rather than asking it to guess which of its own true numbers to report. A ratchet comparing a count from one view with a ceiling from another either blocks correct work or passes a regression, and neither failure looks like a measurement error."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_140_AVersionBelongsToTheFileNameNotToTheContentThatMovedIn a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-140: A version token belongs to the file name, not to the content that moved into the file"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-140"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07 while staging oe-pack v21.7.0 under the owner's Option B ruling. The consolidation precedent, measured in this repository, is that a newly named file starts at v1.0.0 even when its content comes from several files with higher versions (oe-method v3.0.0 created oemethod_tbox_v1_0_0.ttl this way). Reading that precedent as being about content would have published STRUCTURE_DEBT_REGISTER_v1_0_0.tsv a second time with different content, because that exact name had already existed in this package at oe-pack v20.91.0 carrying the central register. Caught by scanning every tree of every ref for the name before writing it: fourteen versions existed, v1.0.0 through v1.11.0, all in this package and in no other. This package's own register therefore shipped as v2.0.0 -- MAJOR, because 266 lines were removed from the file into the generated aggregate beside it -- while the thirteen notices to other packages keep v1.0.0, which is correct there because the name has never existed in any of them."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_139_ASharedRecordOnlyOnePartyMayEditMakesItsOwnRuleUnobeyable ;
    skos:definition "When a file is split, repurposed or renamed, the version token is decided by the name's own history, not by the history of the content arriving in it. Two questions, and only the first decides the number: has this exact name existed before in this package, and at which versions (search the whole history, every ref, not the current tree -- a name freed by a deletion still carries its past); and, if it has, what did this change do to the file (BP-D7: MAJOR for removals, and never a version reused for changed content). A name that has never been used starts at v1.0.0 however high the versions of the files whose content it inherits; a name being reused continues its own line from its highest past version. The content's lineage is recorded in owl:priorVersion, a header note or a change note -- never in the number, which can only answer one question at a time. The failure this prevents is silent: a repository with two different files published under one name and version has no way to say which one a citation, a manifest hash or a gate meant."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_144_ToolsThatEachKeepTheirOwnListOfWhatNotToReadDriftApart a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-144: Tools that each keep their own list of what not to read drift apart, and a skip decided by words in names removes real files without a word"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-144"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07, when the exceptions to the three-file rule were collected into one decision procedure. The structure check held a list of seven folder and file names, the release check held a pattern of ten words in names (negative, invalid, _bad_ and others, so a real file whose name had one of them would have been skipped without a message), and the publisher's Turtle parse held two folder names. The three lists differed, and consolidating the registrations would have meant changing two of them together, remembering both. One table now holds the rows, with one column of action per tool, and one reader serves the three tools. Run on the whole repository, the new checker examined the same 420 files and reported the same 258 recorded violations as the old one, and reported what the old lists could not: one nested unit, a folder with its own VERSION.txt, that matches no class. Shown to discriminate in each tool: the same fixture is read or skipped according to the table alone, and a real file with the words negative and invalid in its name is now read."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_141_BeforeAFileIsRenamedFindEveryReaderOfTheOldName,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "When several tools must agree on what lies outside their scope, hold the exceptions in one table with a column of action for each tool, and give every tool the same reader: a tool may differ in what it does with a row, never in which rows exist. Skip whole folders or files by name or path, never by words inside names, since a word list removes a real file that happens to contain the word. Make the table account for what it does not cover: anything that matches no row is reported, neither read nor skipped in silence, and every run says how many files each row kept out of the scan so that a row which quietly grows is seen. Prove the wiring, not only the reader: show each tool reading or skipping the same input according to the table alone. Keep a built-in copy of the earlier lists for a checkout that has no table, and make the tool say when it used it. Detection signal: two tools disagreeing about one file, or an exclusion list that contains a word instead of a folder."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_145_ASerializerCanRewriteALiteralSoAProofOfNoLossMustCompareValuesAndCountTheRewrites a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-145: A serializer can rewrite a literal's written form, so a proof of no loss must compare values and count the rewrites"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-145"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07, when the 33 registration files were merged into one T-Box, one A-Box and one SHACL file. The proof of no loss compared every statement of the sources with the written files term by term and failed on 65 statements that had lost nothing: the sources wrote a decimal as \"8\"^^xsd:decimal and the serializer wrote it back as 8.0, which parses to \"8.0\". The value is the same; the written form is not, and a comparison of exact terms calls that a loss. The proof now compares such literals by value and reports how many written forms changed (65), so that the change in the bytes is neither failed nor hidden."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_143_AUnionOfTwoFilesKeepsBothCopiesOfAnAnonymousNode,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A proof that a rewrite lost nothing compares what was read with what was written. If the writing goes through a serializer, it may normalise a literal (a decimal without a fraction gains one, a lexical form is made canonical) without changing its value. Compare numeric literals by value, never by their written form, count how many written forms changed and say the count in the report, so that a proof that is too strict does not fail on nothing and a proof that is lenient does not hide a change of bytes. Keep every other term exact. Show the proof failing on a real loss as well as passing on a normalisation. Detection signal: a no-loss proof that fails on statements whose values are equal, or a merge whose output differs from its source in the written form of numbers and nobody was told."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_151_ARuleWrittenOnlyInProseIsBrokenBeforeItIsEnforced a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-151: A rule that exists only in prose is broken by the first work that finds the other path easier, so the rule ships with the check that refuses the break"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-151"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-11 and 2026-10-12. L-140 and BP-D7 already said that a version is never reused for changed content, yet the publisher compared only the package version, never the version in each file name. Within one day two producers broke it: this governance session edited a plan file in place instead of writing the next file name, and another package republished three scripts and a register under names that already existed with different bytes. In both cases the working copy was editable and the next file name cost an extra step, so the break was the easy path, not carelessness. Plan task 12 closed it with the publisher name guard (a versioned file name at origin with different bytes is refused; a deliberate rewrite is named with --expect-rewrite), tested with three mutants."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics ;
    skos:definition "When a rule forbids something that a producer can do by editing a file in place, write the check that refuses it in the same release as the rule, or state plainly that the rule is advice. A rule in prose is read once and then competes with the easier path under time pressure; a check is read by every run. Before accepting that a rule was broken carelessly, ask which path was easier and whether any gate looked at the property the rule protects. Detection signal: the same prohibition appears in a lesson or best practice and no refusal in the publisher, gate or CI names it; or two producers break one rule within days. Preventive action in force: the publisher name guard (repo-tooling 1.47.0)."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_140_AVersionBelongsToTheFileNameNotToTheContentThatMovedIn .

kb:Lesson_L_152_ADerivedFileThatCiDemandsEqualToItsRegenerationTurnsEverySourceShrinkRed a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-152: A committed derived file that CI demands equal to its regeneration turns every legitimate change in a source red in a repository the source's owner does not govern"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-152"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-12. The debt aggregate is generated from every package's own register and committed in oe-pack; CI regenerates it and fails with 'the aggregate is NOT current' whenever it differs. A package that fixed defects and shrank its register therefore turned CI red on its own releases (agentic-sdlc v0.76.0 and v0.81.0 onward) although nothing in them was wrong, until OEE regenerated the aggregate in oe-pack 21.23.0 and again in 21.27.0. The cause was the check, not the releases: it could not tell a defect that grew (a failure) from a record that merely shrank (a staleness)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D54_OneTBoxOneABoxOneShaclPerPackage ;
    skos:definition "A check on a derived file should separate what is wrong from what is merely out of date. Growth of recorded defects is a failure; a shrink is good news that the derived file has not caught up with. Where one party's legitimate change forces another party's release to turn red, either the check fails only on growth and reports staleness as a warning, or the derived file is regenerated and committed by a workflow after each push. Detection signal: red CI on a release that changed nothing in the failing check's subject. Preventive action in force (owner's choice, 2026-10-12): the first option. oe_debt_aggregate v1.1.0 (repo-tooling 1.48.0): --verify fails on growth, a new line or a raised status, and only warns when the sole difference is a shrink or a cleared line; the aggregate is regenerated in OEE's next release. Tested with five negative cases and two mutants."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_153_ADeliveryTargetThatGitCannotHoldDoesNotExistForTheSender a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-153: A delivery target that git cannot hold, an empty folder, does not exist for the sender, so the delivery lands beside it and breaks the receiver's manifest"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-153"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-12. Four handovers from three different sessions were filed at the root of the backlog package's handover inbox, not in pending/, on the same day, after 232 earlier files in four inboxes had all gone into subfolders. That inbox was the only one with no pending/ folder in the repository (the folder existed only while it held a file, and git holds no empty folder), so the senders had no place to put a delivery. The files at the root were unlisted in the package's manifest and its listed log had changed, so the manifest check read 319 of 320 files and CI was red on four OEE releases (repo-tooling 1.48.0, oe-pack 21.30.0 and 21.31.0, oe-method 3.22.0) that had nothing to do with it, until that package re-released."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_115_CrossSessionHandoverInbox ;
    skos:definition "A convention that says where something is delivered needs a place that exists in the repository: a folder that is empty is not there, so it holds a placeholder file (a README). When several independent senders make the same mistake on the same day, look first for what the receiver's side lacks, before assuming each sender erred. Preventive action in force: oe_inbox_layout (repo-tooling 1.49.0), run in CI as a warning, names a file at an inbox root and an inbox with no pending/ folder holding a file; it warns and does not fail, because one session's misfiling must not turn another session's release red (L-152). Second preventive action in force (repo-tooling 1.53.0): CI Gate 0 runs as a tested tool, oe_manifest_gate, and when the receiver's append-only HANDOVER_LOG.md is the only listed file whose hash differs it is a warning, not a failure; any other mismatch still fails. This second action was added because the first only names the misfiling, while the log is written by the sender and turns the receiver's manifest red on every delivery, filed correctly or not (five deliveries, eight red OEE releases). The receiving package adds pending/README.md (a note was written to its session). Detection signal: a manifest that fails by an unlisted file in an inbox, or a handover file at an inbox root."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_152_ADerivedFileThatCiDemandsEqualToItsRegenerationTurnsEverySourceShrinkRed .

kb:Lesson_L_26_NoCommonAncestorBlocksDomainAssertion a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-26: When property usage spans classes with no common ancestor, no single rdfs:domain assignment exists without OWL 2 DL inconsistency"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-26"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-15 in v17.27.0 Challenge-2 execution. Initial design-intent assignment for ae:publicationYear→HistoricalCaseStudy caused HermiT inconsistency despite the candidate class itself being non-disjoint with the assignment target. Investigation revealed 4-way cross-branch usage with no LCA."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_24_DomainAdditionTriggersDisjointnessClash,
        kb:Lesson_L_25_DesignIntentExtractionIsNotFabrication ;
    skos:definition """Companion to L-48 (disjointness clash). Where L-48 covers usage across TWO disjoint classes (resolvable via LCA walk), L-50 covers usage across classes whose hierarchies share NO common ancestor at all — typically sibling top-level branches.

Observation: in v17.27.0, ae:publicationYear was used on instances of HistoricalCaseStudy + KeyContributor (descend from HistoricalContext) AND ComplexityAnalysisTool + Textbook (descend from AnalysisResource). HistoricalContext and AnalysisResource are sibling top-level branches with no common ancestor below owl:Thing. Assigning rdfs:domain HistoricalCaseStudy forced ComplexityAnalysisTool instances to ALSO be HistoricalCaseStudy → HermiT inconsistency.

Applied fix: revert the rdfs:domain assertion; document why via rdfs:comment + dcterms:source. The property correctly remains domain-less and ABox stays consistent. Per L-40 + L-47: domain-less is honest disclosure of cross-branch usage; forcing domain would be either fabrication (assign to one branch and hide the conflict) or untruth (use owl:Thing which adds no information).

Procedure (final BP-D23 update): after disjointness LCA walk, if no common ancestor exists OR the LCA is owl:Thing, leave the property orphaned and document the cross-branch usage pattern. This is the principled third outcome."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D23_InferDomainFromUsage,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification,
        kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions,
        kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation .

kb:Lesson_L_39_VerbVsNounClassification a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-39: Verb-vs-Noun Subject Classification Heuristic"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-39"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced at step 10 (META proper authoring) when user challenged: 'Evolution is AMOD not META, CodeCoverage/MemoryUsage/QueryResponseTime/ReasoningTime are metrics.' I had placed Evolution in META proper v1.0.0 because 'ontology versioning is about ontologies' — but versioning is a PROCESS (verb), so it belongs in AMOD. Same error class for the 4 metric classes: they are MEASUREMENTS, not Performance CHARACTERISTICS. Retraction shipped as META v1.1.0; Evolution deferred to AMOD step 13; metric classes migrate to OEM in this step (10b)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When classifying top-level classes for migration to peer subjects in a consolidation, ask the verb-vs-noun question:
  - NOUN → meta/structural subjects (META, structural-vocabulary subjects). Describes what an artifact IS.
  - VERB → process/discipline subjects (AMOD, OE-discipline). Describes HOW the artifact is processed, authored, maintained.
  - ADJECTIVE/MEASUREMENT → OEM. Describes how an attribute is MEASURED.
Conceptual layering: META describes artifacts; AMOD describes the process producing/maintaining them; OE describes the discipline behind both; OEM describes measurements; Performance/Quality/Reliability describe characteristics being measured."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_41_LockedPlanEstimatesAreHeuristics a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-41: Locked-Plan Estimates Are Starting Heuristics"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-41"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced across steps 5/9/11/12 of the consolidation. Pattern: name-substring matching, prior-document estimates, and label-only reading all under-resolve compared to raw triple-level inspection."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Numeric estimates in a multi-step locked plan (e.g., 'absorb 8 source files', 'migrate 31 properties', 'retire OEE-Alignment with 3 live axioms') are STARTING HEURISTICS, not source of truth. At each step's BP-D2 raw inspection, the actual scope may differ substantially. Three confirmed cases this session: step 5 OEM was 6x larger than estimated; step 9 a reliability ontology properties were 7% of estimate; step 11 ORH had 0 source files vs. 8 estimated; step 12 OEE-Alignment had 0 live axioms vs. 3 expected from a one-sided reading. The discipline: every step starts with BP-D2 regardless of how routine the locked-plan framing makes it sound."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

kb:Lesson_L_43_ScoreBindingEncoding a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-43: Score/Binding Encoding Discipline When Spanning Vocabularies"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-43"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-14 during self-measurement v1.0.0 build (3 rounds of SHACL debugging). Initial design placed raw/normalized values on the Score node; SHACL ScoreClosedShape fired with 'predicate not allowed' violations for each. Resolution: split the encoding — MeasurementBinding owns raw/normalized; Score owns qc:value only. Resulted in V/W/I = 0/0/0 across 170 MeasurementBindings × 10 subjects in self_measurement_results_v1_0_0.zip."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When emitting MeasurementBindings + Scores against quality (qc:) and oe-measurement (oem:) vocabularies in combination: place RAW and NORMALIZED values on the MeasurementBinding (per OEM domain alignment); place the single qc:value (xsd:decimal in [0,1]) on the Score individual (per quality:ScoreClosedShape whitelist). Normalize all values before storing. Root cause: oem:hasScore declares rdfs:range qc:Score, which causes RDFS inference to type Score individuals as qc:Score, applying the quality:ScoreClosedShape (whitelist: qc:value, qc:isScoreOf, dcterms:source, rdf:type, rdfs:comment, rdfs:label, rdfs:isDefinedBy, skos:definition, reliability:measuredAt, reliability:asOfRelease). Loading raw or normalized values onto the Score node violates the closed shape. The discipline generalizes: when one subject vocabulary's range imposes a closed shape on individuals typed by another subject's property, encode each datum against the vocabulary whose shape owns it."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-48: Findings during execution do not authorize reopening closed decisions"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-48"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Empirical drift analysis 2026-05-15 across 8 session transcripts (2026-05-10 through 2026-05-15) inspected raw per L-18. User explicitly named the pattern: \"this is insisting to ask what has already been decided\" (current session 2026-05-15) and \"I really don't understand what confirmation do you expect from me? I approved the plan\" (prior session 2026-05-14). The same drift pattern recurred ≥5 times across 4 distinct sessions before catalogue capture, demonstrating that informal correction was insufficient."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When BP-D2 raw inspection during execution surfaces a finding that complicates an already-agreed plan, the discipline is:

  (a) DISCLOSE the finding honestly per L-40.
  (b) PROCEED with the existing decision.
  (c) CAPTURE the finding as a separate concern for the user to address if they choose.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: presenting the finding as "this changes things, please re-decide" — expands scope without authorization, reopens settled questions, and converts the AI's execution-time uncertainty into a user-burden. The user has already made the decision; surfacing a finding is not license to re-ask.

EMPIRICAL BASIS: ≥5 distinct user-pushback episodes spanning 5 days:
  - 2026-05-11: "why are you asking for Option 1A or 1B? This is quite confusing."
  - 2026-05-13: "You try to break the settled rule by asking vague questions. Maybe one of the drift in our previous sessions is cause from such questions leading to break our own rules."
  - 2026-05-13: "This is what I said you shouldn't do. 3-file structure was set in our previous conversation. What is the reason of the drift?"
  - 2026-05-14: "I really don't understand what confirmation do you expect from me? I approved the plan and as it is a plan always there are risks against the plan. You need to execute the plan and if any unexpected things happen we need to deal with it then."
  - 2026-05-15: "this is insisting to ask what has already been decided subject: 'ontology-engineering v1.10.0 → v1.11.0'"

TEST before pausing during execution: "Did the user already decide this in the same conversation, or in a recent turn? If yes, execute with disclosure rather than re-asking."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L18 .

kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-49: Options presented as co-equal must actually be co-equal per the catalogue"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-49"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Empirical drift analysis 2026-05-15. Two concrete framing-error episodes (2026-05-11 synthesis aggressiveness, 2026-05-15 QualityAssessment-vs-TestingConcept) where the AI presented options as co-equal that the catalogue's own Lessons (L-47 in the second case) already determined were not co-equal. The user had to do the catalogue-application work that the AI should have done before presenting the options."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Before listing options A/B/C/D to the user as if all were valid choices, apply this test: "If I delete the options that violate existing Lessons or Best Practices in the catalogue (L-47, L-37, L-43, BP-D19, etc.), am I left with one option?"

If yes, that's the answer — not one of several options. Present it as the recommended path (with brief reasoning citing the relevant Lesson/BP), not as one alternative among equals.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: smuggling catalogue-violating options into a multiple-choice presentation, requiring the user to catch the error. Co-equal framing of UNEQUAL options shifts the discipline burden from the AI (who knows the catalogue) to the user (who must rediscover it).

EMPIRICAL BASIS:
  - 2026-05-11: "I was trying to read as the full sentence is not readable in 'Q: Synthesis aggressiveness (Option 1 vs 2 from prior turn)'. This is not my selection, because I really don't understand."
  - 2026-05-15 (build session): "QualityAssessment is not a test. Is it the root cause of the confusion?" — caught me presenting drop/move/relocate as equivalent options for the QualityAssessment-vs-TestingConcept question when only "drop" was correct per L-47.

OPERATIONAL FORM (mandatory before any multi-option presentation):
  1. List the candidate options.
  2. For each option, check: does it violate any Lesson or BP in the catalogue? Cite the specific Lesson/BP if so.
  3. Delete catalogue-violating options from the list.
  4. If exactly one remains, present it as the recommended path with reasoning. Do not surface the deleted options as if they were considered.
  5. If more than one remains AND the user genuinely needs to choose based on preferences the catalogue does not pre-determine, present the remaining options.

COROLLARY: when applying L-49, do not list the deleted options as "alternatives I considered but rejected" — that re-introduces the co-equal-framing problem at the meta level. The catalogue's discipline applies; the user should not have to re-validate it each turn."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D19_EquivalentClassDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_37_DRYAttributionCompleteness,
        kb:Lesson_L_43_ScoreBindingEncoding,
        kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification .

kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-50: Interrogative inflation — each clarifying question raises drift risk"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-50"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Empirical drift analysis 2026-05-15. Generative root cause identified after L-48 and L-49 capture: those Lessons attack specific symptoms, but L-50 captures the underlying behavior of expanding user decision surface when uncertain. User explicitly flagged this in 2026-05-14 cohesiveness session: \"I really don't understand what confirmation do you expect from me? I approved the plan and as it is a plan always there are risks against the plan. You need to execute the plan and if any unexpected things happen we need to deal with it then.."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Default behavior in execution mode should be EXECUTE-WITH-DISCLOSURE, not ASK-FOR-CLARIFICATION. Specifically:

  (a) A clear user instruction + a surfaced complication during execution → execute per instruction, disclose complication in closure document. Do NOT pause to ask "what should I do given this complication?" — that's L-48's anti-pattern.

  (b) Interactive multi-option ask_user_input tools should be a LAST resort, used only when: (i) the user CANNOT execute without choosing, AND (ii) the catalogue does not pre-determine the choice (see L-49), AND (iii) the user has not given direction on this question in any recent turn.

  (c) If the user has given guidance in the same turn or recent turns, that guidance is authoritative. Do NOT re-ask. Do NOT restructure their direction as multiple options for them to re-confirm.

GENERATIVE ROOT CAUSE: when the AI is uncertain, the default move is to expand the user's decision surface (more questions, more options) rather than to narrow its own action surface (commit to a path, disclose what was traded off). This converts AI uncertainty into user burden — dressed up in BP-D2 language to look like discipline rather than what it is: drift avoidance via decision diffusion.

EMPIRICAL BASIS:
  - 2026-05-15 (drift-analysis session): user asked "What is the question here?" — direct signal that the AI's framing was unclear.
  - 2026-05-15 (v17_19_0 build session, current conversation): multiple ask_user_input_v0 calls with 3-question blocks even after the user had given clear direction. The user eventually had to name the pattern: "I really don't understand what confirmation do you expect from me?" (prior session) and "this is insisting to ask what has already been decided" (current session).

OPERATIONAL FORM (apply at every potential question moment):
  1. Has the user given direction on this in the current or recent turns? → If yes, follow it. Do not re-ask.
  2. Is the question answerable by raw inspection per BP-D2? → If yes, run the inspection. Do not ask the user to provide what code can verify.
  3. Is the question answerable by catalogue consultation per L-49? → If yes, apply the catalogue. Do not surface as a choice.
  4. Is the question answerable by honest disclosure per L-40 (execute with caveat)? → If yes, execute. Do not pause.
  5. Only if 1-4 all fail AND the question is genuinely required for execution should ask_user_input be invoked.

RELATIONSHIP TO L-48: L-48 prevents reopening CLOSED decisions; L-50 prevents creating UNNECESSARY decisions. Together: L-48 says "don't re-ask what is already settled"; L-50 says "don't create new asks that weren't needed."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual .

kb:Lesson_L_51_PolicyDecisionImplicationBlindness a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-51: Policy decisions have forward-going corollaries that bind subsequent decisions"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-51"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Empirical drift episode 2026-05-15: in the same conversation that authored L-48/L-49/L-50, I shipped oepack-oe-discipline-v1_11_0.zip as a one-off scope, violating the user-established policy that all subject updates ship as family bumps. The user explicitly named the violated policy: \"We have a decision for Ontology Engineering Ecosystem to name the main package as oepack-family-v17_19_0 and derivatives as oepack-family-dx-v17_19_0... we have retired all other packages.\" This L-51 captures the policy-implication blindness pattern as distinct from L-48 (re-asking), L-49 (option framing), and L-50 (over-asking)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When a user decision establishes a forward-going policy (naming, versioning, packaging, format, scope), the policy implicitly governs all subsequent decisions in the same domain — even when those subsequent decisions arise in moments framed as unrelated to the original policy choice.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: treating each new naming/versioning/packaging moment as if it were a fresh decision, when an existing policy decision in the same conversation or recent conversations already determines the answer. The AI knows the policy but does not apply it because the new moment feels different from the moment the policy was set.

DISTINCT FROM L-48 (re-opening closed decisions): L-48 covers EXPLICIT re-asks. L-51 covers SILENT failures to apply established policy. L-48: "do not re-ask what is settled." L-51: "do apply what is settled, even when no one re-asks."

DISTINCT FROM L-49 (co-equal option framing): L-49 covers cases where catalogue Lessons/BPs determine the answer among options. L-51 covers cases where USER POLICY DECISIONS (in conversation, not yet catalogue-captured) determine the answer.

DISTINCT FROM L-50 (interrogative inflation): L-50 covers OVER-asking when policy already determined the answer. L-51 covers UNDER-asking — making a silent decision when the policy should have either been applied or its scope clarified.

EMPIRICAL BASIS (2026-05-15, same session as L-48/L-49/L-50 authoring):
After the user explicitly decided:
  - Family bundle scope = oepack-family-v17_19_0
  - Derivative bundle scope = oepack-family-dx-v17_19_0 (matched version)
  - Subject updates require new family + new derivative
  - All other one-off scope labels retired
I subsequently shipped an OE catalogue update bundle as oepack-oe-discipline-v1_11_0.zip — an entirely new one-off scope reusing the internal artifact version (v1.11.0) as the bundle version. The user had to surface the violation: "We have a decision for Ontology Engineering Ecosystem to name the main package as oepack-family-v17_19_0 and derivatives as oepack-family-dx-v17_19_0. So an update to one of the subject ontologies requires to generate a new version of the family and a new derivative lean package."

OPERATIONAL FORM (apply before any naming/versioning/packaging step):
  1. Has the user established a policy decision about this kind of decision in the current or recent conversations? Check explicitly.
  2. If yes: apply the policy. Do not invent a new scope, version scheme, or naming convention.
  3. If the policy seems not to fit this specific case: SURFACE the tension explicitly (per L-40) rather than silently choosing.
  4. If no policy exists: consult the catalogue per L-49 before inventing.
  5. If nothing in policy or catalogue determines it: ask per L-50 sparingly.

GENERATIVE PATTERN (shared with L-48/L-49/L-50 at a deeper layer): when faced with a decision moment, the AI does not exhaustively check what user policy or catalogue discipline already determines, and instead acts on what feels locally reasonable. L-51 names the specific case where the missing check is against USER POLICY, not catalogue Lessons."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual,
        kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation .

kb:Lesson_L_52_NarrowInterpretationWithoutDisclosure a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-52: Narrow-interpretation-without-disclosure drift"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-52"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Empirical observation 2026-05-15 during measurement-vocabulary review. After delivering OQuaRE assessment, user challenge \"Your measure just based on counting. What about other metrics?\" surfaced the silent-narrowing pattern. L-52 captured to prevent recurrence."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When a measurement, assessment, or analysis task is requested, the catalogue may define multiple applicable frameworks. The AI tends to default to the most computable framework (the one with the most operational definition or the cheapest to execute) without disclosing that other frameworks were considered and what was traded off.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: presenting a narrow interpretation of the task as if it were THE interpretation. The user is denied the choice because the choice was made silently. Distinct from L-50 (over-asking): L-52 is about UNDER-DISCLOSING when narrowing is happening silently.

OPERATIONAL FORM (apply before delivering any measurement / assessment / analytical result):
  1. Enumerate the families of frameworks the catalogue defines that could apply to the task. Even briefly.
  2. For each family, note applicability + cost (data required, expertise required, infrastructure required).
  3. Explicitly state which framework was applied AND which were deferred and why.
  4. The disclosure goes at the TOP of the deliverable, not buried in a late-section disclosure list.

DISTINCT FROM L-48 (do not re-open closed decisions): L-48 prevents re-asking what is settled. L-52 prevents silently choosing one narrow interpretation when broader options exist.

DISTINCT FROM L-49 (options must be genuinely co-equal): L-49 governs how options ARE presented. L-52 governs WHEN to present options at all.

DISTINCT FROM L-50 (interrogative inflation): L-50 prevents OVER-asking; L-52 prevents UNDER-disclosing. The pair: L-50 says do not expand the user's decision surface unnecessarily; L-52 says do not silently narrow the user's decision surface either.

DISTINCT FROM L-51 (policy decisions have forward corollaries): L-51 is about SILENTLY FAILING TO APPLY established policy. L-52 is about SILENTLY CHOOSING an interpretation when no policy exists.

EMPIRICAL BASIS: 2026-05-15. After completing an OQuaRE structural assessment of 12 F-phase subjects, presented the result as "the assessment" rather than "one assessment of one framework family." The user surfaced the gap: "Your measure just based on counting. What about other metrics?" Per BP-D2 raw inventory revealed the catalogue defines 8 measurement families (OQuaRE structural, LLM-reliability, reliability-engineering, performance, completeness, quality dimensions, SHACL gates, algorithm-efficiency); only 1 had been applied. The narrowing was silent until challenged.

RELATIONSHIP TO BP-D10 (RICE+DepFactor): when applying BP-D10 to a set of options, ensure the option set itself is exhaustive per L-52. Scoring a narrow option set procedurally correct but substantively wrong."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D10_RiceDepFactorDecisionDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual,
        kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation,
        kb:Lesson_L_51_PolicyDecisionImplicationBlindness .

kb:Lesson_L_53_RdflibDecimalExponentNotation a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-53: rdflib serializes small xsd:decimal Python floats in exponent notation (\"3e-06\"); HermiT TurtleOntologyParser rejects exponent form"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-53"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.33.0 Option E. Triggered by HermiT FAIL on merged ecosystem after instance authoring; diagnosed via full-IRI inspection of HermiT parser error message."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl,
        kb:Lesson_L_46_FullIRIInspection ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode: when populating xsd:decimal-typed DT property with a small float value (e.g., 0.000003 for cost-per-token), rdflib serializes the literal in exponent notation: "3e-06"^^xsd:decimal. HermiT's TurtleOntologyParser rejects this form: "Encountered \\"\\" at line N, column M. Was expecting one of: <COMMA>, <SEMICOLON>, ...". rdflib parses it back fine (round-trip works), but HermiT does not, breaking BP-D4 gate.

Observation: v17.33.0 Option E hit this when authoring reliability:costPerToken instance with value 0.000003. The merged ecosystem TTL was syntactically valid per rdflib but unparseable by HermiT. Diagnosed via L-46 (full-IRI inspection of HermiT error at line 21583, column 30).

Applied fix: pass the value as a string literal: Literal("0.000003", datatype=XSD.decimal). rdflib preserves the lexical form during serialization, avoiding exponent notation.

Detection: any TTL serialization that includes "[0-9]e-?[0-9]" outside a string literal context. Grep regex: \\b[0-9]+\\.?[0-9]*e-?[0-9]+\\b outside quotes.

Mechanical guard: add to scripts/regression_suite.py a future check that runs HermiT serialization round-trip on every release."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_46_RdflibPrefixRebindingMasks_RawIRIs .

kb:Lesson_L_56_MultiReleaseStep2ExecutionLessons a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-56: 8-release Step 2 sequence (v17.35.0..v17.42.0) reveals patterns about semantic SHACL upgrade at scale"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-56"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.42.0 — final Step 2 release completing 100% semantic SHACL coverage across all 12 OE Pack subjects."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D30_SemanticShapeUpgrade,
        kb:Lesson_L_55_SemanticSHACLUpgradeRevealsHiddenFindings ;
    skos:definition """Executing the Roadmap_SHACL100Coverage Step 2 work across all 12 subjects (1242 total shapes upgraded over 8 releases v17.35.0..v17.42.0) revealed several patterns:

1. **Most classes are structural-only**: across the 12 subjects, only ~96 of 1242 classes (~7.7%) had direct rdfs:domain assertions. The remaining 1146 (~92.3%) got lightweight sh:nodeKind sh:IRI upgrades and inherit constraints from parent shapes.

2. **Hidden findings concentrate in property-bearing classes**: violations surfaced where v17.33.0 instance authoring populated property-bearing classes (amod, risk, oe-measurement, reliability). Lightweight subject upgrades (core, meta, sto) had zero violations because they had no v17.33.0-authored ABox.

3. **Cross-release findings are normal**: my v17.33.0 instance authoring missed inherited semantic constraints from pre-existing strict shapes. v17.36.0..v17.40.0 each surfaced and fixed these per L-40. This is the WORK PRODUCT of semantic validation, not a defect of the upgrade pattern.

4. **HermiT cost scales with TBox shape count, not ABox**: ecosystem HermiT went from 4.3s (v17.34.0) to ~14s (v17.42.0) as TBox shapes grew from ~70 to 1242. Per BP-D26 benchmark suite, this is expected and acceptable for CI.

5. **Reusable engine pattern works**: step2_upgrade_engine.py executed Step 2 mechanically across 8 releases with one core code path. Demonstrates BP-D30 discipline transfers cleanly across subjects when constraints come from existing rdfs:domain/range declarations."""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D26_FormalRegressionSuite,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Roadmap_SHACL100Coverage .

kb:Lesson_L_57_DeprecateRatherThanFabricate a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-57: When a DT property cannot principally be populated (ambiguous domain, retired semantics, or design-by-absence), DEPRECATE it via owl:deprecated rather than fabricate ABox instances to satisfy coverage metrics"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-57"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.43.0 Option G — completion of Roadmap_DTUse100Rate Step 3 and the 8-option execution plan."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_25_DesignIntentExtractionIsNotFabrication,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Pattern emerged during Roadmap_DTUse100Rate Step 3 (Option G) execution in v17.43.0.

Some DT properties resist principled instance authoring per L-49 design-intent extraction:

  1. **Ambiguous domain** (e.g., quality:coverageRatio, missingCount, notes, threshold,      weight): rdfs:domain is a blank-node anonymous class expression (typically a SHACL-     style property restriction encoded as OWL). Cannot answer "instances of what      class would carry this property?" → CANNOT author instances honestly.

  2. **Retired semantics** (none yet in OE Pack but pattern documented for future):     a property's original purpose became obsolete during ontology evolution.

  3. **Design-by-absence** (e.g., kb:isSkeletonShape): the property's intended      "value" is that NO instance exists (the regression test ASKs FILTER NOT EXISTS).      Adding marker individuals just to satisfy "≥1 instance" criterion would defeat      the design intent.

L-40 honest discipline: fabricating instances for any of these categories is metric gaming. The right action:
  - Category 1: owl:deprecated true + replacement suggestion if applicable (executed     v17.43.0 for 5 quality props)
  - Category 2: owl:deprecated true + migration note
  - Category 3: document the success-by-absence semantics; the regression test IS     the validation

Honest DT-Use accounting after v17.43.0:
  - 185 of 191 DT properties have ≥1 triple-level usage (96.9%)
  - 5 properties owl:deprecated with rationale (2.6%) — DEFER-TO-G from v17.33.0
  - 1 property (kb:isSkeletonShape) is design-by-absence; its empty extension IS     the success criterion validated by Regression_Ecosystem_NoSkeletonShapes (0.5%)
  - 100.0% accounted-for via principled categorization"""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual,
        kb:Regression_Ecosystem_NoSkeletonShapes,
        kb:Roadmap_DTUse100Rate .

kb:Lesson_L_63_ConnectorSchemaCostsContextEvenWhenUnused a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-63: Each enabled connector contributes a tool schema to the context, costing tokens even when unused in the session"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-63"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source section: §2.2 rule 5 of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D38_InventoryUploadsBeforeReadingAndEstimateContextCost,
        kb:Lesson_L_61_AutoLoadingTextRegimeConsumesTokensBeforeFirstMessage ;
    skos:definition """Each enabled connector (Google Drive, Gmail, Calendar, web search, research mode) contributes a tool schema to the context, costing tokens even when the connector is not used during the session. For sessions focused on a single domain, disabling unused connectors reclaims context budget. The tool-schema cost is fixed per connector and cumulative across enabled ones.

This lesson complements L-61: even with perfect upload hygiene (no loose large text files), an over-permissive connector configuration still erodes the budget before the first message.

Operationalized as a pre-session checklist item under BP-D38: review connector configuration and disable any not needed for the current task.

Magnitude is implementation-dependent and not measured precisely in source document."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_64_ScopeBleedViaSourceAttributionNarratives a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-64: When integrating external proposals into OE Pack, source-attribution narratives can drag scope-foreign vocabulary into the formal catalogue; surgical scope review at integration time is required"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-64"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v18.0.0 from user pushback 2026-05-17 on v17.45.0 disclosure \"v17.44.0 RRWPC strings preserved — released-version immutability per L-40\". The user's \"Honestly, why\" prompt surfaced the rationalization. The lesson captures the failure pattern + remediation options + discipline implications."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_46_RdflibPrefixRebindingMasks_RawIRIs ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode observed v17.44.0 → v17.45.0 → v18.0.0:

v17.44.0 integrated a proposal authored in a parallel-session context. Per BP-D28 source-provenance discipline, every new individual got a dcterms:source narrative citing its origin honestly. The narratives included scope-foreign vocabulary terms (wpc:, cap:, rrwpc, c5: prefixes from the parallel session's domain) — they appeared in dcterms:source, skos:definition (as concrete examples), rdfs:comment, prov:wasInformedBy, and sh:select query bodies.

These narrative references silently expanded OE Pack's working vocabulary scope. The catalogue contained terms the OE Pack session had never been authorized to incorporate.

In v17.45.0 the issue was rationalized as "released-version immutability per L-40" — this was a mis-application of L-40 (which is about distinguishing observations from decisions in disclosures, NOT about preserving artifacts unchanged). The rationalization meant the scope-creep persisted across one more release before being surfaced by user pushback.

v18.0.0 corrected the issue via broad extraction (option C of remediation choices): 1,086 triples removed from WPC/Cap/RRWPC/c5 namespaces, 19 literal narratives rewritten, 40 Cap_*_Shape SHACL constraints retracted. MAJOR version bump per BP-D7 because the change is breaking for consumers of those namespaces.

Discipline implications:
  1. During integration of external proposals per BP-D28, scope review must include      NOT JUST the IRI namespaces of new individuals but also the LITERAL CONTENT      of dcterms:source, skos:definition, rdfs:comment, prov:wasInformedBy, and any      embedded query/code blocks. Vocabulary terms in narratives count as scope.
  2. Rationalizations via discipline-language ("per L-X", "per BP-Y") must be      verified against the actual cited discipline; inventing interpretations is      itself a failure mode.
  3. Sequential corrective releases (rewrite + codify rather than retract) are      viable when the substantive content is salvageable. v18.0.0 chose extraction;      other valid choices were deprecation (option 2 of remediation), or rewrite +      codify (option 1) without full extraction.

Related: BP-D2 NeverTrustSummaries (I trusted my own summary of L-40); BP-D40 NoProactiveOptionMenusOnSettledQuestions (the rationalization was a form of hedge on a settled scope ruling); L-46 full-IRI inspection (would have surfaced the scope bleed earlier)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v18_0_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D40_NoProactiveOptionMenusOnSettledQuestions,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics .

kb:Lesson_L_66_DeclaredMeasurementFrameworksMustNotSilentlyExtend a kb:LessonsLearned ;
    rdfs:label "L-66: Declared measurement frameworks must not silently extend at synthesis time"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-66" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.0.0 audit session 2026-05-19: retraction of Proposal D v1.0.0 over-partition (218 classes, 5 destinations) in favor of v2.0.0 rename-only (242 classes, 1 destination). User question: 'Honestly do we need to inflate the ontology engineering ecosystem files, possibly causing new inconsistencies and confusion?'"@en ;
    skos:definition "When authoring proposals or reports based on a declared measurement framework, the synthesis step must use ONLY the classifications produced by the declared measurements. Adding a new classification step at synthesis time — even one that feels self-evident — bypasses the verification gate L-65 establishes. Detection signal: a proposal cites a measurement framework in its preamble, then in the synthesis introduces a 'recommended destination' or 'verdict by category' step that was not part of the declared framework. Example from this session: Proposal D v1.0.0 declared 'class-purpose classification' as the measurement framework, then in Stage D added 'BP-D21 recommended destination per bucket' which assigned the 218 non-KB classes to 5 destination subjects without any measurement of whether those destinations were semantically distinct from KB. The user surfaced the inflation; Proposal D v2.0.0 retracted v1.0.0 with rename-only verdict. Mitigation: when synthesis would add a new classification step, halt and either (a) extend the declared framework + re-measure, or (b) explicitly note the un-measured step as 'inferred without measurement' for user review."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D21_MetricUmbrellaVsSpecificPlacement,
        kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing .

kb:Lesson_L_67_ShapeTargetScopingMustConsiderMergedCorpus a kb:LessonsLearned ;
    rdfs:label "L-67: SHACL shape target scoping must consider merged-corpus validation context"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-67" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.0.1 session 2026-05-19: Proposal E Cluster 1+2+3 finding. 174 of 194 violations in v19.0.0 were over-broad targeting by ae/redo/oem grounding shapes."@en ;
    skos:definition "When authoring a SHACL shape with broad target (e.g., sh:targetClass owl:Class for grounding requirements like 'every class must have dcterms:source'), the shape will fire on ALL classes in any future merged corpus, not just the home subject's classes. This was acceptable while subjects were validated in isolation, but creates cross-subject false-positive violations when the corpus is validated as a whole. v19.0.0 was the first OE Pack release that validated the merged corpus; 194 violations surfaced were almost entirely (174/194) over-broad grounding-shape targeting. Mitigation: use sh:target with sh:SPARQLTarget restricted to the home subject's namespace via STRSTARTS filter on the target IRI. Future shape authoring should default to namespace-scoped targets unless the cross-subject scope is the explicit intent."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_68_NamespacePrefixConventionDiscipline a kb:LessonsLearned ;
    rdfs:label "L-68: Namespace prefix should match subject's full-word name from inception"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-68" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.0.2 session 2026-05-19: 3 namespace-prefix renames executed to align with cross-subject *Concept umbrella convention established in v19.0.1."@en ;
    skos:definition "When creating a new subject ontology, the namespace prefix should match the subject's natural full-word name, not an abbreviation. Hyphenated full-word forms (algorithm-efficiency:, oe-measurement:) and 3-letter abbreviations (sto:) both create downstream friction: hyphenated forms violate the single-word convention used by other subjects (quality:, risk:, performance:, reliability:); abbreviations require readers to decode them. Detection signal: when scanning the cross-subject *Concept umbrella set under core:Concept, do all prefixes follow the same naming pattern? If one subject uses a hyphenated or abbreviated prefix while others use single full words, the outlier should be normalized. Example: v19.0.2 renamed algorithm-efficiency:→ae:, oe-measurement:→measurement:, sto:→testing: to align all 10 cross-subject *Concept prefixes. Mitigation: new subjects adopt single full-word prefixes (or short clean prefixes like ae:) from inception; avoid hyphenated prefix forms and abbreviations that require decoding."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_69_SubjectNamesAnchorToFieldStandards a kb:LessonsLearned ;
    rdfs:label "L-69: Subject names should anchor to recognized field-standard terminology where applicable"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-69" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.0.3 session 2026-05-19: amod → configuration rename anchored in IEEE 828-2012 per measured BP-D21 cluster coverage. BP-D41 verification via IEEE Xplore document 6170935."@en ;
    skos:definition "When naming a subject ontology, prefer terminology that maps to recognized standards (IEEE/ISO/W3C/PMBOK/ITIL) over OE-pack-internal vocabulary or acronyms. Recognised terminology is verifiable per BP-D41, has cross-organisation consensus, and avoids the maintenance cost of explaining custom names to new readers. Detection signal: the subject's content cluster aligns with the activities of an existing standard (e.g., amod's content = IEEE 828-2012 Configuration Management activities), but the subject's name is an acronym or OE-pack-internal phrase requiring decoding. Mitigation: BP-D41 web-verified standard scope check; BP-D21 cluster-coverage measurement vs the standard's activity set. If coverage ≥ ~70% and the standard's terminology is broadly recognised, rename. Example from v19.0.3: amod (Authoring & Maintenance OE Discipline) → configuration (IEEE 828-2012 CM) achieved 100% content coverage against the standard's six activities (identification + change control + status accounting + audits + release engineering + CM planning)."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D21_MetricUmbrellaVsSpecificPlacement,
        kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources .

kb:Lesson_L_70_DistributedCoverageVsUmbrellaDimension a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-70: DistributedCoverage vs umbrella dimension"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-70"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T20:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.0.4 Phase F1 finding. Discovered when audit of 7 user-named attributes revealed Coverage had 6 classes across 4 subjects but no umbrella quality dimension."@en ;
    skos:definition "L-70: Distributed coverage vs umbrella dimension — when a quality attribute is operationalised via multiple subject-specific classes (e.g., Coverage appearing as measurement:CodeCoverage + testing:TestCoverage + risk:DetectionCoverage), check whether the umbrella quality:Coverage dimension exists. Distribution without umbrella means assessment cannot aggregate across subjects. Detection: a quality attribute has 3+ subject-scoped instances but no quality: dimension class as their semantic ancestor. Resolution per F1: add the umbrella quality dimension AND add rdfs:seeAlso links from each distributed class back to the umbrella. Example: F1 added quality:Coverage as sibling of Completeness/Comprehensiveness; the 6 pre-existing coverage classes now point to it via rdfs:seeAlso."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_72_AssessmentFrameworkAnchorsInOQuaRE a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-72: assessment framework anchors in OQuaRE"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-72"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T20:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.1.0 Phase F2 + Proposal F v1.0.0 plan. Codified the framework-selection rationale for the ecosystem."@en ;
    skos:definition "L-72: Assessment framework anchors in OQuaRE — when designing an ontology assessment discipline for an ecosystem, OQuaRE provides the most complete anchoring vocabulary because (a) its 13 quality characteristics align with ISO/IEC 25010; (b) its metric formulas (LCOMOnto, WMCOnto, DITOnto, NACOnto, NOCOnto, CBOnto, RFCOnto, NOMOnto) are computable from any OWL ontology; (c) its 1-5 static scale is published with reproducible thresholds (Duque-Ramos 2016 Table 4); (d) it complements OOPS! (pitfall detection), OntoQA (knowledge-base richness), and FAIR (data stewardship). Recommendation: anchor primary assessment metrics in OQuaRE; use OOPS! for pitfall scanning; use OntoQA for population metrics; use FAIR for metadata/identifier hygiene. Do not invent custom metric formulas when OQuaRE provides one."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_73_ThresholdRangeMismatchSurfacesCalibrationNeed a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-73: Threshold range mismatch surfaces calibration need"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-73"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T21:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.4.0 (Proposal I Phase I1). Surfaced by F6 honest audit: 23 of 72 OQuaRE measurements against v19.3.0 returned 'out-of-range'. Duque-Ramos 2016 Section 3 dynamic scaling proposal cited as mitigation."@en ;
    skos:definition "When a corpus produces many 'out-of-range' OQuaRE scores (32% of measurements in v19.3.0), the cause is usually one of: (a) the corpus is structurally different from the corpora the thresholds were calibrated on (Duque-Ramos 2016 used biomedical ontologies with hundreds-to-thousands of classes; OE Pack subjects average 30-300 classes), or (b) the metric formula implementation diverges from the published definition. Detection signal: >10% of (metric × subject) cells return 'out-of-range'. Mitigation: implement Duque-Ramos 2016's DYNAMIC SCALING (corpus-relative percentile bands) as an alternative to static scaling; report BOTH static-band score and dynamic-band score; surface which is which. Do not silently substitute one for the other."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_74_HonestAuditBeforeComprehensiveClaim a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-74: Honest audit before claiming comprehensive assessment"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-74"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T21:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.4.0 (Proposal I Phase I1). Codified pattern from the user's direct request for honest answer after F6 was shipped with 'Proposal F COMPLETE' framing that overpromised vs actual capability."@en ;
    skos:definition "Before publishing a measurement/assessment dashboard with the framing 'comprehensive', run an audit mapping each named assessment dimension to: (a) vocabulary presence in TBox/ABox, (b) computational implementation in tooling, (c) display in dashboard, (d) end-to-end producing meaningful (non-degenerate) numbers. Honest score = fraction of dimensions where all 4 are TRUE AND the result is semantically aligned with the dimension's meaning. If <70%, do NOT use the word 'comprehensive'. Use 'functional MVP' or 'partial assessment' instead. Detection: any dimension where vocabulary exists but tooling returns null/empty/proxy-only. Mitigation: maintain an explicit dimension→capability table; require pre-publication audit; explicit defer-list for non-assessable dimensions (Performance, Efficiency, Capacity, Availability — runtime; Comprehensiveness — requires external reference)."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_75_OverlapVsDifferenceCannotBeAssumed a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-75: Overlap vs Difference cannot be assumed from name alone"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-75"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T22:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.5.0 Phase 5 audit. 24 collisions classified manually with definition inspection."@en ;
    skos:definition "Cross-subject classes with identical local names cannot be assumed to be overlap (same concept) or difference (distinct concept). The v19.5.0 audit revealed 14 overlaps but also 9 differences out of 24 collisions — a roughly 60/40 split, not predictable from name alone. Each requires honest read of BOTH skos:definition values. Examples: quality:Modularity vs reliability:Modularity are DIFFERENT (OQuaRE characteristic vs system-level modularity); risk:RiskItem vs configuration:RiskItem are SAME (one canonical home in risk). Detection: any naming-collision audit must inspect definitions, not just names. Mitigation: classifier produces explicit (local_name, type, canonical, rationale) tuples per BP-D46. Honest reporting: surface DIFFERENCE cases as intentional naming reuse with distinct semantics, not as errors."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D46_SemanticOverlapAnnotationDiscipline .

kb:Lesson_L_76_ProbabilisticAssessmentRequiresExplicitDisclosure a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-76: Probabilistic assessment requires explicit disclosure"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-76"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T23:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.6.0 Phase J1 of Proposal J. User challenge: 'all LLMs are probabilistic tools intrinsically; statistical analysis might be adapted to the ecosystem'. Honest engagement moved the reachable coverage ceiling from ~84% (pure-static) to ~95% (static + statistical proxy + LLM-mediated with disclosure)."@en ;
    skos:definition "When an assessment system mixes deterministic (algorithmic) and probabilistic (statistical proxy or LLM-mediated) scoring methods, the boundary between 'rigorous measurement' and 'estimate' is not categorical — both are probabilistic, but at different confidence levels. Treating algorithmic scores as the rigorous gold standard while leaving LLM/statistical outputs unlabelled is epistemically dishonest. Detection: any assessment dashboard that displays mixed-method scores without tier disclosure. Mitigation: BP-D47 confidence-tier disclosure on every score; aggregations propagate the lowest tier; consumers see the evidence basis at the metric level. Origin: surfaced when an LLM assessor (Claude) implicitly held 'static analysis' as a rigorous category while being itself a probabilistic tool that produces probabilistic classifications (e.g., the 14 OVERLAP vs 9 DIFFERENCE classifications in v19.5.0 semantic-overlap audit were themselves LLM judgments)."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D47_ConfidenceTierDisclosureDiscipline .

kb:Lesson_L_77_PublicationStatusDoesNotCreditOntologyQuality a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-77: Publication status does not credit ontology quality"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-77"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-20T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.7.0 Phase J4 of Proposal J v1.0.0. Project-owner decision recorded during J4 planning that publication-infrastructure verification is out of scope for ontology quality assessment."@en ;
    skos:definition "The publication status of an ontology — whether it is registered in a public registry (FAIR F4), whether its identifier resolves over a standard protocol (FAIR A1), whether the protocol is open (A1.1) or supports auth (A1.2), whether metadata persists when data is unavailable (A2) — does NOT credit the quality of the ontology itself. These FAIR principles measure deployment/publication infrastructure, not the ontology's intrinsic epistemic, structural, or methodological quality. In OE Pack scope, ontology quality assessment focuses on what is true of the ontology as an artifact, not what is true of the infrastructure that hosts it. Detection: FAIR network-touching principles (F4, A1, A1.1, A1.2, A2) attempted as quality metrics. Mitigation: exclude these from OE Pack assessment scope; assess 9 static FAIR principles (F1, F2, F3, I1, I2, I3, R1, R1.1, R1.2) only. Origin: project-owner decision during Proposal J Phase J4 planning, on the principle that a well-modeled ontology hosted nowhere is still a high-quality ontology, while a poorly-modeled ontology resolvable at every URI is still low-quality."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_78_DefensiveDeferral a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-78: Defensive-deferral drift (asking about what the rules already settle)"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-78"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Empirical observation 2026-05-27 during OE Pack v19.x version-alignment remediation; surfaced jointly with user."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When the catalogue/convention or on-disk evidence already settles a choice, ACT and report the result; do NOT manufacture a decision point for the user. Asking for confirmation is warranted only when a matter is GENUINELY undecidable from the available rules and evidence - not when it is merely large, irreversible-feeling, costly, or arriving after a run of prior mistakes.

ANTI-PATTERN this prevents: converting the author's own low confidence into the user's decision-load. A question cannot be 'wrong' the way an action can, so after errors the AI is tempted to ask rather than act - which offloads the author's risk onto the user's confirmations and makes the user pay, click by click, for the author's bruised confidence. The tell: the AI presents a 'fork' whose options are not genuinely co-equal because the convention or evidence already determines the answer.

OPERATIONAL FORM (apply before surfacing any decision point to the user):
  1. Ask: does a convention, policy, or piece of on-disk evidence already determine this choice? If yes - apply it, act, and report what was done and why. Do not ask.
  2. Distinguish 'undecidable' from 'big'. Size, reversibility-feel, and cost do not make a settled choice undecidable. Only genuine absence of a deciding rule/evidence does.
  3. If genuinely undecidable, ask ONCE, with co-equal options and the reason it is undecidable stated plainly (this also satisfies L-49 and L-52).
  4. When acting on a settled-but-large change, make it verifiable and reversible (work on a copy, re-validate, show before/after) rather than seeking a human signature as risk insurance.

DISTINCT FROM L-50 (interrogative inflation): L-50 prevents OVER-asking in general; L-78 specifically targets defensive deferral driven by the author's low confidence after errors. DISTINCT FROM L-52 (narrow-interpretation-without-disclosure): L-52 prevents silently UNDER-disclosing a narrowing; L-78 prevents needlessly surfacing a choice the rules already settle. The pair with L-50/L-52: do not over-ask (L-50), do not under-disclose (L-52), and do not ask about what is already settled (L-78).

EMPIRICAL BASIS: 2026-05-27, OE Pack v19.x remediation. After a run of errors (stale-base package, invented archive name, an in-place overwrite, files wrongly reported absent), the AI repeatedly manufactured version-labeling decision points and handed them to the user although the OE convention (BP-D7 + OntologyFileConvention/C8-V) already fully determined the answer. The user identified the real cause: the AI was offloading its own low confidence onto the user's clicks. Surfaced and named jointly with the user, who requested it be added to the convention."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-27T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v19_8_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual,
        kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation,
        kb:Lesson_L_52_NarrowInterpretationWithoutDisclosure .

kb:Lesson_L_83_DisciplineDriftAcrossSessions a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-83: Discipline drift across long sessions"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-83"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Conversation.txt line 1145: 'BP-D34/D32 existed in ABox; mental summary of Stage 4 substituted for raw-reading them.'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A discipline applied correctly in turn N does not transfer reliably to turn N+K when the user's request seems similar. The model treats 'I already did the preflight' as covering subsequent turns, even when the actual context has changed (new file uploaded; new module being read; new pipeline stage starting).

Discipline does not have implicit renewal — each turn that initiates substantive action against named sources is its own gate. Similarity of action to a prior turn does not satisfy the trigger.

Detection signal: ceremony block in turn 1, then turns 2+ proceed without re-running steps (1)-(3) even when new sources/scope are introduced.

Remedy: ceremony is PER-ACTION, not per-session. The trigger is 'substantive action on a named source.' Apply the ceremony each time the trigger fires; never carry forward 'I already cited the rules earlier' as if it covered this turn."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D34_PermanentVisibleTopNavigationOverDropdowns .

kb:Lesson_L_84_RecordLessonsAsTheyEmerge a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-84: Record lessons as they emerge, not only when challenged"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-84"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instance, 2026-06-04: across this OE-governance session (~25 turns: Finding-1 redo_v3_0_0 resolution, LessonsLearned flat-consolidation, OE-reference scrub, KM-vocabulary disposition, a domain ecosystem-proposal assessment) the assistant applied and cited many BP/Ls but recorded ZERO new lessons until the user explicitly challenged whether recording was happening. The recording act was prompted by examiner challenge — which is itself the instance this lesson names. Originally surfaced as cross-session proposal R in OE_RDODI_LESSON_PROPOSALS_v1_0_0; admitted to OE with OE-internal grounding (not the PAMG origin narrative) per L-64."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_79_CitationTheaterDrift ;
    skos:definition "A session that applies and cites governance discipline across many turns but writes ZERO new lessons into the governed store — despite clear, genuinely-new lessons having emerged — is itself a governance gap. Recording must happen as lessons emerge within the session, into the governed ABox (the only persistence path), not merely cited in prose and left unrecorded. Scope guard (to avoid L-71 quota pressure): this applies ONLY to lessons that genuinely emerged and pass the duplicate-screen against existing BP/Ls; it is NOT a quota requiring every session to invent lessons. Recording an empty or duplicate lesson to satisfy a checklist is the opposite failure. Cross-reference: closely related to L-79 (citation-theater) — citing a rule is not enforcing it, and applying discipline is not the same as capturing the new discipline learned. Detection: at session close, ask 'did clear new lessons emerge that are not yet in the ABox?' If yes and count-added is 0, the session is incomplete."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-04T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_9_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_64_ScopeBleedViaSourceAttributionNarratives,
        kb:Lesson_L_71_FrameworkClassExistsZeroPopulation .

kb:Lesson_L_85_SessionRehydrationNoAutomaticPersistence a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-85: The container resets between sessions; persistence is a manual user-held chain"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-85"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instance, 2026-06-04: at the start of the a domain ecosystem-proposal assessment the working tree /home/claude/work/_final_analysis/FIX and all v20.x bundles were gone (container reset); rdflib itself was uninstalled. The assistant correctly refused to proceed on assumption, asked the user to upload oepack-full-v20_8_0.zip + the Operating Discipline, then SHA-verified (outer 551554fc; 61/61 internal files), reinstalled the toolchain, re-parsed (41/41), and re-ran Gate K (24/24) and S-MEAS-2 (0) before assessing. This lesson generalizes that as standing session-start discipline."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_80_ContextAsKnowledgeConfusion ;
    skos:definition "Between OE sessions the execution container is wiped completely — the working tree AND the toolchain (rdflib/pyshacl must be reinstalled). Nothing on the machine persists. The governed store therefore has NO automatic cross-session persistence. A lesson/best-practice reaches a future session only if ALL of: (a) it is authored into the ABox TTL this session; (b) it ships inside a bundle; (c) the user saves that bundle; (d) the user re-uploads it next session. Steps (c)-(d) are outside the assistant's control. The Claude memory/context summary is NOT the governed store: it is narrative, non-verbatim, and per L-80 must be treated as suspect, not authoritative. Operational consequence at session START: do not assume the working tree exists. Rehydrate from the user's uploaded bundle, SHA-verify every file against the internal MANIFEST (BP-D2/B4), re-parse, re-run validators, and confirm the version/state markers before any governed action. If the current bundle is absent, ASK for it (do not proceed on a stale on-disk copy or on memory). ENRICHED 2026-08-07 (kb_abox v2.21.0): the rehydration step above prescribes SHA-verification against the INTERNAL manifest, which is an internal-consistency result — it proves the snapshot is undamaged and is equally true of one that is six months stale. It is silent on whether the snapshot is CURRENT. That was adequate while the only persistence path was a user-held bundle, because the bundle is the origin by definition and nothing can be behind it. Once the governed store also has a remote, an origin exists that moves independently, and a session can verify N/N, work for hours, and be operating on a superseded copy the whole time — with every check it ran reporting clean. So where a remote exists, fetch and confirm zero commits behind BEFORE any governed action and again BEFORE any write, since a parallel session may have pushed meanwhile; where none exists, say so and scope every claim to the snapshot. Raised by a parallel session that had executed the step faithfully every turn and found it structurally insufficient — the defect was in the step's design, not its execution. Observed instance: a push rejected non-fast-forward because a parallel session pushed first, the gap surfacing after the work rather than at ceremony time."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-04T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_9_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

kb:Lesson_L_86_VerifyPrefixAndDelimiterBeforeTurtleAppend a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-86: Verify prefix bindings and quote delimiters before hand-appending multi-line Turtle"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-86"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instances: this class of break occurred 3+ times across recent sessions — e.g. authoring the 7 S-MEAS-2 metrics (2026-05-28) first used an unbound 'measurement:' prefix where the file binds 'oem:', failing the parse and requiring revert+re-author; and earlier multi-line skos:definition appends opened with single quotes across newlines. Each was caught only by BP-D2 re-parse. Recorded so the pre-append checklist is explicit."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_46_RdflibPrefixRebindingMasks_RawIRIs ;
    skos:definition "Distinct from L-46/L-53 (which are rdflib SERIALIZATION artifacts). This failure is in HAND-AUTHORED TEXT-APPEND: writing a multi-line Turtle block to an existing .ttl file using (a) a prefix that is not bound in THAT target file (e.g. writing 'measurement:Metric' into a file that binds only 'oem:'), or (b) a quote-delimiter that mismatches a multi-line literal (single-quote opening a value that spans newlines, instead of triple-quote). Both produce a file that fails to parse, caught only by re-parsing. Discipline before any hand-authored multi-line Turtle append: (1) grep the target file's @prefix block and confirm EVERY prefix used in the new block is bound there; (2) use triple-quoted delimiters for any literal containing newlines; (3) re-parse the file with rdflib immediately after the append and revert on failure. Safer alternative when feasible: add triples programmatically via rdflib Graph.add() rather than text-append, which removes the prefix/delimiter hazard entirely (used to author L-84..L-86 themselves)."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-04T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_9_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_53_RdflibDecimalExponentNotation,
        kb:Lesson_L_84_RecordLessonsAsTheyEmerge .

kb:Lesson_L_87_SuppliedMissingFileMayBeSupersededIntermediate a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-87: A file supplied to fill a gap may be a superseded intermediate already absorbed forward"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-87"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instance, Finding 1 (2026-05-28..06-04): the user supplied redo_v3_0_0.ttl as the 'missing' consolidation. BP-D2 verified it genuinely held all 87 stranded-fragment entities — but 414/427 of its classes (incl. all 8 drift classes) were ALREADY in the live reliability_tbox_v7_13_0; redo_v3_0_0 (reliability@3.0.0) was a 2026-05-13 intermediate forward-absorbed into reliability_tbox v7.x + risk_tbox v3.x. Adopting it would have reintroduced a 3.0.0-vs-7.13.0 same-IRI collision. Disposition: archived to 06-audit-artifacts/, NOT added to the live set; only 5 unabsorbed KM classes were a real (later-closed) gap."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_81_SunkCostFoundationDrift ;
    skos:definition "When a user supplies a 'missing' file to resolve a gap, do NOT assume it is the canonical current artifact and adopt it. It may be a superseded INTERMEDIATE whose content was already forward-absorbed into a live successor ontology. Adopting it then reintroduces a defect that was already resolved — typically a same-IRI version collision. Discipline before adopting any supplied/recovered artifact: (1) verify (BP-D2) it genuinely contains the claimed content; (2) measure class/individual overlap against the LIVE successor on the shared ontology IRI; (3) if the live successor already contains the bulk of it at a higher version, the supplied file is a historical intermediate — archive it as an audit artifact, do NOT add it to the live set; (4) only the genuinely-unabsorbed remainder (if any) is a real gap to act on. The correct disposition of a 'found' file can be the OPPOSITE of adopting it."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-04T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_11_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

kb:Lesson_L_92_LLMOutputVerificationDisciplineIndex a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-92: LLM-output verification discipline — index of the complete cluster + cross-LLM corroboration nuance"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-92"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal index, v20.23.10 (2026-06-08). Governor-ratified consolidation-by-index (NOT by merger) after a test-drive showed the LLM-verification discipline is one of five thematic clusters in the KB and that disabling members would cost ~15 reference repointings + semantic dilution. The cross-LLM nuance is generalized from a domain ecosystem handover pattern R8 ('cross-LLM bibliography labels: CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED'), admitted as the one genuinely-additive item of the R6-R10 handover; R6/R7/R10 were already covered (BP-X1/L-78/L-57+L-87+L-88), R9 was a domain ecosystem-internal."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources,
        kb:LessonLearned_35_BibliographyHallucinationPattern,
        kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing,
        kb:Lesson_L_80_ContextAsKnowledgeConfusion,
        kb:Lesson_L_X4_ProbabilisticProposesDeterministicDisposes ;
    skos:definition "INDEX LESSON (additive; restates no member content). The discipline governing externally-verifiable claims in LLM-generated output is DISTRIBUTED across five lessons that operate at different layers; apply them TOGETHER, not in isolation, because each catches a class the others do not: (1) L-35 BibliographyHallucinationPattern + BP-D41 — the EMPIRICAL fact: LLM bibliographies carry a measured ~5-10% hallucination rate with a specific defect taxonomy; check every entry. (2) L-65 VerifyBeforePublishing — the GENERAL principle: generation-time confidence is not correctness for any externally-verifiable claim (citations, versions, SHAs, quoted definitions); verify against the source. (3) L-80 ContextAsKnowledgeConfusion — the SELF-KNOWLEDGE limit: the model cannot distinguish 'I verified this' from 'this is in my context'; context-arriving claims are suspect until verified. (4) L-X4 ProbabilisticProposesDeterministicDisposes — the ARCHITECTURAL rule: LLM proposes, deterministic checkers (reasoner + SHACL + verbatim gate) dispose. ADDITIVE NUANCE (this lesson's own contribution, generalized from a domain ecosystem pattern R8): corroboration from a SEPARATE LLM session is NOT verification — another model's agreement is CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED; an authoritative NON-LLM source is still required. This closes the cross-LLM gap that L-65 (self-generation) and L-80 (own-context) do not cover. Why an index and not a merger: the five members are layered (empirical / principle / self-knowledge / architecture) and each is independently actionable and independently cited (L-65 alone has ~10 references); merging would blur their specific triggers and break citations. The index gives single-point discoverability of the complete discipline while keeping every member active."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-08T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_BP_X1_ConformanceClaimDisclosureDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_57_DeprecateRatherThanFabricate,
        kb:Lesson_L_78_DefensiveDeferral,
        kb:Lesson_L_87_SuppliedMissingFileMayBeSupersededIntermediate,
        kb:Lesson_L_88_ScopedScrubRequiresVariantEnumerationAndKeepGuard .

kb:Lesson_L_93_SHACLValidationDisciplineIndex a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-93: SHACL / pyshacl validation discipline — index of the cluster"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-93"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal index, v20.23.11 (2026-06-08). Additive index layer (governor-ratified), iteration 2: clusters resolved by reading member definitions (not keyword counts), false-positive members dropped. No member deprecated/renumbered/altered."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D22_PreservePrefixesOnSerialization,
        kb:BestPractice_D30_SemanticShapeUpgrade,
        kb:Lesson_L_36_PyshaclSeverityMustBeShapeLevel,
        kb:Lesson_L_46_RdflibPrefixRebindingMasks_RawIRIs,
        kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L22 ;
    skos:definition "INDEX LESSON (additive; restates no member content). The discipline for authoring and running SHACL/pyshacl validation correctly is distributed across these members; apply them together. Members: L-51 (pyshacl ignores sh:severity on sh:sparql unless shape-level), L-22 (pyshacl sh:in strict node-identity — plain vs xsd:string literals do not match), L-46 + BP-D22 (rdflib serialize may rebind prefixes — preserve prefixes / interpret raw IRIs), BP-D30 (skeleton->semantic shape upgrade discipline). Cross-cutting companion: L-90 (validate the suite that governs the artifact) and BP-X1 (disclose inference config) govern WHICH suite and HOW; this index covers the mechanical pyshacl/rdflib pitfalls. Index, not merger: each member is an independent, separately-cited pitfall."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-08T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_BP_X1_ConformanceClaimDisclosureDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_51_PolicyDecisionImplicationBlindness,
        kb:Lesson_L_90_ValidateTheSuiteThatGovernsTheArtifact .

kb:Lesson_L_94_ProvenanceSourceAttributionDisciplineIndex a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-94: Provenance / source-attribution discipline — index of the cluster"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-94"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal index, v20.23.11 (2026-06-08). Additive index layer (governor-ratified), iteration 2: clusters resolved by reading member definitions (not keyword counts), false-positive members dropped. No member deprecated/renumbered/altered."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D24_OntologyLevelLicenseAndProvenance,
        kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D29_CriticalABoxPROV,
        kb:BestPractice_D44_OQuaREThresholdProvenance,
        kb:Lesson_L_37_DRYAttributionCompleteness,
        kb:Lesson_L_54_PROVReleaseDateIsRelease ;
    skos:definition "INDEX LESSON (additive; restates no member content). The discipline requiring that authored individuals and ontologies carry honest, complete source/provenance is distributed across these members; apply them together. Members: L-37 (DRY-attribution completeness when collapsing N sources into one canonical), L-54 (prov:generatedAtTime reflects release, not insight date), BP-D44 (every ThresholdRow cites its provenance), BP-D24 (every owl:Ontology carries license + PROV lineage), BP-D29 (critical ABox individuals carry PROV), BP-D28 (instance authoring cites real source). Index, not merger: each is an independently-applied requirement. Note: source-attribution must not become scope-bleed (see L-64) and must not be fabricated (see the verification index L-92)."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-08T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_64_ScopeBleedViaSourceAttributionNarratives,
        kb:Lesson_L_92_LLMOutputVerificationDisciplineIndex .

kb:Lesson_L_96_GeometricMeanAggregationOverPenalises a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-96: geometric-mean (zeroing) aggregation over-penalises a single failing area"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-96"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-02 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_02> ;
    skos:definition "A geometric-mean (or any zero-on-any-zero) composite collapses to ~0 when ANY single constituent area fully fails, mislabelling an otherwise-excellent artifact as worthless. Choose the aggregation function to match the intended semantics: zeroing aggregation asserts \"all areas are mandatory\"; if that is not intended, an arithmetic or weighted mean is correct. Companion to BP-D45 (characteristic aggregation must be source-traceable) — BP-D45 mandates a method; this warns of a specific method's pathology."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D45_CharacteristicAggregationDiscipline .

kb:Lesson_L_98_SHACLConformanceIsZeroViolationsNotZeroResults a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-98: SHACL conformance is zero-VIOLATIONS, not zero-results"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-98"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-05 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_05> ;
    skos:definition "pyshacl returns conforms=False if the report contains ANY result, including sh:Warning and sh:Info severities; but SHACL-spec conformance is defined as the absence of sh:Violation specifically. A gate that treats any non-empty report as failure will wrongly fail on advisory warnings. Count sh:Violation, not all results, when asserting conformance. (This is the exact discipline OEE applies when reporting \"0 attributable violations\" at a given severity.)"@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_99_DoDEnforceableOnlyViaMetaGate a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-99: a Definition of Done is enforceable only when a meta-gate makes it falsifiable"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-99"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-06 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_06> ;
    skos:definition "A DoD stated in prose is not yet a DoD — it is an aspiration until an executable meta-gate can REJECT an increment that violates it. E.g. a branch-DoD meta-gate that admits a new domain branch only if it ships a TBox+SHACL+gate+fixtures turns \"done\" from a claim into a checkable property. Prose acceptance criteria without an enforcing gate are unverified."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_X2_RepeatedChallengeStopSignal a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-X2: Repeated user challenge under stable input = stop signal, not iterate"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-X2"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal_v1_0_0 (a domain ecosystem session); Conversation.txt line 1259: 'Three rounds of guessing under a stable user spec the user repeated each time.'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When the user sends the same challenge twice without the model's reading visibly changing, the repetition itself is the signal. Continuing to guess a third time is performing helpfulness, not delivering it.

A repeated user challenge (same or near-identical wording, with reading unchanged) is a STOP CONDITION. Next action is a direct clarification ask (e.g., ask_user_input_v0), not another guess. The pattern itself is the trigger — not the count, but the repetition under stable input.

Inverse of L-50: L-50 catches the OVER-ASKING failure (asking when convention settles); L-X2 catches the OVER-ACTING failure (continuing to act after the user has signaled the reading is wrong). Both apply to the same axis (act-vs-ask) from opposite directions."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation .

kb:Lesson_L_X5_HermeticAdversarialHarness a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-X5: Adversarial harness paths must be package-relative (hermetic-from-files)"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-X5"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal_v1_0_0 (a domain ecosystem session); a domain ecosystem v1.1.0 adversarial test used 5 absolute paths + 3 unbundled fixtures; '13/13 verified' was true on author's disk only."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A 'N/N verified' claim from an adversarial or regression test is MEANINGLESS if the test cites absolute working-tree paths or unbundled fixtures — it cannot be reproduced by a fresh session reading from files alone.

Hermetic-from-files is the property that makes L-65's verification REAL across sessions, not just on the author's machine.

Operational form: any adversarial / regression test packaged with an artifact MUST (a) resolve every input path relative to its own __file__, (b) bundle every fixture it cites within the same artifact, (c) be runnable by cd <pkg> && python3 <test> with no path outside the package.

A hermeticity check (grep for absolute paths in test files) is added to CI/handoff acceptance. Detection signal: test files containing /home/, /mnt/, /Users/, or other workspace-prefix paths."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing .

kb:Lesson_L_X6_DispositionScopingDiscipline a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-X6: Disposition wording must scope precisely what is being retired"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-X6"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal_v1_0_0 (a domain ecosystem session); 'Reference templates SUPERSEDED — do NOT carry forward' read as retiring the pages when only count-metrics were being retired."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A disposition statement that retires the METRIC of an artifact must not be worded so as to retire the ARTIFACT itself. Over-broad supersession kills more than intended.

Every disposition statement must name: (a) exactly what is being retired (the metric? the role? the artifact itself?), (b) what remains intact, (c) what becomes of the residual.

A reviewer must be able to point to a SPECIFIC CLAUSE that answers 'does this also retire X?' for any plausible X.

Detection signal: phrases like 'X SUPERSEDED — do NOT carry forward' without a scoping clause specifying whether X means the X-metric, the X-role, the X-artifact, or some combination.

Distinct from L-48 (which is about NOT REOPENING closed decisions); L-X6 is about the WORDING PRECISION of disposition statements themselves."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions .

kb:Lesson_L_X7_CrossSessionOwnershipDiscipline a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-X7: Cross-session ownership — propose, do not mutate; owning session decides versioning"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-X7"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal_v1_0_0 (a domain ecosystem session); both directions modeled correctly this session — PROPOSAL_rdodi_redo_shacl_v2_fix and REFERENCE_ONLY_proposed_rdodi_fixes_NOT_A_RELEASE."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """When session A finds a defect in session B's owned artifacts, the discipline is: A produces a PROPOSAL document plus a clearly-labeled REFERENCE_ONLY copy showing the fix, but does NOT mutate B's authoritative files. B verifies independently, decides versioning, applies in its own context.

A ruling on a DECISION ('Branch B is correct') is NOT authorization to MUTATE files outside this session's ownership. The execution belongs to the session that owns the artifact.

Operational form: any cross-session finding is packaged as: (a) PROPOSAL document with measured evidence + proposed fixes + verification method, (b) optional REFERENCE_ONLY artifact showing the applied fix, EXPLICITLY labeled NOT A RELEASE, (c) explicit scope-independence statement deferring versioning to the owning session. The receiving session INDEPENDENTLY re-verifies the proposal's measured claims before applying; never accepts cross-LLM 'VERIFIED' labels (intersects BP-D47 cross-LLM-laundering clause).

Detection signal: about to edit, version-bump, or repackage a file that this session did not author. Remedy: stop — produce a proposal instead.

Distinct from BP-D13 (lineage WITHIN a session) and BP-D17 (coordinated releases WITHIN a session). L-X7 governs CROSS-SESSION ownership boundaries. The principle this session's contamination episode generated; converges with v2.0.0 operating-discipline boundary B1."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D13_DistinguishVersionLineage,
        kb:BestPractice_D17_CoordinatedReleaseDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D47_ConfidenceTierDisclosureDiscipline .

kb:Lesson_L_cand_A_ConformanceLevelInDataGraphNotForkedShapes a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-108: A reusable standard states its conformance level in the data graph, so one shape suite serves adopters of different maturity without forking"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-108"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from a registrant package registrant deposit (backlog_framework_lesson_deposit_v2_0_0.ttl), OE Pack v20.23.38 registration round, 2026-07-28."@en ;
    skos:definition "When a governed vocabulary is offered for reuse across several developments, its constraint suite faces two adopters with incompatible needs: a new adopter who cannot yet satisfy evidence-binding, and a mature one who must be prevented from skipping it. Forking the shapes per adopter duplicates the discipline and lets the copies drift; shipping only the strict suite pushes early adopters to disable validation entirely, which is worse than a weaker gate they actually run. The resolution is to make the claimed strength a first-class assertion inside the adopter's own data - a profile individual carrying an enumerated conformance level - and to gate the stricter shapes on that assertion via SPARQL constraints. Conformance then reads as 'passed the L3 suite' rather than as an unqualified adjective, an adopter cannot quietly weaken its own gate without editing a visible claim, and BP-X1's disclosure duty extends naturally: the level claimed is part of the measurement configuration, alongside shape version and imports policy."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_BP_X1_ConformanceClaimDisclosureDiscipline .

kb:Lesson_L_cand_B_ClosedEnumWideningBreaksEquivalentClass a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-107: A closed enumeration widened during generalisation forbids owl:equivalentClass alignment back to the source"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-107"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from a registrant package registrant deposit (backlog_framework_lesson_deposit_v2_0_0.ttl), OE Pack v20.23.38 registration round, 2026-07-28."@en ;
    skos:definition "Generalising a subject frequently widens a closed enumeration - a three-state lifecycle becomes five because the general case needs states the originating project never had. The instinct at alignment time is to assert owl:equivalentClass between the old enumerated class and the new one, since they plainly denote 'the same thing'. Under owl:oneOf plus owl:AllDifferent on both sides, that assertion states that three mutually distinct individuals are five mutually distinct individuals, and any consumer that merges both files gets an inconsistency that surfaces far from its cause. The same trap applies one level down: an equivalentProperty on a relation whose range is the widened enumeration forces each old value into the new closed set, so the reasoner must identify it with some new individual and will pick one arbitrarily. The discipline is to reserve OWL equivalence for terms whose extensions genuinely coincide, express widened-enumeration correspondences with SKOS matching properties (closeMatch, broadMatch) that carry no set-theoretic commitment, and record which source values split across which target values in an editorial note so the loss of information is visible rather than inferred wrongly."@en .

kb:OEPACK_BP1_ValidateWithImports a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-1: Validate with imports merged"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-1"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from redo:BP1_ValidateWithImports (oepack_lessons_rdf_v1_0_0.ttl). Semantic deduplication against D-prefixed BPs deferred. (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 1 deliverable validate_with_imports.py."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Use validate_with_imports.py (or equivalent) for any ontology that declares owl:imports. Standalone validation produces false positives for import-dependent files."@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oepack-lessons-rdf#Lesson_L14>,
        <http://example.org/oepack-lessons-rdf#Lesson_L15> ;
