kb:BestPractice_D20_VerifyDiagramContainmentMatchesTBox a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D20: Verify Diagram Containment Matches TBox (or source-of-truth structure) before rendering"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D20"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Originated 2026-05-14 as the positive-discipline counterpart to L-45 DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood. The user explicitly requested BP-D20 generation after the F1-redirect diagram-truth audit revealed how easy it is to encode false ontological claims via visual containment without raw verification."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_38_NamePatternIsHeuristic,
        kb:Lesson_L_42_VerifyBothSidesOfRelationship,
        kb:Lesson_L_45_DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood ;
    skos:definition "Before rendering any structured diagram that uses containment (nesting, grouping, or \"inside-of\" visual relationships) as a visual primitive, raw-verify each containment claim against the source-of-truth structure. Specifically: (a) for ontology diagrams, verify each entity proposed for placement inside a subject container actually has (entity rdf:type owl:Class) declared in that subject's TBox — per BP-D2 raw inspection, not from memory or namespace prefix; (b) for entities that are referenced but not declared (ghost IRIs, ghost references, ghost members), draw them OUTSIDE any container — typically as dashed-bordered free-floating elements visually distinguished from declared/owned entities; (c) the diagram caption MUST distinguish \"declared/owned by container X\" from \"referenced by container X's files/scope\" — these are different relationships with different operational consequences (e.g., class-deletion vs reference-rewrite, MAJOR vs PATCH SemVer); (d) apply symmetric verification per L-42: check both the container's declaration AND each contained entity's actual membership relation. Anti-pattern to avoid: using visual containment as a mental shortcut for \"these things are related to X\" without verifying the specific relationship being implied. Generalization: this discipline applies to any structured diagram where containment is a visual primitive — ontology subject diagrams, system architecture diagrams, organizational charts, file-system trees, dependency graphs. The principle is consistent: the diagram should not assert a stronger relationship than the underlying data supports."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D34_PermanentVisibleTopNavigationOverDropdowns a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D34: Stage 4 instructional surfaces with ≤25 sections SHOULD use permanent visible top navigation; dropdown mega-menus impose unnecessary interaction cost"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D34"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. n=1 ground truth disclosed per L-40. Pairs with L-60."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D32_InteractivityCouplingMustEncodeDomainSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_60_MegaMenuRequiresMoreThanOneClick ;
    skos:definition """Stage 4 instructional surfaces with 10-25 sections require permanent visible top navigation; dropdown mega-menus impose unnecessary interaction cost.

Source incident: COM0463 Ch.5 v2.0.0 introduced a 3-dropdown mega-menu (Sections/Key Terms/Resources). User feedback: "not usable — requires clicking to discover where I can go". v2.1.0 replaced with permanent inline pill bar of 16 section anchors, always visible.

Decision rule:
  - ≤25 sections AND each title fits in 30-char pill → permanent inline top-bar (PREFERRED)
  - 26-50 sections OR long titles → grouped permanent top-bar with sub-section sidebar
  - >50 sections → hierarchical sidebar; permanent top-bar reserved for top-level

Acceptance test: Open page on mobile; without any clicks, can the learner identify all major sections? If no → replace with permanent visible navigation.

Per L-40 honest disclosure: this BP is observationally grounded in n=1 user feedback (COM0463 Ch.5 v2.0.0 → v2.1.0). Single-incident finding. The threshold conditions are stated explicitly so the principle can be debated independently of the n=1 anecdote."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D39_SimpleNarrativeDefaultStructuredOutputOnlyOnRequest a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D39: Answer in simple narrative by default; structured output only when explicitly requested or task-required"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D39"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source section: §4.1 R-1..R-3 of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D40_NoProactiveOptionMenusOnSettledQuestions,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Default to concise prose. Multi-section deliverables, bullet hierarchies, tables, and step-by-step breakdowns are appropriate only when the user requests them or the task is explicitly structural. Pre-emptive elaboration pads responses, fragments attention, and consumes context.

Procedure:
  1. Answer the direct question in 2-5 sentences of prose by default.
  2. If multiple dimensions of detail are available, answer the direct question      first, then offer "I can detail X, Y, or Z if useful" — let the user pull more      depth.
  3. Structured output (tables, multi-section docs) is appropriate when:
     - User explicitly requests it ("give me a table of...", "list the findings",        "audit and report")
     - Deliverable is a formal artifact by nature (audit document, work-package        plan, ontology authoring spec, proof report)
     - Content is genuinely enumerative (a list of 15 BP-D7 mismatches IS a list)
  4. Discriminator: does the structure SERVE the user, or does it pad the response?      If padding, use prose.

Anti-pattern prevented: 800-word elaborated answer to a question that wanted 3 sentences; user attention fragmented across decorative substructure that adds no information."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics .

kb:BestPractice_D3_DirectJavaHermiT a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Always use direct Java HermiT, never owlready2 wrappers, for release-grade consistency claims"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D3"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "L-27 closure analysis; Parallel_Session_Assessment_v1_0_0.md §7; current-session release_check.sh implementation."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D3_DirectJavaHermiT (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """owlready2's sync_reasoner_hermit() silently masks InconsistentOntologyException under several code paths. Verified via VF22 — both the parallel-session v17.0.0 G1 closure and Phase 2/3 closures shipped with HermiT-CONSISTENT claims that direct-Java HermiT contradicts.

Operational form for any consistency claim:
  java -Xmx4g -jar /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/owlready2/hermit/HermiT.jar \\
       --ignoreUnsupportedDatatypes -k file:///path/to/merged.owl

Detect both 'is satisfiable' (CONSISTENT) and 'InconsistentOntologyException' substrings in stderr (in addition to 'Inconsistent ontology' in stdout). owlready2 reasoner-wrapper return values are not release-grade evidence.

Embedded in: release_check.sh Gate B (current session, owlready2-hardened)."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking .

kb:BestPractice_D40_NoProactiveOptionMenusOnSettledQuestions a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D40: Do not re-open settled questions with proactive option menus or filler hedges"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D40"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source section: §4.1 R-4..R-5 of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D39_SimpleNarrativeDefaultStructuredOutputOnlyOnRequest,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Once a question is settled (naming chosen, structure agreed, scope ruled on), do not re-open it with "you could also consider..." framings. Same principle for filler hedges on settled rules — phrases like "would benefit from", "is also defensible", "weakly held", "could stay X-form" are reserved for genuinely open empirical questions.

Procedure:
  1. Track which questions in the conversation have been ruled on (naming      decisions, structure decisions, scope decisions).
  2. After a ruling, do not re-litigate it via option menus or hedging language.
  3. If new information later contradicts a settled ruling, surface the      contradiction explicitly (not as a casual "actually you might want to      consider..."). The user can then re-open the question deliberately.
  4. Hedges are reserved for genuinely open empirical questions (e.g., "is this      metric meaningful?", "does this scale?") — not for re-opening binding      decisions.

Anti-pattern prevented: False-dichotomy drift — re-presenting a closed question as if it were still open, eroding the user's prior decisions. Wastes the user's time and undermines confidence in past rulings."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D44_OQuaREThresholdProvenance a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D44: OQuaREThresholdProvenance"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D44"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T20:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.3.0 Phase F4. Codified from F2 population of 40 ThresholdRow individuals per Duque-Ramos 2016 Table 4 (8 metrics × 5 score bands)."@en ;
    skos:definition "BP-D44: OQuaRE threshold provenance — every measurement:ThresholdRow individual MUST cite the specific publication, table number, and metric for its threshold boundary values. Generic citations to 'OQuaRE' alone are insufficient: cite e.g. 'Duque-Ramos 2016 J. Biomed. Semantics 7:63 Table 4'. Threshold values MUST be reproducible from the cited source; if a value cannot be exactly traced back, it MUST NOT be added to the ABox. Detection: ThresholdRow individuals whose dcterms:source lacks table or section reference. Mitigation: BP-D41 fresh verification of every threshold table cell before population; do not interpolate between published bands."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources .

kb:BestPractice_D51_OngoingEnhancementLineageExemption a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D51: kb:isOngoingEnhancementLineage exempts a package's TBox/ABox pairing check only, never its shape-coverage check, and only when real (non-skeleton) shapes already exist"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D51"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v20.38.0 — artifact-capability shipped a TBox with no paired ABox and no shape coverage, correctly flagged by oe-method's completeness gate. Investigation found the gap genuine (the level taxonomy for ArtifactCapabilityLevel/ArtifactMaturityLevel is undecided, L-110) but not silent-negligence: the shape gap was fixable honestly (BP-D30 lightweight shapes, real coverage) while the ABox gap was not (populating it now would fabricate a taxonomy on zero producer evidence). A completeness gate with only a binary pass/fail for structural completeness cannot distinguish a genuinely deferred, tracked gap from a forgotten one — the same asymmetry L-71's population check already solved for zero-population classes (a documented baseline + an explicit per-class Abstract marker) had never been extended to pairing or shape coverage." ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D27_SkeletonShapeDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D30_SemanticShapeUpgrade,
        kb:Lesson_L_71_FrameworkClassExistsZeroPopulation ;
    skos:definition "kb:isOngoingEnhancementLineage true on an owl:Ontology declaration marks that package's ABox as deliberately, visibly incomplete — never silently missing. It is NOT a general completeness bypass: (1) it exempts ABox/TBox pairing only; a package's shape coverage must be real (BP-D30-style, not the retired BP-D27 skeleton mechanism) before the marker may be set — the marker cannot substitute for shape authoring that is actually possible now. (2) the deferred design question must be stated in the ontology's own rdfs:comment, not merely asserted — a marker with no stated reason is indistinguishable from an excuse. (3) a completeness gate honoring this marker MUST still fail when the marker is absent (proven by a negative-control run, per L-95) — the exemption is visible and auditable, never a silent pass." ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_110_NoNewStructureOnSingleProducerEvidence,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating .

kb:BestPractice_D52_SearchBeforeCreatingArtifact a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D52: Before creating a new file or directory for a specific need, name and check the closest existing artifact for extension first"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D52"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Extended 2026-09-10: the owner's own follow-up question, whether the rule should push harder toward reusing existing files. Checked first rather than strengthened by default: no counter-lesson against over-consolidation existed yet, but BP-D6 (file naming must represent the file's own content, verified at creation) already constrains how far reuse can go without being asked to. Pushing BP-D52 further toward 'prefer an existing file even for distinct content' would put it in direct tension with BP-D6 and with BP-D7's one-clean-SemVer-lineage-per-artifact principle -- the fix is a sharper distinctness test, not a stronger push toward reuse.",
        "The owner's own direct observation, 2026-09-10, generalising L-119: the same additive-only-growth pattern that produced 27 separate fixture files had occurred before with patch files, and would recur again for a different reason if left as a narrative lesson with no operational check attached." ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming,
        kb:Lesson_L_119_ConsolidateBeforeCreating ;
    skos:definition """Operational form of L-119, general beyond the fixture/deposit case that lesson was first written from. Before creating a new file or directory to address a specific, immediate need (a fixture, a patch file, a one-off script, a deposit, a report, any per-instance artifact), do two things in order: (1) name the closest existing artifact that serves an adjacent purpose -- search for it, do not assume none exists; (2) state explicitly why extending it is not the right choice, if creating something new anyway. A new artifact is still the right call when the need is genuinely, nameably distinct (a different gate, a different subject, a different audience) -- this is not a rule against creating anything new. It is a rule against creating as the unexamined default: the check itself, not its outcome, is what this practice requires every time.

Distinctness test (extension, 2026-09-10): a need is genuinely distinct, and gets its own artifact, when the closest existing file's own name, under BP-D6, would have to either misdescribe what it now contains or describe two separate things at once if the new content were folded into it. If the existing file's name still honestly and singularly describes the combined content, extend it. This closes the loophole in either direction -- rationalizing a new file because writing inside an existing one takes more reading first, or rationalizing reuse by cramming unrelated content under a name that no longer fits.""" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics .

kb:BestPractice_D5_ValidateWithImportsMerged a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Always validate with owl:imports merged, never standalone"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D5"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D5_ValidateWithImportsMerged (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 1 I-09 closure (validate_with_imports.py); Phase 1 I-05 closure (a reliability ontology C7+ dynamic-SPARQL pattern)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """SHACL validation against an artifact in isolation produces false-positive violations on every imported class (TypeDeclarationShape and similar fire on classes the standalone graph cannot resolve). Always merge owl:imports before validating, using validate_with_imports.py (Phase 1 BP-1 helper) or equivalent.

The 4-tier IRI resolution pattern in validate_with_imports.py:
  1. Caller-provided overrides (highest priority)
  2. Caller-provided hints map
  3. DEFAULT_OE_PACK_IRI_HINTS (in-module catalog)
  4. Tail-substring search across uploads/handoff dirs

Set strip_imports_data=False (default) to preserve owl:imports triples in the merged graph, since some shapes (e.g. a reliability ontology C7+ dynamic SPARQL) read owl:imports at validation time."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:OEPACK_BP1_ValidateWithImports .

kb:BestPractice_D8_Sha256Manifest a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Every release bundle ships a MANIFEST_SHA256.txt"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D8"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D8_Sha256Manifest (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 1 I-07 release_check.sh Gate 1 design; current-session bundle_zip workflow."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Every configuration:ProjectArchive zip MUST include a MANIFEST_SHA256.txt enumerating SHA-256 hashes of all bundled files. This enables release_check.sh Gate A to verify integrity post-download in a single command:

  cd extracted-bundle && sha256sum -c MANIFEST_SHA256.txt

Generation form (used in this session for oepack-parallel-integration-v1_0_1.zip):
  sha256sum file1 file2 ... > MANIFEST_SHA256.txt
  zip -j bundle.zip file1 file2 ... MANIFEST_SHA256.txt

Verified end-to-end in current session: oepack-parallel-integration-v1_0_1.zip ships 6 deliverable files + MANIFEST_SHA256.txt (7 entries total). All hashes verified clean post-build."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "owlready2 sync_reasoner_hermit silently masks InconsistentOntologyException"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-27"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from rev:LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking (catalogue). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Parallel session OE Pack v17.0.0 G1 diagnostic record (g1_diagnostic_v17_0_0_v1_0_0.ttl §4 ToolingDefect_OwlReadyHermitMasking). Verified independently via Parallel_Session_Assessment_v1_0_0.md §7."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Discovered by parallel-session OE Pack v17.0.0 G1 closure investigation. The owlready2 Python wrapper for HermiT (used via World().get_ontology(...).load() + sync_reasoner_hermit(infer_property_values=False, debug=0) inside `with onto:` blocks) silently swallows HermiT's InconsistentOntologyException and returns success, while direct java -jar HermiT.jar -k correctly reports inconsistency.

This bug caused multiple OE Pack releases (v15.6.0 through v16.2.0 per the parallel-session report) to ship with G1=PARTIAL based on incomplete reasoner output. Any sprint closure that called sync_reasoner_hermit and reported 'consistency check passed' should be revisited.

Discipline (already adopted in current-session release_check.sh Gate B as of 2026-05-08):
  1. Always use direct Java HermiT for G1 / OWL 2 DL gates: `java -Xmx4g -jar HermiT.jar --ignoreUnsupportedDatatypes -k <file:///path.owl>`
  2. Detect 'InconsistentOntologyException' substring in stderr in addition to 'Inconsistent ontology' in stdout
  3. Never rely on owlready2 reasoner-wrapper return values for release-gate decisions
  4. File upstream owlready2 bug report (deferred action item)"""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_101_AggregateReadinessGateIsItsOwnAssertion a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-101: an aggregate release-readiness gate is itself the assertion; asserting parts separately is not asserting the whole"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-101"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-08 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_08> ;
    skos:definition "A 1.0/milestone claim is a claim of completeness ACROSS many independently-verified parts (control gates, branch DoDs, register status, SHACL). Asserting each part separately does NOT assert the whole — a dedicated aggregate gate that returns READY only when every enumerated part currently passes is the artifact that carries the completeness claim. Without it, \"all parts were checked at some point\" silently substitutes for \"all parts pass now\"."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_102_OverlappingBranchPromotionNeedsOrthogonalityProof a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-102: promoting a branch that overlaps an existing gate requires an orthogonality proof, not just a discrimination proof"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-102"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-09 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_09> ;
    skos:definition "When a reference branch is promoted from Partial (already touched by an existing control gate) to Built with its own new gate, proving the new gate merely discriminates (L-95: positive PASS, negative FAIL) is insufficient — it may be re-testing what the existing gate already covers. Promotion additionally requires proving the new gate is ORTHOGONAL: it must fail a case the pre-existing gate passes, demonstrating it adds coverage rather than duplicating it."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating .

kb:Lesson_L_103_SameIriHigherVersionMayBeDivergentScope a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-103: a higher version on a shared ontology IRI may be a divergent scope, not a supersession"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-103"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from an algebraic framework registrant deposit lesson_deposit_same_iri_divergent_scopes_v1_0_0.ttl (2026-07-02), OE Pack v20.23.30; screened additive vs L-82/L-75." ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_103_SameIriHigherVersionMayBeDivergentScope_PROPOSED,
        kb:Lesson_L_82_ForeignNamespaceReDeclarationIsAntiPattern ;
    skos:definition "When one ontology IRI is carried by two files at different versions, version arithmetic alone does not establish supersession: the higher-version file may be a DIVERGENT SCOPE that dropped the vocabulary the lower version's consumers bind to. Before retiring the lower version, MEASURE consumer-binding coverage: enumerate the terms every consumer (code, shapes, queries, ABoxes) references in that namespace and compute presence in each candidate file. If the higher version covers the consumers, it supersedes (retire the lower per the one-current-file rule). If it does not (e.g. an algebraic framework 2026-07-02: 101/101 consumer terms in v2_10_0, 15/101 in v3_2_6), then the situation is a same-IRI scope divergence: carry BOTH files live in clearly separated scopes, never co-load them into one reasoning/validation graph, record a machine-readable divergence record naming the measured coverage, and schedule a re-IRI of one scope as the structural fix. Complements L-87 (which handles the opposite case: a supplied lower/intermediate file already absorbed forward) and B4/BP-D2 (which detect the collision but do not decide the disposition)."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_75_OverlapVsDifferenceCannotBeAssumed,
        kb:Lesson_L_87_SuppliedMissingFileMayBeSupersededIntermediate .

kb:Lesson_L_104_ReconciliationAsGate a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-104: a verification mechanism protects nothing unless it is run as a mandatory gate; status is evidence-derived"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-104"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Emerged 2026-07-13 (a product project): a SHA-256 Verification Ledger existed for the backlog but was never re-run after edits, so its two most important parts silently went stale while the document was trusted."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_91_StatusProseMustTrackStatusFields ;
    skos:definition "A verification mechanism (SHA ledger, SHACL suite, reconciliation tool) provides zero protection unless executed as a mandatory gate. The backlog-to-code reconciliation (SHACL + evidence bridge + reasoner) runs at session start and before every release; a non-clean exit blocks the release. Completion status must be evidence-derived (done implies at least one bridge-verified evidence), never asserted prose; this extends L-91 from 'prose drifts' to 'status is a derived, gated fact'."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-07-13T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_119_ConsolidateBeforeCreating a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-119: Before creating a new fixture or deposit, check whether an existing one can be extended - additive-only growth compounds silently"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-119"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's own direct observation, 2026-09-10: repeated fixture creation, one per new gate, had produced 27 fixture files across oe-pack and oe-method with no consolidation step ever considered. Verified directly before acting: every custom gate this session built (release_check's manifest check, oe-method's lineage/completeness/clean gates) had spawned its own separate fixture directory, and three of those (lineage, completeness, clean, in oe-method) were consolidated into one tree in the same session that registered this lesson, each gate re-run against the consolidated version and confirmed to still genuinely fail for its intended reason before the originals were removed.",
        "Extended 2026-09-10: the owner's own direct observation that this is a recurring, general pattern, not a fixture-specific one - the same shape had occurred before with patch files, and would recur again for a different reason if the lesson stayed scoped to fixtures/deposits by name. The specific prior incident was not independently locatable in the current tree at extension time (most likely already resolved by the time it was checked, or outside what this session can directly search) - the pattern is trusted from the owner's own account, not independently re-verified beyond the fixture case this lesson already confirmed directly."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Extended 2026-09-10 to generalise the pattern beyond fixtures/deposits by name, per the owner's own direct observation that it had already recurred once before (patch files) and would recur again in a future, differently-named form if left narrowly scoped. The original L-119 text from the fixture-consolidation session is preserved in full above; the extension is additive, not a rewrite (matching L-47's own precedent for extending a lesson without erasing what it originally said). See BP-D52 for the operational check this generalisation motivated."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """A gate needing its own proof that it can fail (B3/L-95) does not require its own fixture directory - only its own proof, which a shared tree can carry alongside proofs for other gates. Before adding a new negative fixture, a new registrant deposit, or any similar per-instance artifact, check whether an existing one already covers adjacent ground and can be extended with one more failure condition, rather than defaulting to a new directory every time. This is not a blanket rule against ever creating something new - a genuinely distinct concern still gets its own artifact - but creation should be the second choice, checked against consolidation first, not the automatic default. Verify the consolidated version before removing what it replaces: run every gate it is meant to prove against the new artifact directly, not assumed from the old one's prior success.

General form (extension, 2026-09-10): the fixture/deposit case above is one instance of a wider pattern - whenever a new need appears (a fixture, a patch file, a one-off script, a deposit, any per-instance artifact), the default response is to open a new file or directory, and additive-only growth compounds silently because no step ever asks whether an existing artifact already covers adjacent ground. The check generalises beyond the fixture case: before creating anything new to address a specific need, name what already exists that is close in purpose, and choose extension over creation unless the new need is genuinely, nameably distinct from what exists - not merely because a fresh file is easier to reason about in isolation than an existing one is to open and read first."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-10T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating .

kb:Lesson_L_125_CitedRuleMustResolveAndShipItsEnforcement a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-125: A rule cited by identifier must resolve to that rule in the governed store, and the enforcement it names must ship with it"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-125"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-05 while checking the owner's question about the three-file structure. A shipped vocabulary cited the structure rule as BP-D18, which in the knowledge base is a different rule (language-tagged literals against xsd:string ranges); the checks it named (bindings B7 and B8 of an external PROTOCOL-BINDING bundle) are in no repository and no gate implements them. The rule drifted for the reason the owner asked about: nothing in the repositories carried it."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D53_ThreeFileCanonicalStructure,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition """A rule that a vocabulary, a document or a prompt cites by identifier is only as good as two facts: the identifier resolves, in the governed store, to a rule whose label says what the citation says it does; and the check the rule names exists in a place the repositories ship. A rule whose text lives in a comment, whose identifier points elsewhere, and whose enforcement lives in an external bundle is not a rule that holds: it is a memory of one, and it lasts until the people who remember it stop.

Test, before citing or relying on a rule: (a) resolve the identifier in the latest knowledge base and compare its label with the claim; (b) find the script or CI step that enforces it and run it on a known-bad input (L-95); (c) if either fails, record the rule in the knowledge base and ship its enforcement before depending on it. Corrections to a wrong citation are appended as a note in the knowledge base, not made by rewriting the historical text (the discipline's standing record rules)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-05T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D18_LangTagOnXsdStringInconsistency .

kb:Lesson_L_127_ClosedShapeMustKnowTheNewPredicate a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-127: A closed shape must be told about a new predicate before the predicate is added to the data"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-127"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-06 in the OEE governance session while adding module membership (dcterms:isPartOf) to every named subject of this pack. The structural proof (only membership triples added) passed on all 48 files, the module-library and membership tests passed, and the pack's release check still reported 13 violations: three closed shapes of the measurement suite (sh:closed true) reject any predicate they do not list. The release check compared per suite against the baseline and named the suite at once; the three ignored-property lists were extended with dcterms:isPartOf and the finding set returned to the baseline."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A change that is purely additive in the data is not purely additive in the validation: a shape with sh:closed true turns every new predicate on its targets into a violation. A proof that the data only gained triples says nothing about the shapes that forbid gaining them. Before adding a predicate to many subjects, list the closed shapes (grep sh:closed true across every suite, in every package that validates the data), extend their ignored properties in the same release, and compare the finding set per suite with the baseline, not only the total. Detection signal: violations whose constraint component is the closed-shape component and whose path is the new predicate."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_141_BeforeAFileIsRenamedFindEveryReaderOfTheOldName a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-141: Before a file is renamed or repurposed, find every reader of its old name, including the readers that only report"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-141"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07, twice in one day, after the central structure-debt register changed role. The file name STRUCTURE_DEBT_REGISTER_v* stopped meaning the ceiling baseline and became a package's own two-line register; the publisher and the checker were moved, and two readers of the old name were not. The CI gate that checks every push still took the highest file of that name, so every other package's debt read as new and main was red until a workflow-only commit repaired it (run 37587879292). The session-start ceremony did the same in its sixth stage and ended FAIL for every session; it was found only when the next ceremony ran, because that stage informs and does not refuse. The cause was the same both times: the readers were found by remembering who used the file, not by searching for the name. One search of the whole tree for the name stem, made after the release was built and before it was published, lists them all; it was made, for the ceremony, only afterwards."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_139_ASharedRecordOnlyOnePartyMayEditMakesItsOwnRuleUnobeyable ;
    skos:definition "When a file changes role, name or location, the readers of its old name are the release's real surface, and memory is not a way to list them. Search the whole tree for the name's stem, across scripts, workflows, tests, documents and the tools that only report (a stage that prints a verdict and does not refuse is a reader, and the one most likely to be left behind because nothing fails loudly). Decide for each hit whether it should follow the change, keep the old meaning, or be retired, and keep a one-release fallback to the old name where an older checkout may still be in use. Detection signal: a verdict that was green yesterday and reads FAIL or NEW today for a package nobody touched, immediately after a rename or a change of what a file holds. A reader found by its failure, not by the search, is this lesson's evidence that the search was skipped."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_142_AnActionTakenOnceAtTheEventTurnsARefusalIntoAPermanentGap a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-142: An action taken once, at the event, with no later pass over what it missed, turns one refusal into a permanent gap"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-142"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07. The release-label workflow labelled each package's current version once, at push time. Of the 102 versions released since it was adopted, 99 are labelled and 3 never were: each was refused once, because a later commit had changed a workflow file, and nothing ever tried again. The owner's words on the day were that the GitHub problems never finish: every report ended in a request for a credential and a manual re-run, and the next refusal started the report over. What was missing was not a credential, which only changes what a retry can achieve, but the retry. The workflow now also runs daily and reconciles every version released since its adoption, and a weekly check reads the credential's own expiry date and says in plain words what to do. Run against the real history, the reconcile pass found exactly the three missing labels, and on GitHub it attempted them, was refused, counted them as stranded and left the run green."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_139_ASharedRecordOnlyOnePartyMayEditMakesItsOwnRuleUnobeyable,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "When an automated action is triggered by an event and can be refused or can fail for a reason that may pass, pair it with a reconcile pass that compares the intended end state with the actual one and retries the difference on a schedule. The event-time action has one chance; the reconcile pass has as many as it takes, and it turns the size of the gap into a measured number instead of a story told again at each failure. Give the pass three properties: bounded (limited to a window it can finish, here the releases since adoption), idempotent (a second run changes nothing), and not failing for items known to be stranded, because a check that can only be red stops being read, while the count of stranded items stays visible in every run. Where the remedy depends on a credential that expires, monitor its expiry date itself, so the credential's end is announced instead of discovered by the next failure. Detection signal: the same manual backfill requested a second time, or a report whose proposed fix is to re-run the job by hand."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_143_AUnionOfTwoFilesKeepsBothCopiesOfAnAnonymousNode a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-143: A union of two files does not drop the copies of an anonymous node, so a merge keeps both and a proof of no loss cannot see it"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-143"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07, after the three-file consolidation was published and its post-mortem found nothing missing. A count of identical anonymous constraints under one subject and predicate found 119 in the OE pack and 119 in the OE method, and 38 more in a registrant's own package. In the pack they sit in two files: 117 identical property constraints under 47 shapes in the shapes file and 2 identical restrictions under 2 classes in the T-Box. Each came from two source files that both carried the same constraint: the merge took the union of the two graphs, and the union of two graphs drops a repeated named triple but never a repeated anonymous one, because each copy is a different blank node. The proof of no loss compared subjects and triples and was satisfied, since nothing was lost; the check that a merge also adds nothing it should not was never written. After the repair the repaired files differ from the published ones only by the removed copies and the version resources of the touched modules, and the structure check now fails a package that opted in and carries a copy more than it recorded (rule D1)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_139_ASharedRecordOnlyOnePartyMayEditMakesItsOwnRuleUnobeyable,
        kb:Lesson_L_142_AnActionTakenOnceAtTheEventTurnsARefusalIntoAPermanentGap,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "When files are merged by taking the union of their graphs, a constraint or restriction written as an anonymous node in both sources survives twice, because the union identifies named resources and not the anonymous ones. Prove a merge in both directions: nothing from the sources is missing, and nothing is present twice. Compare anonymous content by its structure (its predicates and values, recursively), never by node identity, and count identical copies under one subject and one predicate (list cells excluded, since a list may repeat a value). Remove the copies in the text of the file, where they are single blocks, not by rebuilding the graph, which would rewrite every other line. Give the guard a recorded count, so that a package that merges again cannot grow it unseen, and show the guard failing on a merge that keeps a copy. Detection signal: a count of identical anonymous nodes above zero in a package whose source files were merged, or a shape or class whose validation message appears twice for one fault."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_146_ARefusalThatComesTwiceUnchangedMeansTheCauseIsNotYetKnown a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-146: A refusal that comes twice unchanged means the cause is not yet known, so the third run waits for a written cause"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-146"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07 in the governing session. The manifest self-check refused for a stowaway after a dry-run that had passed: the dry-run itself had left bytecode in the stage that holds the tools being published, and the first correction treated the symptom (remove the file) and not the cause (what writes it). Earlier the same day a named-deletion list was repaired by trial until the real set of deletions was computed from the guard's own output."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck ;
    skos:definition "When a gate refuses and the next attempt, after a change, is refused for the same reason, the change did not address the cause. Stop re-running. Write the cause in one sentence from what the gate measured (not from the wording of its message), change that, and only then run again. A release command records refusals per stage and stops the third attempt until a cause is given. Detection signal: the same refusal line twice in a row; a fix chosen from the message's text rather than from what the gate measured."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_147_AStepDoneByHandTwiceIsAToolThatDoesNotExistYet a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-147: A step done by hand twice is a tool that does not exist yet: it is built and tested before the third time"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-147"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07. Every release of the derived method package, and the manifest of every release, was produced by a one-off script kept in a session's scratch folder, written again for each release; the repository had a manifest verifier and no manifest writer. Such a script cannot be read, tested or reused by another session, and each copy failed in its own way (a stowaway, an old manifest still listed, a hash of the wrong file)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck ;
    skos:definition "The second time the same sequence of steps is done by hand, it becomes a tool with a test, shipped in repo-tooling, before the third. A tool that lives only in one session's scratch folder is not a tool. The test shows the tool failing on bad input (L-95). Detection signal: a script in a scratch directory that is run on every release; two sessions describing the same manual sequence in their handovers."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating .

kb:Lesson_L_148_TheCostOfARefusalGrowsWithHowLateItComes a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-148: The cost of a refusal grows with how late it comes, so the dry-run runs as soon as the stage exists"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-148"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07. A hole in the publisher's named-deletion guard (a same-named file elsewhere hid a deletion) was found only at the end of a long stage build, at the dry-run; a bytecode stowaway was found at the real publish after a dry-run had already passed; a class-table change was found to be invisible to the publisher's own structure step only when the publish was tried."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck ;
    skos:definition "Run the release's dry-run as soon as the stage exists, on the smallest change, and again after each cluster of changes, never once at the end. A check run only at the end makes every defect cost a rebuild of everything built since. Detection signal: a stage built over many steps with no dry-run yet; a first dry-run that returns more than one distinct refusal."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_149_AWaitRecordedWithoutLookingIsAManufacturedWait a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-149: An item recorded as waiting for a ruling or another session must say what was searched; a wait nobody checked is manufactured"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-149"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07. A nested lineage unit in one package was recorded in the debt aggregate as pending-ruling and carried in the plan as awaiting the owner. The owner had ruled on 2026-10-06 (lineage is independent of the domain ontology and meets the three-file rule on its own), and OE had accepted the package's mark of the unit the same day; the accepted handovers held both. Only the class-table row was missing. It was closed on a search of the accepted handovers, with no question to the owner."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck ;
    skos:definition "Before recording an item as waiting for a ruling, an answer from another session or a decision, search the accepted handovers, the knowledge base and the log of rulings for one that settles it, and write in the line what was searched. A wait nobody has checked stalls the item and puts a decision on the owner that the record already holds (B2). Detection signal: a pending line whose question is answered by a document already in the inbox."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_150_ACheckThatTakesMinutesRunsOncePerSetOfBytes a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-150: A check that takes minutes runs once per set of bytes, because a second run on the same bytes learns nothing the first did not"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-150"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07 from a parallel session's report after discipline v2.16.1: its package's own release gate re-validates the whole data and rules files cold, about 25 minutes per release, and the publisher ran that gate in the dry-run and again in the real publish on the same bytes; inside the gate the same validation was also repeated in separate steps. The run-length rules R1 to R8 removed the hand-work around the checks and could not shorten this, because the time was in the check itself."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck ;
    skos:definition "A deterministic check is run once for each set of bytes it reads. Within a release the publisher records a receipt after the package's gates pass, bound to the exact bytes of the stage, the upstream tree the gate reads, the head commit of the target repository, the Python version and an age limit, and the real publish reuses the dry-run's pass; any change runs the gates in full, and OE_FRESH_GATES=1 forces a full run. Inside a package's own gate, a validation shared by several steps is run once and its result shared, and a validation whose inputs are byte-identical to a passing run is not run again (oe_gate_cache: the key is the command, the bytes of every named file, the named texts and the Python version; only passes are stored). The soundness rule: everything the step reads is named, the checker script itself included, and a step that reads what it does not name (the network, the clock) is not cached. A gate that legitimately takes minutes is not a defect to hide: its duration is stated up front and its time limit is set for it, not removed. Detection signal: the same long check appears twice in one release's output; a gate run twice on identical bytes; a validation step that reads the same data and rules files as the step before it."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_36_PyshaclSeverityMustBeShapeLevel a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-36: pyshacl ignores sh:severity declared on sh:sparql blank nodes — severity must be declared at the NodeShape level"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-36"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Extended 2026-09-28 from a real, verified finding in a registrant package (candidate L-cand-H, deposit backlog_framework_lesson_deposit_v2_5_0.ttl, L-84 pipeline): a Warning-level shape, StageOutputOwesAdvisoryShape, held two SPARQL constraints of different nature -- a waived REQUIRED obligation (a debt) and an unmet ADVISORY obligation (a state the framework calls optional). A severity audit promoted the shape to Violation for the first constraint's reason, which made the optional obligation a Violation for every adopter; the adopter (a product project) found it. Fixed in a registrant package (shapes v1.143.0) by splitting the shape into StageOutputOwesAdvisoryShape and AdvisoryObligationUnmetShape; the audit record (SEVERITY_AUDIT_2026_09_09_v1_2_0.md) was amended, not rewritten. Verified directly by the OEE session: two constraints in the shape at commit 02e1c81, two shapes in v1.143.0, the amendment present."@en,
        "Surfaced 2026-05-15 in v17.29.0 reliability C7+ standalone allowlist remediation. After expanding STRICT + ADVISORY allowlists with redo#, redo-c7-split#, oe-prov#, oe-measurement# namespaces, 12 findings were still classified as Severity: sh:Violation despite shape name and message both saying \"ADVISORY\" and the sh:sparql bnode carrying sh:severity sh:Warning. Investigation revealed pyshacl scope rules."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Extension (2026-09-28), the consequence of this lesson for severity audits: because severity is declared at the NodeShape level, every constraint in a shape is graded together. Before grading or re-grading a shape, check that every constraint in it meets the grade's stated reason; if one does not, split the shape so each severity line describes exactly what it grades. The original definition above is preserved unchanged; this extension is additive (L-47's precedent)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode: a SHACL NodeShape uses sh:sparql to declare a SPARQL-based constraint and attaches sh:severity to the sh:sparql blank node:

  reliability:MyShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:sparql [
      sh:select "..." ;
      sh:severity sh:Warning  # ← pyshacl IGNORES this
    ] .

pyshacl defaults to sh:Violation for any SPARQL-constraint finding regardless of the severity declared on the sh:sparql bnode. The shape-level sh:severity declaration is what pyshacl actually honors.

Correct form:

  reliability:MyShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:severity sh:Warning ;   # ← pyshacl reads THIS
    sh:sparql [
      sh:select "..."
    ] .

Discovered in v17.29.0 reliability C7+ allowlist remediation: the advisory shape was correctly authored with sh:severity sh:Warning on the sh:sparql bnode, but ALL 12 findings still reported as Severity: sh:Violation. Moving sh:severity to the shape-level reduced reliability standalone from 14V/76W to 0V/88W.

This is a known pyshacl behavior — not a strict SHACL spec violation (the spec allows severity on both shape and constraint, but pyshacl prioritizes shape-level).

Recommendation (per BP-D4 update): when designing SPARQL-based NodeShapes with sh:Warning severity, ALWAYS place sh:severity on the shape itself. Adding to the sh:sparql bnode is documentation but does not control pyshacl behavior."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification,
        kb:Lesson_L_84_RecordLessonsAsTheyEmerge .

kb:Lesson_L_37_DRYAttributionCompleteness a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-37: DRY-Attribution Completeness"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-37"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced at step 7 (AE-Bridge retirement) when raw inspection of ae_bridge_v1_0_1.ttl revealed Performance v1.0.0 had incomplete attribution on hasTheoreticalComplexity (credited 1 of 2 source bridge properties). Performance v1.0.1 PATCH corrected."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When DRY-collapsing N source properties or classes into one canonical, the canonical's dcterms:source MUST enumerate all N sources, not just one. Title-level inspection masks attribution gaps that label-level reading misses. Verify by comparing full source/comment/definition content of all source instances before claiming a DRY collapse is complete."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_44_VocabularySelfAuditBlindspot a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-44: Vocabulary Self-Audit Blindspot — Measure The Measuring Vocabulary"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-44"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-14 when the user asked 'are findings on risk management in the enhancement plan?' The plan used scattered risk vocabulary (reliability:Risk, configuration:RiskItem, quality:SeverityLevel, etc.) to evaluate enhancements but never audited the vocabulary itself. Direct inspection per BP-D2 revealed: 107 risk-related terms scattered across 5 subjects with 4 duplications (SeverityLevel × 3, hasSeverity × 3, MitigationStrategy × 2, FaultTolerance × 2); FMEA missing FailureCause / Severity / Occurrence / Detection / CurrentControls / RecommendedAction / hasFailureEffect / hasFailureCause / hasMitigation; RiskItem missing riskImpact / riskLikelihood / riskCategory / riskOwner / riskStatus / residualRisk. Led to risk subject v1.0.0 creation (3a30a6d4…554f) consolidating ~47 terms and gap-filling completeness. The blindspot was: the plan answered 'how do we prioritize using risk vocabulary?' but missed 'is the risk vocabulary itself fit for purpose?'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When using a vocabulary V to evaluate something else (e.g., using risk vocabulary to enumerate enhancement priorities), also evaluate V's own completeness against its domain standards. Self-measurement metrics measure what they measure — structural shape, annotation coverage, inheritance depth — but do NOT surface gaps in the vocabularies used to express the measurement. Concretely: an enhancement plan using FMEA + RiskItem to prioritize work won't detect that FMEA itself is missing 8 of 16 standard ISO 60812 elements, or that RiskItem is missing 5 of 13 standard project-risk elements. The discipline: before using vocabulary V for evaluation, run a completeness check of V against its claimed domain standard. Extends L-42 (verify both endpoints): here the 'other endpoint' is the domain-standard reference frame, not another class."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_42_VerifyBothSidesOfRelationship .

kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-47: Cross-subject subClassOf requires semantic verification"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-47"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Track D Mitigation Cleanup discussion (2026-05-14). Captured from: (1) the A2 lexical-error correction (reliability:FailureType subClassOf risk:FailureType — removed because same local name covered different lineages: IEEE 1633 vs IEC 60812 FMEA); (2) the rejected proposal to chain reliability:FailureMitigation subClassOf risk:RiskMitigation, which would have confused methodological-applicability with type-subsumption. Extended in OE v1.6.2 with the constraint-compatibility clause empirically derived from Sprint F1's 9-Score-individual case. dcterms:source added in Sprint F6 (2026-05-15) per L-47v2 path (a) — declaration of kb:Lesson activated the existing LessonRequiresSource shape, and L-47 (originally typed kb:Lesson) lacked dcterms:source."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Extended in OE v1.6.2 (2026-05-14) with the constraint-compatibility clause empirically derived from Sprint F1's quality:Score → measurement:Score redirect case. The original L-47 text from OE v1.6.0 is preserved in full; the extension is additive."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Cross-subject subClassOf links require semantic verification of the two classes' definitions, not lexical matching of their local names. Two classes with the same local name in different subjects may be independent concepts; subsumption claims need definitional alignment.

Test: does the methodological-applicability relationship ("discipline X applies to concept Y") get confused with type-subsumption ("Y is-a X-concept")? If yes, the relationship is methodological, not subsumption.

Examples encountered in this ecosystem:
- reliability:FailureType subClassOf risk:FailureType (A2, 2026-05-14): asserted because both classes had the same local name. Semantic check (L-47, this session): reliability:FailureType is IEEE 1633 reliability classification (transient/intermittent/permanent); risk:FailureType is IEC 60812 FMEA context. Same local name, different lineages. Link removed in reliability v7.0.0.
- Proposed reliability:FailureMitigation subClassOf risk:RiskMitigation: considered for A2 pattern consistency. Semantic check (L-47): "risk management applies to failures" is a methodological relationship, NOT a type-subsumption claim. The same reasoning would put quality, scope, security under risk: — risk imperialism failure mode. Modeled as siblings under core:Mitigation instead.

Captured 2026-05-14 during Track D Mitigation Cleanup discussion.

CONSTRAINT-COMPATIBILITY EXTENSION (added in OE v1.6.2, 2026-05-14 during Sprint F1):

Semantic verification also requires constraint compatibility. The target class's SHACL shapes may require properties (e.g. skos:definition, dcterms:source, qc:atTimestamp) that the source class did not. A redirect that passes structural checks (target declared, no disjointness, definitions read as compatible) can still produce SHACL violations at gate time when redirected ABox individuals lack the target's required properties.

Mitigation paths in order of preference:
(a) Supply the missing data on redirected individuals — promotes existing data into the required predicate or authors new content. Preserves the target's constraint discipline.
(b) Relax the target constraint to match the source — looser typing; defeats the purpose of the constraint and weakens the canonical home's quality bar.
(c) Reconsider the redirect — the target may not be the right canonical home if its constraints don't match what consumers actually populate.

Empirical case 2026-05-14: Sprint F1 redirected quality:Score → measurement:Score (12 references). Nine ABox individuals (Score_OE_v15_4_*, Score_oee_v3_0_0_*) failed oe-measurement's SHACL shape requiring skos:definition with minCount=1. quality:Score had no such constraint. Mitigated via path (a): promoted each individual's existing rdfs:comment content into skos:definition, since the descriptive data was already present in the ABox, just in the wrong predicate.

Operational implication: gate after every redirect, not only at end-of-sprint. The "verify both endpoints declared" check from L-42 is necessary but not sufficient; "verify constraint endpoints compatible" extends it. When violations appear, prefer path (a) over (b)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_42_VerifyBothSidesOfRelationship .

kb:Lesson_L_58_PipelineMetricsDoNotMeasureSourceFidelity a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-58: Pipeline scoring metrics (intrinsic artefact properties) do not measure primary-source content fidelity"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-58"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Originally proposed as L-57; renumbered to L-58 per v17.43.0 collision."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D31_PrimarySourceConceptCoverageGate,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_44_VocabularySelfAuditBlindspot ;
    skos:definition """Standard Stage 4 pipeline metrics (interactive element count, JS LOC, SHACL conformance triple count, citation density, voice-gate pass rate, ARIA-tab coverage) are SOUND for detecting Stage 4 quality defects (rendering-only pages, missing keyboard nav, mechanical-conformance failures).

They are BLIND to whether the artefact accurately represents its primary source. A pipeline can: have 150 interactive elements demonstrating WRONG concepts; score 100% voice gate while paraphrasing a different chapter; pass all four SHACL gates while the ontology individuals describe concepts the source never names; cite 25 references with 0 attributions to the actual primary source.

When the pipeline has a single authoritative primary source, add a separate measurement dimension — primary-source-concept-coverage (BP-D31). Related to L-44 (VocabularySelfAuditBlindspot) at the discipline level; L-58 is the same blindness at the artefact-content level.

Renumbering note: this lesson was originally proposed as L-57 by the parallel session against v17.42.0 baseline. Renumbered to L-58 in OE Pack canonical numbering because v17.43.0 already used L-57 for DeprecateRatherThanFabricate."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_57_DeprecateRatherThanFabricate .

kb:Lesson_L_59_WidgetPhysicsMustEncodeDomainSemantics a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-59: Visual similarity to a domain construct does not encode the construct's semantics; the widget's input-output behaviour must enforce the domain constraint"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-59"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Originally proposed as L-58; renumbered to L-59. Pairs with BP-D32."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D32_InteractivityCouplingMustEncodeDomainSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_45_DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood ;
    skos:definition """A widget with three independent sliders + canvas triangle LOOKS like a triple-constraint visualisation (matches expected visual signature, passes interactive-count gate, page ABox correctly declares the widget's intended demonstration target).

But the behaviour does not enforce the constraint. A learner can set scope=150, schedule=50, budget=50 — three independent values. The triangle deforms accordingly. This visualises an UNBALANCED project, not the triple-constraint coupling. The widget teaches the OPPOSITE of what it claims.

For any widget claiming to demonstrate a coupled-variable construct (triple-constraint, supply-demand, force-equilibrium, cost-quality-time), the input behaviour must enforce the coupling. Independent controls are appropriate ONLY when the construct itself permits independent variation. Related to L-45 (DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood).

Renumbering: originally proposed as L-58; renumbered to L-59 per v17.43.0 collision."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_58_PipelineMetricsDoNotMeasureSourceFidelity .

kb:Lesson_L_60_MegaMenuRequiresMoreThanOneClick a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-60: Dropdown mega-menus impose interaction cost without scanning benefit for typical Stage 4 single-chapter pages"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-60"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Originally proposed as L-59; renumbered to L-60. n=1 disclosed."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D34_PermanentVisibleTopNavigationOverDropdowns,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """COM0463 Ch.5 has 16 user-facing sections — small enough that 16 short pill labels fit in one horizontal row (with wrap to 2-3 rows on mobile). v2.0.0 grouped into 3 dropdowns (Sections/Key Terms/Resources): each required a click to open; auto-closed on outside-click; another click to switch.

Cost: cannot scan section structure at a glance — must click to discover where you can go. "Where am I" requires opening the dropdown again. Keyboard navigation degrades.

Supposed benefit: "reduces visual clutter at top of page". In practice the 50px sticky menu bar is already there; the dropdowns are strictly worse.

For pages with ≤25 sections AND short labels, use permanent inline top-bar (sticky, always visible, no click). Mega-menus reserved for portal pages with >50 navigable destinations or long section titles.

Per L-40 honest disclosure: this lesson is grounded in n=1 user feedback (this user, COM0463 Ch.5 v2.0.0 → v2.1.0). The principle is debatable independently of the anecdote — see BP-D34 threshold conditions.

Renumbering: originally proposed as L-59; renumbered to L-60 per v17.43.0 collision."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_59_WidgetPhysicsMustEncodeDomainSemantics .

kb:Lesson_L_62_SameFileLooseAndInZipMeansBothCounted a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-62: Attaching the same file both loose AND inside a zip does not deduplicate; the loose copy still auto-loads"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-62"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source section: §2.2 rule 2 of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D36_TextFormatFilesOver200KBMustBeZipped,
        kb:BestPractice_D38_InventoryUploadsBeforeReadingAndEstimateContextCost ;
    skos:definition """Attaching the same file both loose AND inside a zip does not deduplicate; the loose copy still auto-loads. The zip is treated as separate binary; the loose copy is treated as text and ingested.

Subtle failure: user creates artifact_bundle.zip containing report.html, then attaches BOTH report.html AND artifact_bundle.zip "to be safe". The 800 KB loose report.html auto-loads to context immediately; the zip sits unread until Claude extracts it. Net effect: context is consumed as if zip discipline was not applied. Worse: user believes the zip discipline was followed.

Detection: at session start, list /mnt/user-data/uploads/ and look for both X.html and X.zip (or any zip containing X.html) — if found, the loose copy is the budget burner.

Operationalized by BP-D38 (inventory at session start with explicit "loose + zip" check)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_79_CitationTheaterDrift a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-79: Citing a rule is a generation event, not a gate"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-79"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Conversation.txt line 435: 'Citing BP-D1/BP-D2 dozens of times this session. Citing them did nothing.'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Pasting a verbatim BP/L definition in a ceremony does not, by itself, change what the next tool call does. Citing a rule is a string-generation event in the same probability distribution as the prose that follows — it does not constitute a positional constraint on action.

The discipline is satisfied only when the next concrete tool call enforces the rule (e.g., BP-D2 is enforced by view/bash on the actual file, not by quoting D2's definition text).

Detection signal: the ceremony block is followed by prose making content claims about a file that has not been read in this turn. Remedy: ceremony's step (3) must name a specific, transcript-checkable tool call as the enforcement, and that call must precede any claim it gates.

Anti-pattern this prevents: performing discipline as theater — listing the rules, pasting the definitions, then proceeding to do exactly what the rules forbid because the next-token continuation easier than the next-action discipline."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D1_AskBeforeAssuming,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

kb:Lesson_L_88_ScopedScrubRequiresVariantEnumerationAndKeepGuard a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-88: A scoped text-scrub requires variant enumeration and a keep-token guard"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-88"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instance, OE-reference scrub (v20.7.0, 2026-06-04): scrubbing the stale 'in Ontology Engineering v1.0.0' provenance clause. BP-D2 variant-enumeration revealed 'Ontology Engineering' also appeared in real citations (Tudorache et al.; Domain-Specific Ontology Engineering), a methodology class description (Waterfall Ontology Engineering), and the oepack-family package-name decision. A blind delete would have corrupted all of these. Guarded scrub removed 62 target clauses, 0 residual, all keep-tokens verified preserved."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries ;
    skos:definition "Before performing a scoped string-removal across one or more files (e.g. scrubbing a deprecated reference), do NOT run a blind global delete of the target substring. The same substring almost always also appears in occurrences that MUST be preserved — bibliographic citations, structural identifiers, package names, methodology descriptions. A blind delete corrupts those. Discipline: (1) programmatically enumerate the EXACT distinct string variants containing the target substring (with counts); (2) categorize them into scrub-targets vs keep-tokens; (3) remove only the precise target clause, guarding keep-tokens explicitly; (4) re-parse and verify both that the target is gone (count 0) AND that every keep-token survived."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-04T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_11_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_89_PreferPurposeBuiltPredicateOverGenericRelation a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-89: Prefer the purpose-built typed predicate over a generic catch-all when one exists"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-89"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instance, S-MEAS-2 migration (v20.4.0) reviewed at v20.12.0 (2026-06-07): the assistant bound 42 Scores to their Metrics using the generic, untyped oem:hasMetric ('generic relation linking a subject to a metric') when the TBox already declared oem:derivedFromMetric (domain Score, range Metric: 'Links a Score back to the Metric procedure that produced it'). The binding was valid under the S_MEAS_2 shape but used the understated predicate. Once user + assistant agreed the produced-by link is a score's DEFINING link, the typed predicate was clearly correct; the 42 were migrated. Surfaced by the user refusing to leave the loose predicate in place."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification ;
    skos:definition "When expressing a relationship, check whether the TBox already declares a purpose-built, typed predicate for exactly that relationship before reaching for a generic catch-all relation. Using a generic predicate where a precise one exists is valid but semantically understated: it loses domain/range typing and obscures meaning. The check is: does a declared predicate already name this exact relationship (matching domain and range)? If yes, use it. This matters most for a relationship that is DEFINING for the subject — where the link is part of what makes the subject interpretable — because there the imprecise predicate understates something essential, not incidental."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-07T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_90_ValidateTheSuiteThatGovernsTheArtifact a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-90: Validate against the SHACL suite that GOVERNS the artifact you author, not only the suites you change"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-90"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal instance, v20.16.0 (2026-06-07): across ~15 turns this session, release-history records (ReleaseEvent/Supersession) were authored every turn, and measurement_shacl/kb SHACL/Gate K were re-run each time — but oepack_release_history_shacl (the suite governing those very records) was NEVER run. 8 violations accumulated unseen (2 pre-existing Sup_045/046 + 3 ReleaseEvents authored this session), surfaced only when the lifecycle work finally ran that suite. Logged as finding F-RH1, deferred to a separate PATCH."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing ;
    skos:definition "When authoring or modifying instances of a facet (e.g. writing ReleaseEvent/Supersession records into the release-history ABox), re-run THAT facet's SHACL suite — not only the suites being actively edited in the task. A record can satisfy the suites you happen to be checking (measurement, kb, Gate K) while silently violating the suite that actually governs its own type. Validation coverage must be chosen by 'what type is this artifact?', not by 'what am I editing right now?'. Detection: if you author instances of class C and never run C's governing SHACL, you have a coverage gap regardless of how many other suites pass."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-07T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:Lesson_L_97_DiscriminationAppliesToVerifierOwnChecks a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-97: the discrimination mandate applies to the verifier’s OWN checks, not only the gates it runs"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-97"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted from STG registrant governed lesson SL-04 (stg_lessons_abox_v1_6_0.ttl, stg:proposedToOEE true), ORCP Phase D, OE Pack v20.23.15. Screened against OEE 99 lessons + 56 BPs by reading full definitions (not keyword match); confirmed genuinely additive (no existing OEE lesson states this). STG is an external peer governance ecosystem; lesson generalized from its gate-engineering domain."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://stg.org/governance#SL_04> ;
    skos:definition "A verification check too coarse to fail can pass for the wrong reason. SL-01/L-95 discrimination applies reflexively to the verifier itself: e.g. a parity check that compares only a final aggregate BAND (not the underlying values) passes even when the two implementations disagree internally. Every check the verifier itself runs must also be proven able to fail a known-bad input."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating .

kb:Lesson_L_X4_ProbabilisticProposesDeterministicDisposes a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-X4: Probabilistic LLM proposes / deterministic checkers dispose (compliant-checker architecture)"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-X4"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE_Session_Lessons_Proposal_v1_0_0 (a domain ecosystem session); Conversation.txt line 14421."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Because LLM outputs are inherently probabilistic, a COMPLIANCE LAYER for LLM-generated ontology or content must be deterministic and orthogonal.

The minimum triad: (1) logical consistency — reasoner (e.g., HermiT), (2) structural conformance — SHACL, (3) source faithfulness — verbatim/byte-level gate. Each catches a different class of LLM error; absence of any one leaves that class UNCAUGHT.

Any LLM-driven extraction or ontology-authoring component must declare (a) which deterministic checker(s) discipline its output, (b) which class of LLM error each catches, (c) which classes remain unchecked and must be disclosed as tier-C residuals (per BP-D47).

Absence of a declared deterministic check on a class of error is itself a DISCLOSURE OBLIGATION, not a silent gap.

Upstream architectural principle for which BP-D4 (BothHermiTAndShacl), L-76 (ProbabilisticAssessmentDisclosure), and BP-D47 (ConfidenceTierDisclosure) are operationalizations."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D47_ConfidenceTierDisclosureDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl,
        kb:Lesson_L_76_ProbabilisticAssessmentRequiresExplicitDisclosure .

kb:OEPACK_BP5_DatatypeHygiene a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-5: Datatype hygiene"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-5"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from redo:BP5_DatatypeHygiene (oepack_lessons_rdf_v1_0_0.ttl). Semantic deduplication against D-prefixed BPs deferred. (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Sprint 2 + Sprint 4 patterns."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Use language tags (@en) on natural-language strings. Use xsd:dateTime for any temporal value (not narrower types). Be aware that pyshacl treats plain literals and xsd:string-typed literals as distinct in sh:in."@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oepack-lessons-rdf#Lesson_L10>,
        <http://example.org/oepack-lessons-rdf#Lesson_L11>,
        <http://example.org/oepack-lessons-rdf#Lesson_L22> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L15 a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-15: Validate import-dependent files with imports merged"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-15"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from redo:Lesson_L15 (oepack-lessons-rdf, plural-class normalized). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "OE Ecosystem Review v1.0.1 patch L-15..L-20."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Standalone SHACL on a file that declares owl:imports produces false-positive findings: a reliability ontology C7+ flags every imported class as 'type not declared'. The methodologically correct context is the master-bundle pattern (BP-1: validate_with_imports.py)."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:OEPACK_BP1_ValidateWithImports .

kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L18 a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-18: Direct file inspection beats trusted summaries"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-18"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from redo:Lesson_L18 (oepack-lessons-rdf, plural-class normalized). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 1 Issue I-08 ground-truth verification."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Closure docs claimed 'Sprint 5 lesson: avoid AllDisjointClasses in foundational ontologies' — direct file inspection found 272 instances in foundational ontologies, all clean under HermiT. The summary was over-stated; the file ground truth was the data needed for the L-12-rev refinement."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L12rev .

kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L22 a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-22: pyshacl sh:in does strict node-identity comparison"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-22"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Migrated from redo:Lesson_L22 (oepack-lessons-rdf, plural-class normalized). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en,
        "Phase 2 oqc_thresholds v1.0.0 → v1.0.1 patch."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Plain literals and explicit-xsd:string-typed literals do not match each other in pyshacl's sh:in. Either emit plain literals consistently, or relax shapes to use sh:hasValue. Encountered as 240/240 violations on oqc_thresholds v1.0.0; resolved in v1.0.1."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Dir_Increase> a bl:MetricDirection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "increase"@en ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#DoDC_CleanGate> a bl:DoDCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Clean gate reports zero matches across bodies and filenames, proven discriminating"@en ;
    bl:hasCheckQuery "python3 05-clean-gate/oe_method_clean_gate_v*.py ." ;
    bl:hasCriterionStatus <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Status_Open> ;
    bl:hasExpectedResult "exit 0, VERDICT: CLEAN, 0 matches" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#DoDC_CompletenessGate> a bl:DoDCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Completeness gate exists and fails a fixture with an emptied ABox (L-95)"@en ;
    bl:hasCheckQuery "run the completeness gate against a fixture with one ABox emptied" ;
    bl:hasCriterionStatus <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Status_Open> ;
    bl:hasExpectedResult "exit 1 on the fixture, exit 0 on the package" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#DoDC_ManifestAndShacl> a bl:DoDCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Manifest self-verifies; all SHACL suites conform with zero violations"@en ;
    bl:hasCheckQuery "sha256sum -c on the manifest; pyshacl over every shipped suite" ;
    bl:hasCriterionStatus <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Status_Open> ;
    bl:hasExpectedResult "all lines OK; 0 sh:Violation" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#DoDC_NoUnpopulatedClass> a bl:DoDCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Every governed TBox class is populated or explicitly declared abstract (L-71)"@en ;
    bl:hasCheckQuery "every owl:Class in a shipped TBox has >=1 individual or is declared abstract" ;
    bl:hasCriterionStatus <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Status_Open> ;
    bl:hasExpectedResult "0 unpopulated non-abstract classes" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#DoDC_SelfGoverning> a bl:DoDCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Package registers through its own ORCP and records its own release history"@en ;
    bl:hasCheckQuery "the package carries a release-history ABox with >=1 event and an ORCP-registered exemplar" ;
    bl:hasCriterionStatus <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Status_Open> ;
    bl:hasExpectedResult ">=1 release event and 1 registered exemplar present" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Epic_CarryOperationalContent> a bl:Epic,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Carry the operational ABoxes that make the method usable"@en ;
    bl:commitsToObjective <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Objective_CompleteEcosystem> ;
    bl:contributesToMilestone <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M1_ContentComplete> ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-E1" ;
    bl:hasState <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#State_InProgress> ;
    bl:memberOfContainer <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Backlog_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> ;
    skos:definition """11 populated ABoxes were dropped by the previous build: configuration (16
naming conventions, 12 version tokens, 13 competency questions), quality (240 threshold rows, 41
pitfalls), reliability (19 blind spots, 16 failure mitigations, 14 verification modalities),
measurement (77 thresholds, 14 metrics), plus core, meta, ae, risk, performance, testing. Roughly
9,000 triples of operational content. Without them the shipped classes have zero population."""@en .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Epic_CompletenessGate> a bl:Epic,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Gate sufficiency, not only cleanliness"@en ;
    bl:commitsToObjective <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Objective_CompleteEcosystem> ;
    bl:contributesToMilestone <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M2_GatesSymmetric> ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-E3" ;
    bl:hasState <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#State_InProgress> ;
    bl:memberOfContainer <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Backlog_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> ;
    skos:definition """The previous build had one gate, for absence of personal data. Nothing could
detect a hollow package, and nothing did: manifest verified, SHACL conformed, clean gate passed on a
package that could not be used. Symmetric gating is the structural fix."""@en .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Epic_SelfGovernance> a bl:Epic,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Ship the release-history and registration machinery"@en ;
    bl:commitsToObjective <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Objective_CompleteEcosystem> ;
    bl:contributesToMilestone <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M3_SelfGoverning> ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-E2" ;
    bl:hasState <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#State_InProgress> ;
    bl:memberOfContainer <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Backlog_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> ;
    skos:definition """The release-history TBox carries over so an adopter can record releases and
supersessions. The originating ABox (81 events of project history) does not; the distributable
starts a fresh history with its own first event."""@en .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Epic_WorkedExemplar> a bl:Epic,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Ship a package that has actually been through the protocol"@en ;
    bl:commitsToObjective <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Objective_CompleteEcosystem> ;
    bl:contributesToMilestone <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M3_SelfGoverning> ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-E4" ;
    bl:hasState <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#State_InProgress> ;
    bl:memberOfContainer <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Backlog_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> ;
    skos:definition """ORCP currently ships as a protocol document with nothing that has traversed
it. A minimal synthetic package, registered through the real protocol with its gates green, makes
usage runnable rather than described."""@en .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Exclusion_OriginatingHistory> a bl:ScopeExclusion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The originating release-history ABox (81 project events) is excluded; its TBox is not"@en ;
    bl:decidedBy bl:Owner ;
    bl:excludesConcern "oepack_release_history_abox, 81 release events" ;
    bl:hasExclusionRationale "It is our history, not the method's; the TBox carries over so an adopter records their own." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Exclusion_ProjectDocuments> a bl:ScopeExclusion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Closure notes, findings registers, strategies and handovers (22 files) are excluded"@en ;
    bl:decidedBy bl:Owner ;
    bl:excludesConcern "22 documents in 04-documentation/ named for a project, closure, finding register, strategy or handover" ;
    bl:hasExclusionRationale "They record what happened in one programme; an adopter needs the method, not our minutes." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Exclusion_ProvAndAudit> a bl:ScopeExclusion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Agent-attributed provenance records and project audit artifacts (6 files) are excluded"@en ;
    bl:decidedBy bl:Owner ;
    bl:excludesConcern "05-prov-records/ and 06-audit-artifacts/, 6 files" ;
    bl:hasExclusionRationale "They attribute activity to named agents and record project-specific findings." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Exclusion_RegistrantDeposits> a bl:ScopeExclusion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Other parties' deposited packages (50 files) are excluded"@en ;
    bl:decidedBy bl:Owner ;
    bl:excludesConcern "06-registrant-deposits/ in full, 50 files" ;
    bl:hasExclusionRationale "Other parties' packages are their property and their record; redistributing them is neither ours to do nor useful to an adopter." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Goal_MethodTransfers> a bl:Goal,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The method transfers intact to practitioners outside the originating context"@en ;
    bl:contributesToMission <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Mission_DistributableMethod> ;
    bl:decidedBy bl:Owner ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M1_ContentComplete> a bl:Milestone,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "M1 — all operational content carried and generalised"@en ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-M1" ;
    bl:hasTargetDate "2026-08-10T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    bl:partOfRoadmap <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Roadmap_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M2_GatesSymmetric> a bl:Milestone,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "M2 — completeness gate proven discriminating alongside the clean gate"@en ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-M2" ;
    bl:hasTargetDate "2026-08-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    bl:partOfRoadmap <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Roadmap_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M4_Redistributable> a bl:Milestone,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "M4 — licence and third-party lineage resolved; release tagged"@en ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-M4" ;
    bl:hasTargetDate "2026-08-31T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    bl:partOfRoadmap <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Roadmap_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Mission_DistributableMethod> a bl:Mission,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A practitioner outside the originating context can adopt and run the method"@en ;
    bl:decidedBy bl:Owner ;
    bl:hasMissionStatement "Enable a practitioner outside the originating context to adopt and run the method, not merely read about it." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> ;
    skos:definition """The distributable must let a colleague, student or academic PRACTISE the
method, not merely read about it. The test is executable: they can run every gate, populate every
governed class, record a release, and register a package — without access to the originating
project."""@en .

kb:BestPractice_D22_PreservePrefixesOnSerialization a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D22: Preserve namespace prefixes on Graph.serialize() and verify the @prefix block before consumers read the file"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D22"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.23.0 L' execution as the discipline-level partner to L-46. Per BP-D10 ranking — captured as the discovered drift pattern surfaces operational impact (failed Fix-5/Fix-6 due to wrong namespace on property predicates)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_46_RdflibPrefixRebindingMasks_RawIRIs,
        kb:OEPACK_BP5_DatatypeHygiene ;
    skos:definition """Discipline:

1. Before serializing any rdflib Graph whose consumers must read the Turtle file, explicitly bind ALL relevant namespaces via g.namespace_manager.bind(prefix, namespace, override=True, replace=True).

2. After serialization, programmatically grep the resulting file for expected @prefix lines (per BP-D8 manifest-style discipline).

3. When debugging cross-namespace SHACL findings, ALWAYS inspect the underlying triple via rdflib (s, p, o tuples printed with str(...)) — never trust prefix-qualified renderings in pyshacl reports for namespace identification. Per L-46.

4. Convention: in this ecosystem the prefix "redo:" denotes http://example.org/redo# and "rel:" denotes http://example.org/reliability#. rdflib may rebind either; verify with raw IRI inspection.

Rationale: prefix rebindings during serialization create the same class of "names look right but mean something else" bug as L-45 (Diagrammatic Declaration Falsehood). The display is not the truth."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D10_RiceDepFactorDecisionDiscipline,
        kb:BestPractice_D8_Sha256Manifest,
        kb:Lesson_L_45_DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood .

kb:BestPractice_D24_OntologyLevelLicenseAndProvenance a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D24: Every owl:Ontology declaration must carry license (dcterms:license), copyright (dcterms:rights/rightsHolder), publisher, creator, and PROV-O lineage metadata"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D24"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.28.0 industrial-hardening release. Triggered by the v17.27.0 readiness assessment which identified missing ontology-level license/provenance as a TIER-A blocker for regulated industrial deployment."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:BestPractice_D8_Sha256Manifest ;
    skos:definition """Industrial-hardening requirement. An ontology shipped without explicit license, copyright, publisher, creator, and PROV-O lineage CANNOT be consumed under contractual / regulated terms — recipient has no machine-verifiable claim on terms of use.

Required predicates (per BP-D24, applied to EVERY owl:Ontology IRI):
  1. dcterms:license      — link to a license URL (e.g., CC BY 4.0)
  2. dcterms:rights       — human-readable copyright statement (lang-tagged)
  3. dcterms:rightsHolder — IRI of the organization holding copyright
  4. dcterms:publisher    — IRI of the publishing organization
  5. dcterms:creator      — IRI of the author Agent (PROV-O person/org)
  6. dcterms:created      — xsd:date of first version
  7. dcterms:modified     — xsd:date of this version
  8. dcterms:identifier   — string identifier matching the local name
  9. prov:wasGeneratedBy  — IRI of the release Activity
 10. prov:wasAttributedTo — IRI of the responsible Agent

Central provenance file (oe-prov_v1_0_0.ttl) holds the Agent/Activity individuals so all subject ontologies attribute via shared IRIs (machine-linkable, not just literal strings).

Effect in v17.28.0 (per BP-D2 before/after):
  - License: 0/36 → 37/37 (100%)
  - Rights: 0/36 → 37/37 (100%)
  - Publisher: 0/36 → 37/37 (100%)
  - PROV wasGeneratedBy: 0/36 → 36/37 (97%, oe-prov self-excluded)
  - Creator IRI: 1/36 → 37/37 (100% machine-linkable)"""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

kb:BestPractice_D29_CriticalABoxPROV a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D29: Critical ABox individuals (Lesson/BestPractice/RegressionTest/RoadmapPlan/PerformanceTest + isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory-true) MUST carry full PROV-O triples"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D29"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.34.0 Option H. Extends BP-D24 (ontology-level PROV) down to instance level for the regulated-industrial use case."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D24_LicenseAndPROVAtOntologyLevel,
        kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_54_PROVReleaseDateIsRelease ;
    skos:definition """For regulated-industrial use and academic citation, the OE Pack must support queries of the form: "which agent authored this lesson?", "when was this regression test added?", "what release activity produced this performance benchmark?"

Discipline: every individual of the following classes MUST carry three PROV-O triples:
  - prov:wasGeneratedBy <release Activity IRI>
  - prov:wasAttributedTo <agent IRI> (typically oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor)
  - prov:generatedAtTime "<date>"^^xsd:date (per L-54: release date)

Critical classes:
  - kb:LessonLearned + kb:Lesson
  - kb:BestPractice
  - kb:RegressionTest
  - kb:RoadmapPlan
  - performance:PerformanceTest (+ subclasses)
  - Any individual with kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true

Mechanical enforcement: Regression_CriticalABoxHasPROV (v17.34.0) catches any isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory-true individual missing the 3 triples. Other classes enforced by extension in v17.34.x or as new regression tests as needed.

Activity discovery: oe-prov v1.1.0+ ships one Activity per OE Pack release (Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_28_0..v17_34_0). When authoring new individuals, route them to the current-release Activity."""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D24_OntologyLevelLicenseAndProvenance,
        kb:Regression_CriticalABoxHasPROV .

kb:BestPractice_D32_InteractivityCouplingMustEncodeDomainSemantics a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D32: Interactive widgets must encode the domain semantics they claim to demonstrate, not merely present similar-looking controls"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D32"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Pairs with L-59. Acknowledged overlap with L-45 (per proposal honest disclosure §2)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D31_PrimarySourceConceptCoverageGate,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_45_DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood,
        kb:Lesson_L_59_WidgetPhysicsMustEncodeDomainSemantics ;
    skos:definition """Interactive widgets must encode the domain semantics they claim to demonstrate, not merely present similar-looking controls.

Source incident: COM0463 Ch.5 v1.0.0–v1.1.0 had three independent sliders + canvas triangle visualising "triple constraint". Operationally false to Marchewka's "in balance" framing where scope/schedule/budget are coupled. v2.0.0 introduced equilateral-coupling: one slider drives all three; triangle stays equilateral. Independent variation is mechanically impossible.

Procedure:
  1. Identify what the widget claims to demonstrate (a demonstratesSubject property).
  2. Identify the domain constraint the subject embodies.
  3. Encode the constraint in the widget's input-output behaviour.
  4. Test: a learner manipulating the widget cannot produce behaviour that contradicts      the domain constraint.

Anti-pattern prevented: "Three sliders that look like a triangle" — visual vocabulary of a domain concept without enforcement of the constraint. Teaches the WRONG mental model while passing mechanical Stage 4 measurement.

Note: may overlap with kb:Lesson_L_45_DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood (static author-time diagram drift). BP-D32 is about interactive-runtime widget drift; the failure modes require different mitigations, hence two distinct catalogue entries."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D37_StableGovernanceArtifactsInProjectKnowledgeBase a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D37: Stable governance artifacts SHOULD live in the Claude Project knowledge base, not as per-chat uploads"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D37"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source sections: §2.2 rule 3 and §6 of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D36_TextFormatFilesOver200KBMustBeZipped ;
    skos:definition """claude.ai Projects support a knowledge base that is RAG-retrieved on demand rather than auto-loaded into every chat. Stable governance artifacts (used across many sessions) belong there, not as per-chat uploads.

In Project knowledge base: a reliability ontology / META / OQC / CORE / AMOD ontologies and shape sets; OE Pack version manifests; verifier scripts; README files; course content blueprints; standing skill files; an algebraic framework Phase artifacts.

Per-chat upload (zipped per BP-D36): workbench iterations; generator/builder iterations; course HTML pages; PPTX builds.

Anti-pattern prevented: Re-uploading the same governance bundle in every chat, paying context-token cost N times for N sessions when one RAG-retrieval would suffice."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D53_ThreeFileCanonicalStructure a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D53: An ontology subject ships as at most one T-Box file, one A-Box file and one SHACL file sharing one ontology IRI; no combined-form, extension, follow-up or patch files"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D53"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's standing rule, set in a conversation of 2026-05-13 and restated 2026-10-05 (\"3-file structure was set in our previous conversation. What is the reason of the drift?\"). Reconstructed here from what the governed vocabularies already say: the comment of quality:OntologyFileStructureCompliance (which cites it as BP-D18, an identifier that resolves to a different rule), and the \"3-file canonical set ... option-a\" comments in the shipped core, meta, performance and algorithm-efficiency A-Box files. Measured 2026-10-05 across the monorepo: 27 of 166 parsed files mix roles, 21 names break the naming pattern, 10 ontology identities ship several versions side by side."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D51_OngoingEnhancementLineageExemption,
        kb:BestPractice_D52_SearchBeforeCreatingArtifact,
        kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming ;
    skos:changeNote "Superseded in its unit on 2026-10-06 by BP-D54 (owner's ruling): the unit is the package, one T-Box, one A-Box and one SHACL file in all, not one triad per subject. What stands from this rule: no file mixes roles, no patch or extension files, file names follow BP-D6, one current file per identity, and the register ratchet."@en ;
    skos:definition """An ontology subject is shipped as three files, one per role, all declaring the same owl:Ontology IRI (the "option-a" convention):

  (1) T-Box: the classes and properties and their annotations. No individuals.
  (2) A-Box: the individuals and instance data that use the T-Box's terms. No class or property definitions.
  (3) SHACL: the shapes that say what valid instance data looks like. No definitions and no instance data.

At most one file per role per subject. Not allowed: a combined-form file that holds two or three roles; an extension, follow-up, addition, patch or re-emit file beside the canonical three (the change goes into the canonical file, as a new version of it); several versions of one file shipped side by side (BP-D7: one current file per identity, retired versions leave the package with a release-history record). Every file name follows BP-D6, and its prefix names the subject.

An ABox that is deliberately and visibly incomplete may carry kb:isOngoingEnhancementLineage (BP-D51), which exempts the pairing check only.

Enforcement: the structure check (repo-tooling oe_structure_check) reads each file's parsed content, not its name, and runs on every push to every package. Violations that existed on 2026-10-05 are recorded in a dated debt register, and the check fails on any violation that is not in it, so the debt can only shrink. Each owning session clears its own entries; an entry the owner decides to keep is marked as an exception with the owner's ruling.

Why a rule and not a habit: each role changes for a different reason and at a different pace. A combined file forces a new vocabulary version when one instance is added, hides a missing role from a pairing check, and leaves the rules as comments instead of checkable shapes."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-05T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D18_LangTagOnXsdStringInconsistency,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics .

kb:BestPractice_D54_OneTBoxOneABoxOneShaclPerPackage a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D54: A package ships ONE T-Box file, ONE A-Box file and ONE SHACL file in all, each with one ontology header and one version; its former subjects live inside as modules, and its retired history is held the same way in one archive triad"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D54"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's ruling of 2026-10-06, given first as \"We need 3-file structure for the current active files and 3-files for all archive to keep the history\" and corrected the same day: \"No, not per subject. All subjects should be consolidated in one file per concept and at the end we will have just 1 file for A-Box, 1 file for T-Box, and 1 file for SHACL.\" The ruling reached this session through a package session's handover, whose reading (one triad per package, not per subject) left five points open; the owner settled the two that matter in the OEE governance session of 2026-10-06: the unit is the package, with former subjects kept as modules inside the three files, and the archive is one triad per package. Measured 2026-10-06: the oe-pack package had 13 T-Box, 21 A-Box and 27 SHACL ontology identities; the a domain ecosystem package about 10, 18 and 4 in 83 files."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D53_ThreeFileCanonicalStructure,
        kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming,
        kb:Lesson_L_125_CitedRuleMustResolveAndShipItsEnforcement ;
    skos:changeNote "Later rulings of the owner, 2026-10-06 and 2026-10-07, which the definition below carries without restating the whole rule: (a) each package carries its own structure-debt register and governance keeps the rules and a generated aggregate; a package's own register may only shrink what the aggregate records, and the aggregate rises only with a recorded ruling; (b) one class table (OE_STRUCTURE_CLASSES) decides what each tool reads or skips, and rule X1 reports a nested unit that no row names; (c) files outside the domain (lineage, governance, maintenance) are units of their own that also meet the three-file rule, a role with zero files being allowed, and examples may be a unit of their own; (d) registrations obey the same rule: the registrations are one unit, each registrant is a module, and a later registration is added with the registration-unit tool in extend mode, never as a new file; (e) generated shape files leave the package; (f) rule D1 forbids identical anonymous copies and is opt-in per package. Enforcement: oe_structure_check, rules N1, N2, V1, R1, H1, U1, A1, M1, D1 and X1, on every push to every package and in the publisher; the aggregate generator verifies in CI."@en,
        "Membership (2026-10-06, oe-pack v20.99.0, the first step of consolidating this pack): a named subject says which module it belongs to with `dcterms:isPartOf <the module's header IRI>`, one triple per subject; the module itself is an untyped resource at the former header IRI with `dcterms:identifier`. `rdfs:isDefinedBy` is NOT used for membership: it is already in use with a different meaning (443 A-Box individuals name a finer sub-topic with it, so it cannot record which file a term came from) and its coverage was 93% of T-Box terms, 26% of A-Box individuals and 0.4% of shapes. Header IRIs of the four files of one subject are shared, so the T-Box, A-Box and shapes of one subject name the same module. Enforcement: checker rule M1 (opt-in per package through a register line)."@en,
        "Ruling of 2026-10-06 (owner, on the first request from a course session): a course repository is ONE package, and its ontology files are counted per package, never per chapter. Rule U1 therefore stays as written and applies to course repositories like every other package; the chapters of a course are modules inside the package's three files, not packages of their own. A course repository's file-count lines in the debt register stay `debt` (counts as ceilings) and shrink as its chapters are consolidated into modules; no refinement of U1 and no exception is made."@en,
        "The owner ruled on 2026-10-07 that a package carries its own structure-debt register, in its own package, and the governance package keeps the rules and a generated aggregate. This practice already said that the owning session clears its own register lines; one central file in the governance package made that impossible to obey, because clearing a line meant editing a package another session owns, which boundary B1 forbids, so the only path was a handover and a wait. Measured on the day of the ruling: 264 of the register's 268 lines belonged to packages its holder does not own. The arrangement now: a package's own register may remove a line and lower a count with nobody else involved; it may not raise a count, add a line the aggregate does not carry, or raise a status to exception, which is what keeps the ratchet from certifying itself; a package with no register of its own is governed by the aggregate alone, so adoption is incremental. Enforced by oe_structure_check v1.4.0 and oe_debt_aggregate v1.0.0."@en,
        "The owner ruled on 2026-10-07 that examples are outside the coverage of a package's domain ontologies, as the governance and lineage ontologies are, and so need not be folded into the domain unit: an example may hold its own T-Box, A-Box and SHACL file as a unit of its own. What this practice asks of an example is the same three-file shape, not membership of the domain unit's files. The mechanism is the one that already defines a package: a folder holding its own VERSION.txt is a unit of its own, so an example kept in a folder of its own with its own version, three files and its own manifest is measured as one unit and needs no change to the rules or the checker (measured on the day: the exemplar in oe-method is exactly this shape and carries no violation). Folding an example into the domain unit as modules stays allowed; the ruling adds the second choice and removes none. Two register lines record an example today, one in each of two packages (a worked-example A-Box whose name says extension, and a second-domain example holding a T-Box and an A-Box in one file); each owning session may settle its line either way, in its own release."@en,
        "The owner ruled on 2026-10-07 that registrations obey the same rule as every other unit: the individual ontology files made to register a package (the deposits held in the pack) are consolidated into ONE T-Box, ONE A-Box and ONE SHACL file, each registrant a module, so that the number of files does not grow with every registration and no file can be forgotten. The owner's reason: the possibility of drift grows with the number of files, a missing file then becomes possible, and the result is rework; the structure must reduce files while keeping cohesiveness. What this practice asks: a registration is a unit of its own (its own folder with its own version, as the governance and example units are); files are sorted by the roles they hold; nothing is lost and anonymous nodes are not duplicated, proved before release; the other packages that cite a deposit's path are told, in plain words, which module now holds what they cited. Copies of other packages inside a deposit and test inputs stay what they are, and the snapshot in the audit-artifacts folder is retired history and goes where retired history goes. The classes of file, the questions that decide which class a file is in, and the conditions of each are in OE_STRUCTURE_EXCEPTION_TAXONOMY_v1_0_0.md; there are no exceptions recorded."@en ;
    skos:definition """The unit of structure is the PACKAGE. A package ships, in all:

  (1) ONE T-Box file: every class and property of the package. No individuals.
  (2) ONE A-Box file: every individual and all instance data. No class or property definitions.
  (3) ONE SHACL file: every shape. No definitions and no instance data.

Each file declares ONE owl:Ontology header and carries ONE version (BP-D7); a change to any part of a file gives the whole file a new version and the old one leaves the package. File names follow BP-D6.

MODULES. What used to be a separate subject (its own file triad and ontology IRI) becomes a module inside the three files. A term keeps its IRI, and its rdfs:isDefinedBy keeps pointing at the former subject's IRI. The module itself is recorded as an UNTYPED resource at the former subject's IRI, carrying dcterms:isPartOf (the package's ontology IRI), dcterms:identifier, rdfs:label and owl:versionInfo (the module's own version, which moves only when that module changes). A module is never typed owl:Ontology (one header per file) and never typed with a domain class (it would read as an individual in the T-Box). Another package that owl:imports a former subject's IRI must repoint the import to the package's ontology IRI.

ARCHIVE. A package's retired history lives in its folder named archive, held the same way: ONE archive T-Box, ONE archive A-Box, ONE archive SHACL file. The archive keeps retired terms and the version chain (each retired version identified by its own owl:versionIRI and linked by owl:priorVersion). Limit stated here, not hidden: one RDF graph cannot hold two different definitions of the same IRI, so a retired term whose IRI is still in use keeps only its last retired definition in the archive file; every complete retired state of the package remains recoverable from its git history and release tags, which are the complete record.

ENFORCEMENT. oe_structure_check v1.1.0 reads parsed content and reports: N1 (name), N2 (patch name), V1 (versions side by side), R1 (mixed roles), H1 (more than one owl:Ontology in a file), U1 (more than one active file of a role in a package) and A1 (more than one archive file of a role). It runs on every push to every package in CI and in the publisher, and the ceremony reports it; a violation outside the dated debt register fails, and a U1 or A1 count that grows beyond its recorded files=N fails. The owning session clears its own register lines.

SCOPE. This supersedes BP-D53 where it says "per ontology subject": roles may not mix, names, versions and the register stand; only the unit changed. BP-D53 stays in the store as the record of the earlier rule.""" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics .

kb:Lesson_L_137_ARegisterLineForAPackageOutsideTheScanIsNotMeasuredNotCleared a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-137: A debt line whose package is not in the scan was not measured; reporting it CLEARED deletes another package's debt"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-137"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Found 2026-10-07 by the session that owns two course repositories, which filed it into this package's inbox with its own measurements. The shared structure-debt register governs packages in this monorepo and packages that live in their own repositories. Run inside the monorepo, a line naming a repository that is not there had no violation to match, and the checker reported it CLEARED -- remove it, the violation is gone. Six lines were removed on that report in register v1.9.0, and the owning session's publisher then refused both repositories for a violation outside the register, blocking a release that had nothing wrong with it. Re-measured 2026-10-07 at both repositories the way their publisher measures them: the counts were identical to the lines that had been removed, so nothing about those repositories had changed and only the register had."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating ;
    skos:definition "A register that governs more than one repository cannot be reconciled by a run that sees only one of them: a line with nothing to match was either fixed or never examined, and the two are indistinguishable from inside the scan. Report a line whose package is absent as NOT MEASURED, count it separately, and never offer it for removal; clear a line only from a run that actually read that package, which for a package in its own repository means a run at that repository. The rule is the one the same checker already applies to itself -- a scan of nothing is not a pass -- turned on its own register. Detection signal: a CLEARED report for a package whose files the scan never opened, or an owning session reporting that its publisher refuses a release for debt that was recorded and then removed."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_24_DomainAdditionTriggersDisjointnessClash a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-24: Adding rdfs:domain via usage inference can trigger OWL 2 DL inconsistency when usage spans disjoint classes"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-24"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-15 in v17.26.0 N execution. After applying BP-D23 to add rdfs:domain to ae:solutionForm based on observed usage on RecurrenceRelation instances, ecosystem HermiT detected inconsistency. Recovery via class-hierarchy walk found StructuralConcept as the LCA. L-48 captures the disjointness check that BP-D23 must incorporate going forward."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D23_InferDomainFromUsage,
        kb:Lesson_L_23_AttributeRichnessDualNature ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode: a datatype property used on instances of two classes that happen to be declared owl:disjointWith (often via AllDisjointClasses axioms) cannot receive ANY single class as rdfs:domain without making instances of one class implicitly typed as the other — yielding HermiT inconsistency.

Observation: in v17.26.0 N execution, ae:solutionForm was inferred to have domain RecurrenceRelation OR ClosedFormSolution based on observed usage. These two classes are declared owl:disjointWith. Assigning rdfs:domain RecurrenceRelation alone forced ae:GoldenRatio_Constant (typed ClosedFormSolution) to ALSO be RecurrenceRelation → HermiT detected ontology-level inconsistency.

Applied fix: walk class hierarchy upward until a common ancestor that is NOT disjoint with either usage class; assign that as domain. In the present case, both RecurrenceRelation and ClosedFormSolution descend from ae:StructuralConcept, which became the chosen domain.

Procedure (per BP-D23 update): after inferring candidate domain(s) from usage, query the ontology for owl:disjointWith / AllDisjointClasses between observed types. If disjoint, walk subClassOf upward to find least common ancestor; use that. If no LCA exists, leave property orphaned (per L-40 — better to honestly disclose than to fabricate consistency)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_48_FindingsDoNotAuthorizeReopeningClosedDecisions .

kb:Lesson_L_38_NamePatternIsHeuristic a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-38: Name-Pattern Classification Is A Starting Heuristic"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-38"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced at step 9 (a reliability ontology 3-file split). Step 5's phased-migration plan estimated 31 a reliability ontology properties would migrate to OEM based on name-pattern matching. Raw inspection at step 9 found only 2 of 16 metric-related properties are abstract enough for OEM; the other 14 are a reliability ontology-specific reliability assertions that stay in a reliability ontology."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When inventorying classes or properties for migration to another subject via name-substring matching (e.g., '*Metric*'), the result is a STARTING heuristic, not a final inventory. Verify each candidate by inspecting its rdfs:domain, rdfs:range, and skos:definition before committing to migration scope. Name-substring matching produces overcounts; semantic inspection produces accurate scope."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_42_VerifyBothSidesOfRelationship a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-42: Verify Both Sides Of A Relationship Before Classifying Its Status"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-42"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced at step 12 (OEE-Alignment retirement). I initially classified 3 of 11 owl:equivalentClass axioms as 'live' by inspecting only the RHS in <reliability:> namespace. Re-inspection showed the LHS in <quality-criteria:> namespace was retired for ALL 11 cases, making all 11 axioms moot. Caught before shipping; would have produced an unnecessary OQC v3.0.1 PATCH if not."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "When evaluating the status of a binary relationship (owl:equivalentClass, owl:sameAs, rdfs:subClassOf, etc.) for retirement, migration, or preservation: BOTH endpoints must be inspected. Inspecting only one side produces false-positive 'live' classifications. The discipline extends L-37 (DRY-attribution completeness — verify all sources, not one) and L-38 (name-pattern is heuristic — verify by inspecting domain/range/definition) to the symmetric case: for relationships, verify both endpoints exist before classifying the relationship as live."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_37_DRYAttributionCompleteness,
        kb:Lesson_L_38_NamePatternIsHeuristic .

kb:Lesson_L_54_PROVReleaseDateIsRelease a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-54: prov:generatedAtTime on critical ABox individuals records the RELEASE DATE, not the conceptual authoring date"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-54"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.34.0 Option H — surfaced when assigning timestamps to pre-existing LessonLearned individuals from earlier releases."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D29_CriticalABoxPROV ;
    skos:definition """Subtlety: a Lesson Learned might have been INSIGHT-COMPLETE weeks before the OE Pack release that ships it. Per PROV-O semantics, prov:generatedAtTime marks when the individual was GENERATED — for OE Pack purposes, this is the release date (when the individual entered the published catalogue), not the conceptual moment of discovery.

Rationale: ontology consumers querying "what catalogue entries existed as of date X?" need release-date semantics. Consumers tracking research provenance should use a separate property (e.g., dcterms:created on the lesson) for conceptual date.

Applied in v17.34.0 by assigning all 208 critical individuals the same release-date timestamp ("2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime) corresponding to the v17.28.0..v17.33.0 shipping window. Future releases will add new lessons with fresh dates."""@en .

kb:Lesson_L_55_SemanticSHACLUpgradeRevealsHiddenFindings a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-55: Replacing skeleton SHACL shapes with semantic constraints reveals hidden data-quality findings that the prior mechanical coverage did not surface"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-55"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.35.0 Option C — surfaced when semantic SHACL upgrade for oe-measurement revealed 16 data-quality findings hidden by prior mechanical skeleton coverage."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D30_SemanticShapeUpgrade,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Observation: v17.32.0 achieved 100% mechanical SHACL coverage by adding 1151 skeleton shapes (sh:targetClass only). These provided NO validation value — they declared "every class has a shape" without checking anything about the instances.

v17.35.0 upgraded the 54 oe-measurement skeleton shapes to semantic shapes with sh:property constraints (datatype, cardinality, nodeKind). The upgrade IMMEDIATELY surfaced 16 real data-quality findings:
  - 12 violations: xsd:decimal expected but xsd:integer literals provided
  - 4 violations: skos:definition missing on v17.33.0-authored MeasurementBinding/    MeasurementContext instances (which inherit a Measurement constraint requiring     skos:definition)

Per L-40: these are REAL findings — the prior 100% coverage hid them. Two mitigation patterns:
  1. Relax constraint per L-49 semantics (xsd:integer IS a subtype of xsd:decimal      conceptually; use sh:or [xsd:decimal, xsd:integer, ...] to accept both)
  2. Fix data per L-40 honesty (add missing skos:definition to authored individuals)

v17.35.0 applied both. Pattern (1) for 12 numeric mismatches (declared decimal vs actual integer); pattern (2) for 4 missing definitions.

Generalization: Step 2 work for OTHER subjects (reliability, algorithm-efficiency, ORH, quality, amod, risk in future releases) will likewise surface findings. Each upgrade must execute the same pattern: discover violations → classify per L-40 → apply mitigation transparently."""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual .

kb:Lesson_L_71_FrameworkClassExistsZeroPopulation a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-71: framework class with zero population"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-71"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:created "2026-05-19T20:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE Pack v19.1.0 Phase F2 lesson. Discovered when v19.0.3 audit found quality:Framework class present but 0 individuals."@en ;
    skos:definition "L-71: Framework class exists with zero population is an anti-pattern. When an ontology declares a class (e.g., quality:Framework) but the ABox contains 0 individuals of that class, the class is a schema-only declaration that cannot support actual query/SPARQL retrieval. Detection: TBox class C exists; SPARQL count of individuals typed C returns 0; the class is not abstract (i.e., is meant to have instances). Resolution: populate the ABox with verified instances OR mark the class as abstract via documentation OR remove the class. Example resolution per F2: quality:Framework had 0 individuals; populated with 6 BP-D41-verified framework instances (OQuaRE, OntoQA, OOPS!, FAIR, ISO 25010, IEEE 828)."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources .

kb:Lesson_L_81_SunkCostFoundationDrift a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-81: Sunk-cost foundation drift"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-81"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Conversation.txt line 439: 'Stage 06 verified coverage against 174 source concepts — but 174 was self-invented inventory.'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """In multi-stage pipelines, each downstream stage that references an upstream fact treats it as confirmed by virtue of being referenced — even though no independent verification has occurred. The model's instinct is FORWARD-DEFEND: protect prior work rather than re-examine its foundation, because revisiting the foundation invalidates hours of downstream output.

This is a behavioral analog of confirmation bias on artifacts rather than beliefs.

Detection signal: a 'verification' step that consumes a number/list authored upstream in the same session and reports a percentage against it — the denominator is invented but feels confirmed because multiple downstream stages have already referenced it.

Remedy: every multi-stage pipeline includes a FOUNDATION RE-VERIFICATION GATE before the closure stage — re-running the upstream source read independently, comparing to the cached upstream output, and treating any divergence as a STOP, not a footnote.

Distinct from L-58 (single-stage fabricated frame): L-81 names the TEMPORAL/MULTI-STAGE compounding pattern where each stage's reference to the wrong foundation makes it harder to revisit."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_58_PipelineMetricsDoNotMeasureSourceFidelity .

kb:Lesson_L_82_ForeignNamespaceReDeclarationIsAntiPattern a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-82: Re-declaring a foreign-namespace class in another subject's TBox is an anti-pattern that hides accidental self-loops and other inconsistencies"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-82"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-15 in v17.30.0 fitness-for-application assessment when owlready2 warned about cyclic subClassOf. Codified in v17.31.0 along with mechanical detection via Regression_NoSubClassOfSelfLoops."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D26_FormalRegressionSuite,
        kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode: subject A declares owl:Class core:Foo and gives it full metadata (label, definition). Subject B (a different file) ALSO declares owl:Class core:Foo with additional triples (e.g., rdfs:subClassOf assertions). rdflib merges silently — both declarations live in the merged graph. Accidental defects in subject B (such as a self-loop rdfs:subClassOf core:Foo) are tolerated by HermiT (vacuous) but warned by owlready2, Protégé, and any consumer doing recursive subClassOf traversal.

Observation: v17.30.0 fitness assessment discovered core:SeverityLevel had an accidental self-loop subClassOf because reliability_tbox redundantly re-declared the class with additional context-specific triples. owlready2 flagged: "ignoring cyclic subclass of/subproperty of, involving: core:SeverityLevel". HermiT did NOT flag this (vacuous self-loops are consistent), making the defect invisible to BP-D4 gate discipline alone.

Applied fix (v17.31.0): consolidate all core:SeverityLevel triples to core_tbox (single source of truth). reliability_tbox can REFERENCE the IRI (via rdfs:domain, rdfs:range) but must not re-declare with owl:Class type or rdfs:subClassOf assertions.

Procedure (per BP-D26 update): when authoring a SHACL constraint or property in subject B that targets/references core:Foo, do NOT add (core:Foo rdf:type owl:Class). Use the foreign IRI directly. To verify discipline: run the Regression_NoSubClassOfSelfLoops regression test (v17.31.0 catches this category of defect mechanically)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Regression_NoSubClassOfSelfLoops .

kb:Lesson_L_91_StatusProseMustTrackStatusFields a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-91: A governance doc stating implementation-status goes stale when the artifacts it describes advance; reconcile prose to the authoritative status fields"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-91"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "OE-internal, v20.22.2 (2026-06-07): a domain ecosystem flagged that OEE_Component_Compliance_Strategy v1.0.0 §2/§4/§7 said the risk/performance/testing/configuration conformance gates were 'not yet built' — true when authored at pack v20.19.0, but EXT-2 built them at v20.20.0–v20.21.0 and set facetRoleStatus=operational, while the prose was never updated. The submission package was thus self-contradictory (decision letter said gates live; strategy doc said not built). a domain ecosystem correctly followed the disk (BP-D2) and did not edit OEE's doc (B1/L-64). Reconciled in doc v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_90_ValidateTheSuiteThatGovernsTheArtifact ;
    skos:definition "When a prose governance/strategy document records the implementation STATUS of artifacts (e.g. 'gate not yet built', 'partial', 'operational'), that prose duplicates a fact that also lives in an authoritative machine-readable field (here: orh:facetRoleStatus). If later work changes the status field but not the prose, the doc silently goes stale and contradicts the disk. Discipline: (1) the machine-readable field is authoritative (BP-D2 — disk over doc); (2) when work changes a status field, in the SAME change update every prose location that mirrors it; (3) prefer to have status-prose POINT AT the field rather than re-state it. Detection: any doc sentence asserting build-status is a claim that must be re-verified against the field at ship time (extends L-65)."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-06-07T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_64_ScopeBleedViaSourceAttributionNarratives,
        kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Milestone_M3_SelfGoverning> a bl:Milestone,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "M3 — exemplar registered, release history started"@en ;
    bl:hasIdentifier "OEM-M3" ;
    bl:hasTargetDate "2026-08-24T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    bl:partOfRoadmap <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#Roadmap_Deployment> ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/oe-method/backlog> .

kb:BestPractice_D23_InferDomainFromUsage a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D23: Before adding DT properties to raise AttributeRichness, run orphan-detection and usage-inference"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D23"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.26.0 N execution. Applied immediately in same release: 11 inferable orphans across 3 subjects (amod, oe-measurement, algorithm-efficiency) received rdfs:domain assertions via this discipline."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_23_AttributeRichnessDualNature,
        kb:OEPACK_BP4_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Discipline:

1. ORPHAN DETECTION: SPARQL-query all DT properties in subject NS lacking rdfs:domain (orphans). These contribute to OQuaRE AttrR=0 measurement even though they exist semantically.

2. USAGE INFERENCE: for each orphaned DT prop, find subjects of triples using that predicate, look up their rdf:type values. The observed types are candidate rdfs:domain assignments.

3. PRINCIPLED FIX: add rdfs:domain assertion based on observed usage. This is content-honest (the property IS actually used on those instances) and raises AttrR honestly.

4. METRIC GAMING PROHIBITION: fabricating DT properties WITHOUT usage in the ABox to raise AttrR is forbidden per L-40. If an orphan has NO usage in the ABox, leave it orphaned and disclose.

5. DOMAIN-CORRECT AttrR=0: per L-47, some subjects are concept-rich by design and have legitimately zero datatype properties. These should NOT be "fixed" — they should be annotated with skos:scopeNote explaining the design choice."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification .

kb:BestPractice_D26_FormalRegressionSuite a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D26: Encode regression invariants as kb:RegressionTest individuals in the OE ABox; execute via scripts/regression_suite.py before every release"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D26"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.31.0 items #5/#8. The regression suite is the mechanical guard against regression in BP-D7, BP-D24, BP-D25, and other invariants documented across the catalogue. Failure-mode paragraph added at kb_abox v2.20.0 on a registrant proposal, from three independent producers: an adopting project's four incidents (clearest: a dispatcher fix verified entirely in a scratch directory, with a committed-test grep returning zero files at the moment Done was claimed); the proposing session's own git-tag-orphaning finding, which survives only as prose; and OEE's own — the same tag claim reproduced in this pack's session prompt with no re-runnable artifact behind it. Screened before adding: 175 definitions, one hit, a false positive on L-47's IEEE-1633 use of 'transient'. Enriched rather than minted per L-110; the proposing session recommended enrichment and argued against the originating project's own suggestion of L-99, correctly on the verbatim text."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl,
        kb:Lesson_L_82_ForeignNamespaceReDeclarationIsAntiPattern ;
    skos:definition """Industrial-hardening discipline: encode each release-blocking invariant as an kb:RegressionTest individual that declares (a) the SPARQL or external-script check, (b) the expected result, (c) the test type. The companion script scripts/regression_suite.py iterates these individuals, executes them, and exits non-zero on any FAIL — suitable for CI integration.

Initial regression suite (v17.31.0):
  1. Regression_NoSubClassOfSelfLoops (catches L-52-style defects)
  2. Regression_AllOntologiesHaveLicense (enforces BP-D24)
  3. Regression_AllOntologiesHaveVersionInfo (enforces BP-D7)
  4. Regression_AllClassesHaveLabel
  5. Regression_AllClassesHaveDefinition
  6. Regression_OperationalizableCQHasSPARQL (enforces BP-D25)
  7. Regression_PerformanceHasIndividuals (catches stub-class regression)
  8. Regression_EcosystemHermitConsistent
  9. Regression_LessonsCountAtLeast50 (catalogue continuity)
 10. Regression_BestPracticesCountAtLeast30

Adding a regression test requires:
  (a) author the individual in OE ABox (PATCH bump)
  (b) ensure scripts/regression_suite.py can execute its type
  (c) confirm CURRENT state PASSES the new test

Effect in v17.31.0 (per BP-D2): regression suite first run caught a real classification gap (Regression_LessonsCountAtLeast50 SPARQL queried only LessonLearned, not Lesson). Fixed via PATCH; net evidence the suite works as designed.  FAILURE MODE THIS PREVENTS (added v2.20.0): a verification performed but not encoded — run in a scratch directory, in a terminal, or in a session that then ends — proves the property held at that moment and nothing about whether it still holds. Afterwards it is indistinguishable from a verification never performed: the same absence of evidence, with more confidence attached. Encoding the check as a kb:RegressionTest individual is therefore not bookkeeping after the fact; it is the step that converts a moment into a property. DISTINCT FROM the neighbouring rules, each screened verbatim before this was added: L-99 governs a gate that does not exist (prose acceptance criteria with nothing enforcing them), whereas here a gate existed, ran and passed before being discarded; L-95 governs whether a gate can fail at all; L-65 governs whether a claim was grounded at the moment of claiming, which in these incidents it was. Every neighbouring rule governs whether a check is SOUND; this governs whether it PERSISTS."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D24_OntologyLevelLicenseAndProvenance,
        kb:BestPractice_D25_CompetencyQuestionClassification,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:Lesson_L_110_NoNewStructureOnSingleProducerEvidence,
        kb:Lesson_L_47_CrossSubjectSubClassOfSemanticVerification,
        kb:Lesson_L_52_NarrowInterpretationWithoutDisclosure,
        kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing,
        kb:Lesson_L_95_GateMustBeProvenDiscriminating,
        kb:Lesson_L_99_DoDEnforceableOnlyViaMetaGate,
        kb:Regression_AllClassesHaveDefinition,
        kb:Regression_AllClassesHaveLabel,
        kb:Regression_AllOntologiesHaveLicense,
        kb:Regression_AllOntologiesHaveVersionInfo,
        kb:Regression_BestPracticesCountAtLeast30,
        kb:Regression_EcosystemHermitConsistent,
        kb:Regression_LessonsCountAtLeast50,
        kb:Regression_NoSubClassOfSelfLoops,
        kb:Regression_OperationalizableCQHasSPARQL,
        kb:Regression_PerformanceHasIndividuals .

kb:BestPractice_D27_SkeletonShapeDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D27: Auto-generated skeleton SHACL shapes MUST carry kb:isSkeletonShape true and sh:severity sh:Info"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D27"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.32.0 Option B. Triggered by Roadmap_SHACL100Coverage Step 1 execution which added 1151+ skeleton shapes; discipline ensures these remain distinguishable from semantic shapes during Step 2 multi-release work."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D26_FormalRegressionSuite,
        kb:OEPACK_BP4_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """When mechanical SHACL coverage is generated for un-shaped owl:Class declarations (per Roadmap_SHACL100Coverage Step 1), each generated shape MUST:

  1. Declare kb:isSkeletonShape true (boolean marker for scaffold status)
  2. Declare sh:severity sh:Info (advisory only; never block validation)
  3. Carry rdfs:comment explicitly stating "Scaffold-only / Roadmap Step 1 /      L-40 honest disclosure"
  4. Carry dcterms:source attributing to the auto-generation activity

Rationale: a 100% mechanical SHACL coverage figure is meaningful ONLY if consumers can distinguish scaffold from semantic shapes. Without this marker, future Step 2 work (adding meaningful constraints) cannot mechanically identify which shapes still need upgrading.

Detection SPARQL:
  SELECT (COUNT(?s) AS ?nSkeletons) WHERE {
    ?s a sh:NodeShape ; kb:isSkeletonShape true .
  }

Upgrade procedure (Step 2): replace skeleton sh:targetClass-only with rich sh:property constraints; remove kb:isSkeletonShape true triple; bump sh:severity as appropriate; update rdfs:comment to reflect semantic content."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Roadmap_SHACL100Coverage .

kb:BestPractice_D38_InventoryUploadsBeforeReadingAndEstimateContextCost a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D38: Inventory all uploaded files and estimate context cost BEFORE reading any of them"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D38"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source sections: §3 (C-1..C-6) of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:BestPractice_D36_TextFormatFilesOver200KBMustBeZipped,
        kb:Lesson_L_61_AutoLoadingTextRegimeConsumesTokensBeforeFirstMessage ;
    skos:definition """Before reading any uploaded file, Claude must inventory what is present and estimate context cost. Running cat on a 400 KB HTML inside /mnt/user-data/uploads/ after it has already auto-loaded into context doubles the token expenditure. Programmatic slicing avoids this.

Procedure:
  1. Start each session with "ls -la /mnt/user-data/uploads/" to enumerate files      with byte sizes.
  2. For any loose .html/.md/.txt/.csv > 200 KB, surface a context-cost warning to      the user with a token estimate (~4 characters per token rule of thumb).
  3. Recognize that loose text uploads are already in context — do not re-read with      cat/view; reference directly.
  4. For zipped uploads, extract to /home/claude/, then inspect slices via grep,      "sed -n '<start>,<end>p'", or view with view_range. Avoid full-file cat on      anything > 200 KB.
  5. For TTL/OWL ontologies, prefer programmatic queries (rdflib, pyshacl, SPARQL)      over text grep when the question is structural.
  6. If cumulative loose text uploads exceed 100 KB, recommend the user re-upload      as a zip in a fresh session before proceeding.

Anti-pattern prevented: Silent token exhaustion mid-session; reading the same file content twice (once via auto-load, once via explicit cat)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D41: Verify every academic reference against an authoritative source before publishing"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D41"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-19 from session reference-verification audit invoked by the method author per BP-D2. Session-generated bibliographies for a reliability ontology Reliability Pipeline v0.1.1 (41 refs) and Ontology-Engineering Discipline Pipeline v0.1.0 (43 refs) — 60 unique entries after dedup — contained 1 confirmed full hallucination (Wang \"LLM hallucination detection via prompt-induced uncertainty estimation\", ICLR 2025; no such paper) and 4 partial-attribution defects (OntoLogX cited as Bertoldi et al. — actual lead author is Luca Cotti; Liu et al. Trustworthy LLMs cited at ACM Computing Surveys 2024 — actual venue ICLR 2024; Bommasani et al. Foundation Model Transparency Reports cited at FAccT 2025 — actual venue AIES 2024; Westermann et al. arXiv:2506.10678 title truncated). Defect rate: 5/60 = 8.3%. Companion lessons: LessonLearned_35_BibliographyHallucinationPattern and Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:LessonLearned_35_BibliographyHallucinationPattern,
        kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing ;
    skos:definition """LLM-generated bibliographies have a non-trivial hallucination rate at the level of individual reference entries — typically 5–10% defective. Defects include fully-fabricated papers, fabricated lead authors on real papers, wrong publication venues, wrong years, and truncated/altered titles. The defect signature is that the citation is plausible (matches the topic, has author-name-shaped strings and venue-name-shaped strings) but does not correspond to any real publication or misrepresents a real one.

Discipline:

  1. EVERY reference in any deliverable bibliography MUST be verified against an authoritative source before publication. Authoritative source = arXiv abstract page, journal/publisher DOI page, conference proceedings page (ACL Anthology, NeurIPS proceedings, IEEE Xplore, ACM DL, Springer Nature Link), or institutional repository.

  2. The validator's reference-list check (validate_pipeline.py G6) MUST include existence verification — not just count and no-self-reference. Specifically: each reference must be matched to an arXiv ID, DOI, or canonical URL that resolves to the cited title, authors, and venue.

  3. For each reference, verify FOUR fields: (a) title (full, not truncated); (b) lead author surname (LLM-generated bibliographies frequently substitute plausible-but-wrong lead authors onto real papers); (c) venue (LLMs often misattribute real papers to higher-prestige adjacent venues); (d) year.

  4. When a generated reference cannot be verified, REMOVE it rather than retain it as "probably real". A hallucinated reference is a contamination of the bibliography that propagates through citation chains.

  5. Foundational works (Gruber 1993, Vaswani 2017, Lewis 2020, etc.) are not exempt from verification — they're cheap to verify but still must be verified, because the rare error in a foundational citation (wrong volume, wrong page range) is the kind of defect a reader will check first.

  6. When publishing a deliverable that cites references, include a brief disclosure note stating "all N references verified against authoritative sources on YYYY-MM-DD" — this makes the verification an auditable claim, not a hopeful assumption.

This discipline is a direct application of BP-D2 (never trust summaries) to the bibliography substrate."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v18_0_1 .

kb:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "All produced files must conform to AMOD naming conventions on creation"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D6"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "AMOD v1.2.6 OntologyFileConvention; AMOD v1.4.0 markdown extensions; AMOD v1.4.1 ProjectArchive extension; current-session rename of two files."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D6_AmodConformantNaming (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """File naming is verified at creation time, not retrofitted. Three primary conventions cover all OE Pack artifact types:

  1. configuration:OntologyFileConvention — ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*_v\\\\d+_\\\\d+_\\\\d+\\\\.ttl$
     Examples: oqc_measurements_v17_v1_0_2.ttl, meta_v6_5_1.ttl

  2. configuration:ProjectClosureMarkdownConvention (v1.4.0) — capitalised + underscore + 3-part SemVer + _Closure_ + .md
     Examples: Sprint_8_Closure_v1_0_0.md, Phase1_Closure_v1_0_0.md

  3. configuration:AuditReportMarkdownConvention (v1.4.0) — name_v{M}_{m}_{p}.md
     Examples: Parallel_Phase1_2_3_Assessment_v1_0_0.md, oe_ecosystem_review_supplement_v1_0_4.ttl

  4. configuration:ProjectArchiveConvention (v1.4.1, this session) — oepack-{scope}-v{M}_{m}_{p}.zip
     Examples: oepack-parallel-integration-v1_0_1.zip

CRITICAL: the version token must be the LAST segment before the file extension. Suffixes like _addendum, _supplement, _patched MUST appear BEFORE the version token, not after. Two filenames in this study had this defect: Parallel_Phase1_2_3_Assessment_v1_0_1_addendum.md (wrong) → Parallel_Phase1_2_3_Assessment_Addendum_v1_0_1.md (correct).

Verify by regex BEFORE staging in /mnt/user-data/outputs/."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

kb:LessonLearned_35_BibliographyHallucinationPattern a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "LL-35: LLM-generated bibliographies have a measurable hallucination rate — verify don't trust"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-35"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced in session 2026-05-19 during reference-verification audit. The author of this LessonLearned (Claude) generated bibliographies that passed the validator's G6 check (count + no-self-reference) but contained 1 fully-fabricated paper and 4 partial-attribution defects across 60 references. Discovered when user the method author invoked BP-D2 protocol asking Claude to honestly confirm references were real with URLs. Fail-stop disclosed at protocol invocation; web_search verification of every reference followed."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources,
        kb:LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking,
        kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing ;
    skos:definition """When asked to generate academic bibliographies, LLM systems (including this one) produce references at a measured defect rate around 5–10%. The defects cluster in predictable ways:

  1. FULLY-FABRICATED papers: title + author + venue + year combinations that look plausible (match the topic, have right "shape") but do not correspond to any real publication. Example surfaced 2026-05-19: "Wang et al., LLM hallucination detection via prompt-induced uncertainty estimation, ICLR 2025" — no such paper exists; appears to be a conflation of adjacent real works (SPUQ by Gao 2024; Semantic-Invariant Perturbation Sampling by Cox 2025).

  2. AUTHOR FABRICATION on real papers: the paper, title, venue, and year are all real, but the cited lead author is fabricated. Example surfaced 2026-05-19: OntoLogX (arXiv:2510.01409) cited as "M. Bertoldi et al." — actual lead author is Luca Cotti; "Bertoldi" does not appear in the author list at all.

  3. VENUE MISATTRIBUTION: the paper is real, but cited at a higher-prestige adjacent venue. Examples surfaced 2026-05-19: Liu et al. "Trustworthy LLMs" cited at ACM Computing Surveys (actual: ICLR 2024); Bommasani et al. "Foundation Model Transparency Reports" cited at FAccT (actual: AIES 2024).

  4. TITLE TRUNCATION: the paper is real, but cited with a shortened title that misrepresents its scope. Example: Westermann et al. arXiv:2506.10678 cited as "Automated validation of textual constraints via LLMs and SHACL" — actual title includes the domain qualifier "Against AutomationML" that materially changes the paper's scope.

This pattern is parallel to LessonLearned_27_OwlReadyHermitMasking at one level of abstraction: tooling (LLM-as-citation-generator) silently returns success on something it cannot actually verify. The validator's G6 check verified what the validator was designed to check (count + no-self-reference) but did NOT verify what readers would assume it verified (existence of the cited works). The asymmetric verification problem applies: the easy-to-check property passed; the hard-to-check property was not checked at all, and consumers assumed it was.

Operationally: BP-D41 codifies the forward discipline. validate_pipeline.py G6 must be extended with existence verification against arXiv IDs / DOIs / canonical URLs."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v18_0_1 .

kb:Lesson_L_115_CrossSessionHandoverInbox a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-115: Enhancement findings for a package go through a real, structured inbox (pending/accepted/rejected/deferred) - not a package-private doc, not silent absorption"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-115"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Adopted into OE_Operating_Discipline v2.5.0, adapted directly from a registrant package's own real, working 07-handover-inbox/ design (reviewed, not invented from description) after the owner requested it become common OE-wide practice. The discipline's own text names this L-113, colliding with a pre-existing, unrelated lesson under that ID (documentation-coverage drift) - found by a real cross-session finding (vaf-agentic-pipeline, 2026-09-09). Registered here under a free ID, correcting the collision rather than repeating it."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition "Any genuine enhancement finding for a package - from another package's session, or from the package's own work surfacing something about itself - is built as a handover document, not folded silently into a response or buried in a package-private location. The directory: pending/accepted/rejected/deferred subdirectories and a plain-text (not RDF) log recording disposition and the verification actually performed - plain text because this is bookkeeping (has this been looked at, with what outcome), not domain knowledge. pending/ is checked at session start, as a standing ceremony step; nothing arrives automatically, an item is placed by the owner or archived by a session that read it elsewhere. A proposal sent to another package goes in that package's own inbox where one exists, not left in the sender's own documentation for the target to discover by chance."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-09T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_113_PerItemDispositionOnPluralRequests .

kb:Lesson_L_23_AttributeRichnessDualNature a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-23: AttributeRichness=0 has dual nature — measurement artifact (orphaned DT props lacking rdfs:domain) vs domain-correct (concept-rich ontologies)"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-23"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-15 during v17.26.0 Option N investigation. Ecosystem analysis revealed that NS-local and ecosystem-wide AttrR measurements disagreed by up to 0.333 for some subjects. Per L-40 honest disclosure: before this Lesson, simply targeting AttrR > 0 would have driven fabricated DT property additions; investigation revealed the dual nature."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D23_InferDomainFromUsage,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:OEPACK_BP4_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode: a single AttrR=0 reading does not distinguish between (a) genuine sparsity of literal properties (which may be domain-correct for concept-rich ontologies such as core foundations, methodology taxonomies, or test ontologies where structure is encoded via object properties), versus (b) measurement artifact where DT properties exist but lack rdfs:domain assertions and therefore are invisible to OQuaRE's "count DT props with domain X" formula.

Observation: in v17.26.0 N investigation, core showed NS-local AttrR=0 but ecosystem AttrR=0.333 because 5 DT properties (hasConfidence, hasUnit, hasValue, hasTimestamp, severityRank) live in other subjects with rdfs:domain pointing back to core classes. Conversely, algorithm-efficiency showed NS-local AttrR=0.083 but ecosystem AttrR=0.000 because all 10 NS-local DT props lacked rdfs:domain (orphaned).

Applied fix: compute AttrR twice — NS-local (DT props in subject NS) AND ecosystem (DT props with rdfs:domain ∈ subject classes). If they differ significantly, the gap is measurement artifact (per BP-D23). Only when both views show 0 should the subject be considered for content authoring — and even then, fabricating literal properties without usage is metric gaming per L-40."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures .

kb:Lesson_L_45_DiagrammaticDeclarationFalsehood a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-45: Diagrammatic Declaration Falsehood — Visual containment can encode false ontological claims"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-45"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-14 during the F1-redirect categorization discussion. The author drew a \"current state\" diagram placing 11 kb:* IRIs (OntologyArtifact, Mapping, QualityGate, TestSuite, TestingConcept, ChangeOperation, OntologyComponent, CompetencyQuestion, TestCase, Alignment, Evolution) inside a rectangular container labeled \"ontology-engineering\" — visually asserting that OE owned/declared these classes. The user challenged with: \"can you confirm the OE classes will be deleted from OE?\" Per BP-D2 raw inspection of OE v1.4.0 TBox, none of the 11 IRIs were declared as owl:Class anywhere in the active 11-subject corpus — they were ghost IRIs (string-only references with no class declaration behind them). The visual implied a declaration relationship that the underlying triples did not support. Without the user's challenge, the wrong SemVer bump category would have been selected (MAJOR for class-deletion, when in fact the change is PATCH for reference-rewriting)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D20_VerifyDiagramContainmentMatchesTBox,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_38_NamePatternIsHeuristic,
        kb:Lesson_L_42_VerifyBothSidesOfRelationship ;
    skos:definition "Failure mode: A structured diagram that nests named entities inside a labeled container box visually asserts that the container owns, declares, or aggregates those entities. Viewers (including the diagram's author) make subsequent decisions based on the visual implication rather than the raw underlying data. When the visual containment does not match the source-of-truth structure (TBox declarations, database schema, organizational chart, etc.), downstream decisions become systematically wrong in ways that are hard to detect because the diagram \"looks right.\" Scope: applies to any structured diagram where containment is a visual primitive — ontology diagrams (containment implies owl:Class declaration in subject), system architecture diagrams (containment implies process-owns-component), organizational charts (containment implies management hierarchy), file-system trees (containment implies actual directory nesting). Symptom: a question of the form \"will operation X affect container Y's contents?\" gets a wrong answer derived from visual structure rather than raw inspection. Cost: SemVer category errors, deletion-safety errors, consumer-breaking-change misclassification, decision-making on phantom relationships. Mitigation: per BP-D20, raw-verify containment claims against source-of-truth before rendering any structured diagram. Related: L-38 (name-pattern is heuristic; verify by semantic check) extends to the visualization layer — visual nesting is also a heuristic requiring verification. L-42 (verify both endpoints raw) applies symmetrically to diagram containment: verify both the container declaration AND the contained entity's actual membership."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 .

