    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D36_TextFormatFilesOver200KBMustBeZipped,
        kb:BestPractice_D38_InventoryUploadsBeforeReadingAndEstimateContextCost ;
    skos:definition """claude.ai distinguishes auto-loaded text formats (.html, .md, .txt, .csv) from on-demand binary formats (.ttl, .json, .py, .zip, images, PDFs, Office docs). Auto-loaded files enter the context window the moment they are attached — tokens are spent before the first message is processed.

Failure modes this lesson explains:
  - "Conversation too long" firing on the first message despite the user having     sent only a short prompt
  - Apparently-stable sessions exhausting context budget faster than expected due     to multiple text-format attachments
  - Re-reading auto-loaded files with cat/view paying token cost twice

The four auto-loading extensions are exhaustive at time of authoring (2026-05-13, claude.ai web/mobile/desktop). Token math rule of thumb: ~4 characters per token for HTML/Markdown/Turkish prose; 200 KB ≈ 50,000 tokens ≈ 25% of a 200K-window. A 1 MB HTML upload guarantees overflow.

Operationalized by BP-D36 (file-size threshold + zip rule) and BP-D38 (inventory + cost estimate at session start). Out of scope: Claude Code CLI and API direct usage — different context handling."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:Lesson_L_80_ContextAsKnowledgeConfusion a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-80: Context-as-knowledge confusion"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-80"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Conversation.txt line 433: 'Built every claim about OE Pack from user-memory summary, never opened the file.'"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """The model has no internal signal distinguishing 'I read this file this turn' from 'this fact is in my context window' (user-memory, prior-turn summary, system prompt). Both produce equally fluent confident continuation.

Operational consequence: content claims arriving via context (version numbers, file paths, module lists, counts, structural facts about specific artifacts) must be treated as SUSPECT until verified against the actual file this turn. Operational facts (the user's name, role, preferences) inherit forward; content facts about specific files do not.

Detection signal: any content claim sourced from context rather than a tool call made in this turn. Remedy: when a user message names a file or package, the first tool call must be ls / unzip -l / view against it, before any prose response.

Distinct from BP-D2 (which is the RULE 'read raw'); this lesson names WHY the rule gets violated even when cited — the model cannot tell from inside generation whether a fact came from context or from a read."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-28T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v20_2_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries .

<http://example.org/oe-method/backlog#State_Blocked> a bl:LifecycleState,
