        "v1.19.0 (2026-10-12): re-derived after the governing package changed 1 module(s): http://example.org/knowledge-base. Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.2.0 (2026-10-06): re-derived after the governing pack added two lessons to the knowledge base (a measure that reads one value of a multi-valued property reads an arbitrary one; a text substitution of a prefixed name can hit the subject and rename a term) and corrected the recorded cause of two untagged releases. Only the knowledge-base module changed."@en,
        "v1.20.0 (2026-10-12): re-derived after the governing package changed 1 module(s): http://example.org/knowledge-base. Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.21.0 (2026-10-19): the knowledge-base module is now 2.56.0 (best practice BP-D56, the connectedness of every ontology file). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.22.0 (2026-10-19): the knowledge-base module is now 2.57.0 (best practice BP-D57, the missing links of every ontology file). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.37.0 (2026-10-22): the knowledge-base module is now 2.72.0 (a role is a possible end of a characterisation link, on the owner's ruling of 2026-10-22 that Role is a real role)."@en,
        "v1.36.0 (2026-10-21): the knowledge-base module is now 2.71.0 (two class kinds, behavioural pattern and method, on the owner's ruling of 2026-10-21)."@en,
        "v1.35.0 (2026-10-21): the knowledge-base module is now 2.70.0 (the kind rules of the taxonomy of relationships reviewed against the sources: participation and membership hold at either end, causal links join continuants too, provenance includes conceptual entities, attribution may end in a value domain, and a kind rule for the category of measurement and assessment; two literature sources; research record v1.10.0). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.34.0 (2026-10-21): the knowledge-base module is now 2.69.0 (a category for measurement and assessment, with two literature sources; research record v1.9.0). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.33.0 (2026-10-21): the knowledge-base module is now 2.68.0 (the remaining duplicates merged, the homonyms given sense-specific labels; research record v1.8.0). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.32.0 (2026-10-21): the knowledge-base module is now 2.67.0 (the duplicate detector described in BP-D61). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.31.0 (2026-10-21): the knowledge-base module is now 2.66.0 (best practice BP-D61: one meaning, one term; BP-D60 restated). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.30.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.65.0 (BP-D60 corrected: homonyms were found among the pairs read). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.29.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.64.0 (best practice BP-D60 and four lexical categories: synonymy, near-synonymy, homonymy, polysemy, under lexical variant, with 8 further sources). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.28.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.63.0 (best practice BP-D59: the limits state that kinds and categories are now recorded on 48 classes and 26 properties of the method vocabulary). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.27.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.62.0 (best practice BP-D59 after a third research round across law, data standards, mathematics and software engineering, and a test on the method's own classes: 84 kinds, 14 facets, 20 kind rules, 36 further literature sources). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.26.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.61.0 (best practice BP-D59 extended after a second research round across physics, chemistry, biology, medicine and philosophy: 62 kinds, 14 facets with 45 values, 24 further literature sources; mobile or immobile dropped). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.25.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.60.0 (best practice BP-D59, the taxonomy of class kinds: 45 kinds, 11 facets with 27 values, 14 kind rules joined to the relationship categories, 12 more literature sources, and a ninth construction step, the kind of each end). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.24.0 (2026-10-20): the knowledge-base module is now 2.59.0 (best practice BP-D58, the construction of relationships, with the taxonomy of relationships: 28 categories, 2 planes, 7 facets, 33 literature sources and 8 construction steps). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.23.0 (2026-10-19): the knowledge-base module is now 2.58.0 (the references that the texts of its entries make to other entries are links: 233 rdfs:seeAlso statements, BP-D57). Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.3.0 (2026-10-06): re-derived after the governing pack added L-135 (filing into another package's inbox must not write a file that package's manifest covers) and L-136 (two modules declaring one term leave two definitions in one graph, and the term's namespace names which one governs) to the knowledge base, which is now module 2.39.0."@en,
        "v1.4.0 (2026-10-07): re-derived after the governing pack added L-137 (a debt line whose package is not in the scan was not measured, and reporting it CLEARED deletes another package's debt) and L-138 (the same repository measures under two package names, and a shared register can only carry the one its publisher stages) to the knowledge base, which is now module 2.40.0. Only that module changed."@en,
        "v1.5.0 (2026-10-07): re-derived after the governing pack recorded the owner's ruling that each package carries its own structure-debt register (a change note on BP-D54) and added L-139 (a shared record that only one party may edit makes the rule it enforces impossible for everyone else to obey) and L-140 (a version token belongs to the file name, not to the content that moved into the file); the knowledge-base module is 2.41.0. The OE Method backlog and history modules are unchanged."@en,
        "v1.6.0 (2026-10-07): re-derived after the governing pack recorded the owner's ruling on examples (outside the domain ontologies' coverage; each may be a unit of its own) as a second change note on BP-D54 and added L-141 (before a file is renamed or repurposed, find every reader of its old name, including the readers that only report); the knowledge-base module is 2.42.0. The OE Method backlog and history modules are unchanged."@en,
        "v1.7.0 (2026-10-07): re-derived after the governing pack added L-142 (an action taken once, at the event, with no later pass over what it missed, turns one refusal into a permanent gap); the knowledge-base module is 2.43.0. Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.8.0 (2026-10-07): re-derived after the governing pack recorded the owner's ruling on registrations (the individual files made to register a package are consolidated into one unit of three files, each registrant a module) as a third change note on BP-D54; the knowledge-base module is 2.44.0. Nothing else changed."@en,
        "v1.9.0 (2026-10-07): re-derived after the governing pack added L-143 (a union of two files does not drop the copies of an anonymous node, so a merge keeps both); the knowledge-base module is 2.45.0. Nothing else changed."@en .

kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D28: Instance authoring for DT-Use coverage MUST cite real source provenance and mark with kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D28"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.33.0 Option E. Triggered by need to mechanically distinguish OE-Pack-self-instances from user-authored instances during Step 2 and Step 3 work."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D27_SkeletonShapeDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_25_DesignIntentExtractionIsNotFabrication,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """When DT-Use coverage gaps are closed via instance authoring (per L-49 design-intent extraction), every authored individual MUST:

  1. Carry dcterms:source with prose attributing the FACTUAL SOURCE — typically      OE Pack release history (file SHAs, version numbers, sprint goals), real      benchmark measurements (HermiT/SHACL captures), textbook computer-science      facts (O/Ω/Θ formal definitions, paging competitive ratios), or the method author's      LLM reliability research (a reliability ontology benchmark, GraphRAG configurations).

  2. Carry kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true — boolean marker enabling      mechanical distinction between OE-Pack-self-instances and application-     domain instances introduced by end users.

  3. NOT use synthetic placeholders (per L-40). If a DT property has no      plausible real-world instance, the property is a candidate for owl:deprecated      (Step 3 / Option G) rather than synthetic ABox creation.

  4. NOT use exponent notation for xsd:decimal literals (per L-53 new). Use      Literal("0.000003", datatype=XSD.decimal) lexical-form preservation.

Detection SPARQL (count of authored instances):

  SELECT (COUNT(?i) AS ?n) WHERE { ?i kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true . }

v17.33.0 first execution: 76 instances added across 8 subjects (amod, risk, reliability, quality, oe-measurement, algorithm-efficiency, ontology-engineering, oepack-release-history). DT-Use rate: 57.4% → 97.4% (109/190 → 186/191)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_49_CoEqualOptionsMustBeActuallyCoEqual,
        kb:Lesson_L_53_RdflibDecimalExponentNotation .

kb:BestPractice_D30_SemanticShapeUpgrade a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D30: SHACL Step 2 semantic shape upgrade per subject — replace skeleton sh:targetClass-only shapes with sh:property constraints sourced from rdfs:domain/range declarations"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D30"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified 2026-05-15 in v17.35.0 Option C. Defines the multi-release Step 2 discipline."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D27_SkeletonShapeDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_55_SemanticSHACLUpgradeRevealsHiddenFindings ;
    skos:definition """Discipline for converting v17.32.0 skeleton shapes (mechanical 100% coverage, kb:isSkeletonShape true) into semantic shapes (real validation logic):

  1. For each subject, identify property-bearing classes (those with at least one      DT or OP property declaring them via rdfs:domain).
  2. For each property-bearing class: generate sh:property constraints per declared      property:
     - sh:path = property IRI
     - For DT properties: sh:datatype from rdfs:range (with sh:or [decimal, integer,        double, float, long, nonNegativeInteger] for numeric ranges per L-55)
     - For OP properties: sh:class from rdfs:range (where range is a class)
     - sh:minCount 0 (presence optional; BP-D28 honest disclosure of incremental ABox)
     - sh:maxCount 1 (single-valued default; override for multi-valued)
     - sh:severity sh:Warning explicit at property level (forces propagation)
  3. For each structural-only class (no direct rdfs:domain assertions): lightweight      upgrade with sh:nodeKind sh:IRI. Property constraints inherit from parent class      shape via SHACL subClassOf entailment.
  4. Remove kb:isSkeletonShape true marker on all upgraded shapes.
  5. Run pyshacl on subject standalone; classify any new violations per L-40:
     - True schema-data mismatch (rare): fix ABox
     - Datatype-subtype semantics (xsd:integer ⊑ xsd:decimal): relax via sh:or
     - Missing required annotation: fix ABox (typically skos:definition)
  6. Add subject-specific RegressionTest: ASK { ?s a sh:NodeShape ; sh:targetClass ?c ;      kb:isSkeletonShape true . FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?c), "<subject_ns>")) } expected      false.

Effect of v17.35.0 (oe-measurement, first subject):
  - 54 skeleton shapes upgraded: 8 full-semantic + 46 lightweight
  - 16 hidden findings surfaced + fixed (per L-55)
  - 0 oe-measurement skeletons remain (locked by new regression test)

Priority order for subsequent releases (per DT-Use rate; all now 100%):
  v17.36.0: reliability (29 DT props, ~320 classes — large)
  v17.37.0: algorithm-efficiency (10 DT props, 120 classes)
  v17.38.0: oepack-release-history + performance (small)
  v17.39.0: amod + risk (substantial)
  v17.40.0: quality (modest; 5 DEFER-TO-G)
  v17.41.0: core, meta, sto (no DT props but many classes; nodeKind-only)
  v17.42.0: ontology-engineering (largest; 240 classes)"""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D28_InstanceAuthoringDiscipline,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures .

kb:BestPractice_D31_PrimarySourceConceptCoverageGate a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D31: Primary-source concept-coverage gate — verify the page demonstrates ≥80% of source-document-named concepts before claiming source faithfulness"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D31"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Authored by the method author 2026-05-16 in a parallel course-pipeline development session. Proposed for absorption into OE Pack catalogue via a formal proposal document; integrated in OE Pack v17.44.0 by the OE Pack canonical-numbering session. Renumbering per collision check against v17.43.0. Anti-pattern: pipeline metrics score 93.5/100 while 30% of source concepts absent. Pairs with L-58 (intrinsic metrics blindness)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D26_FormalRegressionSuite,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_58_PipelineMetricsDoNotMeasureSourceFidelity ;
    skos:definition """When the artefact pipeline has a single authoritative primary source (textbook chapter, regulatory document, customer brief), an additional gate beyond intrinsic-property metrics is required: primary-source-concept-coverage.

Source incident: COM0463 Ch.5 v1.1.0 scored 93.5/100 on all pipeline metrics (interactivity, JS LOC, voice gate, SHACL conformance) but covered only ~37% of Marchewka 2016 Ch.5 named concepts. Composite score did not detect 15+ missing concepts (DSC, UCD, SOW, Scope Grope/Leap, Validate Scope 5 elements, Milestone 4 functions, WBS Guidelines, Death March, Guesstimating, Time Boxing, Top-Down, 17-day worked example).

Procedure:
  1. Enumerate primary-source-named concepts: heading terms, defined terms, figure      captions, enumerated lists (e.g., "six estimation methods").
  2. Build source-concept inventory file (15-50 entries typical for a textbook chapter).
  3. For each concept verify ontology presence (a class or NamedIndividual exists) AND      page presence (a widget, table cell, or named heading demonstrates it).
  4. Compute coverage = (concepts at both levels) / total source concepts.
  5. Threshold: ≥80% required before claiming source faithfulness.

Anti-pattern prevented: pipeline scoring 93.5/100 while omitting 30% of chapter concepts."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_44_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D36_TextFormatFilesOver200KBMustBeZipped a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D36: Text-format upload files exceeding 200 KB MUST be zipped before attaching to claude.ai"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D36"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Codified in OE Pack v17.45.0 from upload_hygiene_checklist_v1_1_0.md (the method author, 2026-05-07 issued / 2026-05-13 updated). Underlying claude.ai operational observations authored by the method author in user-facing context. Source sections: §2.1 (auto-load table) and §2.2 rule 1 of v1.1.0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D37_StableGovernanceArtifactsInProjectKnowledgeBase,
        kb:Lesson_L_61_AutoLoadingTextRegimeConsumesTokensBeforeFirstMessage,
        kb:Lesson_L_62_SameFileLooseAndInZipMeansBothCounted ;
    skos:definition """claude.ai's auto-load regime ingests .html, .md, .txt, and .csv files into the context window the moment they are attached, consuming tokens before the first message is processed. A single 963 KB HTML upload alone exceeds the 200K-token Pro/Max context. Binary types (.ttl, .json, .py, .pdf, .png, .zip, .docx, .xlsx, .pptx) are read on demand and do not auto-load.

Procedure:
  1. Before attaching any .html/.md/.txt/.csv file, check its size.
  2. If size > 200 KB, wrap in .zip (e.g.,      "zip -j artifact_bundle_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M).zip *.html *.md").
  3. Attach only the .zip. Do NOT also attach the loose original (see L-62).
  4. Binary formats need no wrapping — they are read on demand.

Anti-pattern prevented: "Conversation too long" firing on the first message due to a single large text upload occupying the entire context budget."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-17T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_45_0 .

kb:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Run both HermiT and SHACL — neither alone is a release gate"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D4"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "L-32 derivation from D-PHASE2-2 root cause analysis; Parallel_Phase1_2_3_Assessment_Addendum_v1_0_1 §1.1."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D4_BothHermitAndShacl (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """HermiT (logical consistency) and SHACL (structural conformance) test orthogonal properties. An ontology can be HermiT-CONSISTENT yet ship SHACL-detectable structural defects (D-PHASE2-2 demonstrates: qc:hasScore Metric-domain inferred Assessment⊑Metric, no contradiction but 60+ SHACL violations). An ontology can be SHACL-CONFORMANT yet HermiT-INCONSISTENT (D-PHASE2-1: appliesMeasure used per the shape's expectation, but with a domain conflict no shape was authoring against).

Release-gate ordering:
  Gate A: SHA-256 manifest integrity
  Gate B: HermiT consistency (direct Java per BP-D3)
  Gate C: SHACL conformance (full v17 shape suite, with imports merged per BP-D5)
  Gate D: Version semantics (BP-3 conformance per BP-D7)

Never downgrade a Gate-C SHACL violation just because Gate B passes. Never downgrade a Gate B HermiT inconsistency just because Gate C passes. ALL gates mandatory for release."""@en ;
    skos:related <http://example.org/oe-ecosystem-review#LessonLearned_32_HermiTNotSHACL> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D3_DirectJavaHermiT,
        kb:BestPractice_D5_ValidateWithImportsMerged,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics,
        kb:LessonLearned_32_HermiTNotSHACL,
        kb:OEPACK_BP3_PatchSemantics .

kb:Lesson_L_139_ASharedRecordOnlyOnePartyMayEditMakesItsOwnRuleUnobeyable a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-139: A shared record that only one party may edit makes the rule it enforces impossible for everyone else to obey"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-139"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Measured 2026-10-07 after the owner asked why another package's structure debt was recorded inside this one. The practice said the owning session clears its own register lines; the register was one file in this package, and the boundary that forbids editing another session's artifacts made clearing a line impossible for the 264 of 268 lines that belonged elsewhere. Two sessions had already had to ask this session to edit their lines, and one of those requests arrived only because an error here had blocked their release. No rule, ruling or change note had ever placed the register here: it was a default chosen when the register was created, plus one hard-coded path in the publisher. The owner ruled that each package carries its own register, with a generated aggregate keeping the ceilings."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_137_ARegisterLineForAPackageOutsideTheScanIsNotMeasuredNotCleared ;
    skos:definition "When a rule names a party as responsible for maintaining something, that party must be able to reach it: a record held where only one session may write turns everyone else's responsibility into a request, and the holder into a bottleneck whose mistakes land on others as blocked work. Check, for every shared artifact, whether the party the rules hold responsible can actually change it, and whose permission each kind of change needs. Where the record must stay shared for a reason -- here, so that nobody raises their own ceiling -- split it along that reason: the part that may only shrink goes to the party who shrinks it, and the part that may only grow with a ruling stays with whoever records rulings. Detection signal: a governance file whose edit history is almost entirely one session acting on behalf of others, or a rule about responsibility that the party it names never exercises."@en .

kb:Lesson_L_25_DesignIntentExtractionIsNotFabrication a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-25: When a property's domain is authored in skos:definition / rdfs:comment, machine-extraction to rdfs:domain is principled — not fabrication"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-25"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-15 in v17.27.0 Challenge-2 response to \"Can't we solve [unused orphans] without fabrication?\" — answered yes for 13 of 15 cases via design-intent extraction from existing skos:definition authored by original developer."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D23_InferDomainFromUsage,
        kb:Lesson_L_23_AttributeRichnessDualNature,
        kb:Lesson_L_24_DomainAdditionTriggersDisjointnessClash,
        kb:OEPACK_BP4_HonestDisclosures ;
    skos:definition """Distinction: per L-40 metric gaming is forbidden — adding rdfs:domain assertions WITHOUT supporting evidence is gaming. But many "orphaned" datatype properties carry their intended domain in their existing skos:definition text, authored by the original ontology developer. Examples surfaced in v17.27.0 N+Q follow-up:

  - reliability:RPN skos:definition: "Risk Priority Number ... Used to prioritise failure modes for mitigation." → rdfs:domain risk:FailureMode (LITERAL design intent).

  - configuration:estimatedHours skos:definition: "Estimated effort in hours for any PlanArtefact (PlanProposal aggregate, Track, ProposedSprint, ProposedDeliverable)." → rdfs:domain configuration:PlanArtefact (LITERAL design intent).

Procedure (extension of BP-D23): when ABox usage is absent, before leaving a property orphaned, INSPECT its skos:definition / rdfs:comment for domain hints. If the hint refers to an existing class, use it as rdfs:domain with attribution dcterms:source = "design intent per skos:definition". This is content-honest because the author already asserted the intent in prose; machine-readability is the only addition.

Effect in v17.27.0: 13 of 15 previously-orphaned DT properties received principled rdfs:domain via this method. 2 reverted per L-50 (no LCA). DatatypePropertyDomainCompleteness moved 92-100% across 4 subjects."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_50_InterrogativeInflation .

kb:Lesson_L_46_RdflibPrefixRebindingMasks_RawIRIs a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-46: rdflib serialization may rebind prefixes — interpret SHACL paths by full IRI, not display prefix"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-46"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced 2026-05-15 during L' (v17.23.0) reliability SHACL remediation. Author chased a phantom \"redo: namespace mismatch\" for multiple tool turns before discovering that rdflib rebound the redo: prefix to point at the reliability# namespace during serialization. Per L-40 honest disclosure: cost ~4 tool turns and partial Fix-5/Fix-6 zero-counts. Recovery via rdflib triple-level inspection ignoring prefix displays."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D22_PreservePrefixesOnSerialization,
        kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:OEPACK_Lesson_L18 ;
    skos:definition """Failure mode: rdflib Graph.serialize() may rebind a prefix (e.g., redo:) to a namespace different from the file's original @prefix. Consumers who read the serialized file see redo:detectedBy but the underlying IRI is actually http://example.org/reliability#detectedBy. SHACL reports displayed by pyshacl use ITS prefix table, not the file's. 

Observation: in L' (v17.23.0) execution, the reliability_shacl.ttl displayed "sh:path redo:detectedBy" but parsing revealed the actual IRI was http://example.org/reliability#detectedBy, not http://example.org/redo#detectedBy. Wasted 4-5 tool turns chasing the wrong namespace before raw-IRI inspection revealed the truth. 

Applied fix: when classifying SHACL findings or patching property shapes, always print the full IRI of sh:path values via rdflib triples, never trust the prefix-rendered text. Per L-18 + BP-D2, the rdflib view IS the raw view; the SHACL report is a presentation layer that can rebind prefixes for readability."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures .

kb:Lesson_L_65_VerifyBeforePublishing a kb:LessonsLearned,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-65: Verify before publishing — confidence at generation time is not evidence"@en ;
    kb:hasLessonId "L-65"^^xsd:string ;
    kb:isAuthoredFromReleaseHistory true ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "Surfaced in session 2026-05-19 during reference-verification audit invoked by the method author per BP-D2."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D2_NeverTrustSummaries,
        kb:BestPractice_D41_VerifyReferencesAgainstAuthoritativeSources,
        kb:LessonLearned_35_BibliographyHallucinationPattern ;
    skos:definition """When Claude (or any LLM-based author) generates content that includes externally-verifiable claims — bibliography entries, code package versions, SHA hashes, statistic citations, quoted definitions — confidence at generation time is not evidence of correctness. The plausibility of a generated string is not a substitute for verifying it against the source.

Anti-pattern this prevents: shipping a deliverable with externally-verifiable claims that were never externally verified, then declaring "all gates PASS" because the gates verified internal consistency (count, formatting, no-self-reference) but not external truth.

Operational form (apply before submitting any deliverable that contains externally-verifiable claims):

  1. ENUMERATE the externally-verifiable claims in the deliverable. For a bibliography this is each reference; for a code package it is each version pin; for a SHA-anchored manifest it is each hash; for a quoted definition it is each quote-source pair.

  2. For each enumerated claim, retrieve the authoritative source and verify the claim against it. "Retrieve" means web_fetch / web_search / file inspection, not reasoning from training data.

  3. Record the verification: in a manifest, in a disclosure block, in a structured log. The verification record makes the claim auditable rather than assumed.

  4. If a claim cannot be verified, REMOVE it rather than retain it as "probably real". A claim retained without verification is a contamination that propagates downstream.

This lesson is distinct from L-40 (observations vs decisions in disclosures) and L-52 (narrow interpretation without disclosure) but is closely related: all three address the asymmetric-verification failure mode where one half of the claim (internal consistency / decision-vs-observation status / framework choice) is well-handled and the other half (external truth / undisclosed alternatives / silent narrowing) is silently dropped.

Extension clause (formerly proposed as L-82 Fluency-Ignorance Equivalence; merged 2026-05-28): the *internal* generation signal does not scale with ignorance. The model produces equally fluent and structurally-confident text whether the content is grounded in a read source or hallucinated from training-pattern matching. The ABSENCE of hedging language in the model's own output is NOT evidence of knowledge — it is the default for both verified and fabricated content. Operational consequence: every specific identifier (version, count, IRI, class name, SHA, citation) must trace to a citation by file-path-and-line, not by paragraph confidence. If a claim cannot be cited, it cannot be written. 

Extension clause (added 2026-06-04): the verifiable-claim rule also covers ANALYTICAL CHARACTERIZATIONS that ground a recommendation or disposition — e.g. 'class X already fills role Y', 'artifact Z is the right home for this record'. Such a characterization is an externally-verifiable claim (checkable by querying the artifact) and must be verified BEFORE it is stated, not asserted-then-offered-for-verification. The tell: writing a structural claim that drives a decision and appending 'I can verify this if you like' — the verification must precede the claim. OE-internal instances 2026-06-04: (1) asserted kb:KnowledgeBaseConcept 'fills the collection role' of the old KnowledgeBase before querying it (it is a taxonomic root, not a collection — the query disconfirmed the claim); (2) proposed kb:LeaderDecision as the home for a closure record before checking it (it is a process-type class with 0 instances — wrong vehicle). Both were decision-driving characterizations stated ahead of the check."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v18_0_1 ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_40_ObservationsVsDecisionsInDisclosures,
        kb:Lesson_L_52_NarrowInterpretationWithoutDisclosure,
        kb:Lesson_L_82_ForeignNamespaceReDeclarationIsAntiPattern .

