kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D55: A release runs on ONE short path, one command that runs every check once and in order with a time limit per step; a run is shortened by removing the hand-work around the checks, never a check"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D55"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's direction of 2026-10-07, after seeing that parallel sessions fall into long-running states: 'update the OEE discipline under the new rulings and enforce the application of the OEE rules in a concise way ... maybe we can help them for short-cuts but keeping the standards', and, on the proposal, 'I want you to include preventive rulings and enforcements against long runs, as you have the same experiences'. The experiences are the governing session's own, listed in L-146 to L-149: a refusal retried unchanged, a step rebuilt by hand each release, a defect found only at the end, a wait recorded without looking."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D54_OneTBoxOneABoxOneShaclPerPackage,
        kb:Lesson_L_146_ARefusalThatComesTwiceUnchangedMeansTheCauseIsNotYetKnown,
        kb:Lesson_L_147_AStepDoneByHandTwiceIsAToolThatDoesNotExistYet,
        kb:Lesson_L_148_TheCostOfARefusalGrowsWithHowLateItComes,
        kb:Lesson_L_149_AWaitRecordedWithoutLookingIsAManufacturedWait,
        kb:Lesson_L_150_ACheckThatTakesMinutesRunsOncePerSetOfBytes ;
    skos:definition "A release is made with oe_release (repo-tooling): the package's own gates, the manifest (oe_manifest: bytecode and the record removed, the file written and verified both ways), the publisher's dry-run, and with --publish the real publish followed by a wait, inside the same command, for the CI run and the release tag. Each step has a time limit and its duration is reported. A refusal prints the command that clears it; a deletion refusal becomes a list in a file for the session to read and accept by naming it, never an acceptance made for it; the same refusal twice stops the command until a cause is written (L-146). The derived package is brought up to its sources by oe_derive_method, which reads its own LINEAGE rows and runs the lineage gate; consolidated modules it reports and never edits. Nothing here removes or loosens a check: the publisher still refuses everything it refused. The discipline's run-length rules R1 to R9 say which of them have a gate and which are advice; R9 (L-150) says a check that takes minutes runs once per set of bytes: the publisher reuses the dry-run's gate pass through a receipt bound to those bytes, and a package may cache its own validation steps with oe_gate_cache. Detection signal: a release assembled from a private script, a manifest written by anything but oe_manifest, a dry-run run once at the end."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-07T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D56_EveryClassIsLinkedAndTheClassesOfAFileFormOneModel a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D56: Every named class of an ontology file is linked to at least one other class of the same file, and the classes of a file form one connected model; a class that nothing refers to, or a group cut off from the rest, is a sign of an incorrect build"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D56"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's ruling of 2026-10-19, on a finding in a registrant package where a class was referred to by nothing because the model cited it as text: 'all ontology files should have a connectedness measure (every class connected to at least one other); unconnected classes indicate an incorrect build', and 'we need to set the connectedness rules for all OEE governed ontologies to ensure that the classes are properly connected against possible semantic loss and missing connections'. The measure and a first script came from the registrant's handover of 2026-10-19."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D54_OneTBoxOneABoxOneShaclPerPackage,
        kb:BestPractice_D55_OneShortReleasePathThatKeepsEveryCheck ;
    skos:definition "The connectedness of an ontology is measured per T-Box file (the highest version of each; fixtures, archives and copies of other packages are not read). A link between two named classes of the file is a subclass or equivalence statement, a restriction whose filler is the class (someValuesFrom, allValuesFrom, onClass, also inside an intersection or a union), or an object property whose domain names one class and whose range names the other. Disjointness, complement and datatype properties are not links. Two numbers are read: the classes standing alone (no link at all) and the islands (separate groups of linked classes beside the largest one). The rule is zero of each. A class whose only links lead outside the file (another vocabulary, a class declared in a sibling file) is connected outward only: it is disclosed and does not fail. The fix for a failure is the missing relation, found by asking what the class is for; a relation added only to clear the number is not a fix, and a class that has no relation to anything is removed or its meaning is stated by the relation it lacks. The existing files that already stand outside the rule are held by a baseline of recorded counts (oe-pack, CONNECTEDNESS_BASELINE): a file may not exceed its recorded counts, a file not recorded must have none, and a lower count is recorded at the next release of the baseline. Enforcement: the check oe_connectedness (repo-tooling, shipped in the method edition), run by the publisher before the push (growth refuses) and by CI Gate C on the whole tree. Limit: the check sees that a link is present, not that it is meaningful."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D57_MissingLinksAreDiscoveredDecidedAndSetNeverLeftAsText a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D57: A relationship that a text states, the data uses or the history implies must be carried by the model as a link; such relationships are discovered in three families, syntactic, semantic and temporal, given a trust level from all their evidence, and set, declared or rejected with a reason by a ceremony"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D57"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's request of 2026-10-19, after the connectedness rule was applied to the OEE's own files and showed relations that were used or accepted but not linked: 'discover the missing relationships, that are somehow used or accepted but physically not linked syntactic, semantic, and temporal relationships. We need to set a rule on this subject as well with a ceremony to discover and appropriately set them. You can use VAF discovery conventions if it helps.' The owner's standing rules of 2026-10-19: connections are always links, never text; a reference to a modelled thing is an object link, not a string. The VAF discovery principle of 2026-10-06: no source is exceptional, findings from all sources are consolidated into one trust level per entity, and the corpus is read as a whole, not as islands."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D56_EveryClassIsLinkedAndTheClassesOfAFileFormOneModel,
        kb:BestPractice_D54_OneTBoxOneABoxOneShaclPerPackage,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics ;
    skos:definition """A file set is the highest T-Box and the highest A-Box of one stem, read as one graph. Four families of missing link are found in it (the fourth, duplicates, is described in BP-D61). Syntactic: a text (definition, comment, example, note, source) names another modelled thing by its identifier, IRI name or label and no link joins the two; a term of the set's own namespaces is used as predicate or type and is declared in no file set of the tree. Semantic: an object property is used between typed things in the data and declares no domain or no range; or declares neither and its own definition names the classes it relates. Temporal: a text says one thing supersedes, replaces or is replaced by another named thing and no link joins them; a change note names a version that the ontology's owl:priorVersion chain does not carry.

Each candidate has a trust level consolidated from all its evidence. High is a deterministic finding, a use in the data, a name by identifier, two kinds of text naming it, or both things naming each other; medium and low are one weaker sign. Only high counts against the record.

The ceremony runs in the same step as the work, in this order: discover (the tool lists the candidates with their evidence); decide each high-trust candidate from the files (set the link, declare the term, state the domain and range, or reject it with a reason); set it as a triple, never as text, in a new version of the file (BP-D7); verify by running the tool again; lower the record in the same release, with each rejection and its reason in the ledger. A new set must have none; an existing set is held by a baseline that may only fall. Enforcement: the publisher (step 4b2), which judges only the staged package against a copy of the governance clone, and CI Gate E on the whole tree. Limit: the tool finds that a relation is probably missing and where; that setting it is right is the ceremony's judgment."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-19T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Strict BP-3 version semantics with versionInfo and versionIRI alignment"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D7"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "BP-3 (canonical); BP-D7 emerges from this session's discipline-encoding work; verified against all PATCH artifacts produced this session."@en,
        "Migrated from rev:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics (catalogue PATCH chain v1.0.5..v1.0.7.13). (See pre-migration source statement(s) above.)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    skos:definition """Every ontology release follows BP-3 SemVer:
  - PATCH (x.y.z → x.y.z.1): bug fixes, no vocabulary change. Always preferred for fix-only patches over MINOR bumps.
  - MINOR (x.y.0 → x.(y+1).0): backwards-compatible vocabulary additions (new classes, properties, individuals).
  - MAJOR (x.0.0 → (x+1).0.0): breaking changes (removed/renamed classes, changed disjointness, etc.).

For each release, ensure all of these align:
  - owl:versionInfo (string literal): '1.4.1'
  - owl:versionIRI (URI): http://.../{ontology}/1.4.1
  - owl:priorVersion (URI): http://.../{predecessor}/{predecessor-version}
  - dcterms:modified (date): the release date

A common defect (caught in this session): versionInfo says '1.4.1' but versionIRI ends in /1.0.0 — these MUST match. The release_check.sh Gate D should verify alignment.

Examples of BP-3 application:
  - I-17 patches: PATCH (v6.5.0 → v6.5.1, removed conflicting axioms, no vocabulary change)
  - amod_v1_4_1_archive_extension: MINOR (v1.4.0 → v1.4.1, new ProjectArchive class)
  - oqc_measurements v1.0.0 → v1.0.1: PATCH (D-PHASE2-1 fix only)
  - oqc_measurements v1.0.1 → v1.0.2: PATCH (D-PHASE2-2 fix only)"""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-05-15T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy oe-prov:Activity_OEPackRelease_v17_33_0 ;
