kb:BestPractice_D58_ARelationshipIsConstructedFromANeedAndACategoryNotImprovised a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D58: A relationship is constructed from a need and a category of the taxonomy, not improvised; it reuses a standard relation where one exists, follows a design pattern where one fits, is declared with domain, range and characteristics, carries every rule as a link, and is test driven when its grouping is unsure"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D58"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's request of 2026-10-20 (a systematic method to construct relationships and a taxonomy of them, from the widest literature) and the literature research of that date, recorded in RELATIONSHIP_TAXONOMY_RESEARCH_v1_11_0.md. The owner asked on 2026-10-20 whether the OEE has a systematic way to construct links; the answer was that it had one to detect and repair them, not to construct them."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D56_EveryClassIsLinkedAndTheClassesOfAFileFormOneModel,
        kb:BestPractice_D57_MissingLinksAreDiscoveredDecidedAndSetNeverLeftAsText,
        kb:BestPractice_D54_OneTBoxOneABoxOneShaclPerPackage,
        kb:BestPractice_D7_StrictBP3VersionSemantics ;
    kb:hasTaxonomyEntry kb:RelationshipCategory_Syntactic,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_ReferenceByName,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_LexicalVariant,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_GrammaticalDependency,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Structural,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_ContainmentMembership,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_OrderingSequence,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Reification,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Connectedness,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Semantic,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Taxonomic,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Compositional,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Spatial,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Characterization,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Participation,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Causal,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Intentional,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Comparative,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Association,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Temporal,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_TemporalOrder,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_SuccessionTransformation,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Versioning,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Provenance,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Correspondence,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Epistemic,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Assessment,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Normative,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Requirement,
        kb:RelationshipPlane_Representation,
        kb:RelationshipPlane_Domain,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_Plane,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_Level,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_FormalMaterial,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_Arity,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_Logical,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_TimeIndex,
        kb:RelationshipFacet_Universality ;
    kb:hasStep kb:ConstructionStep_Need,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Category,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Kinds,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Reuse,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Pattern,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Declare,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Links,
        kb:ConstructionStep_TestDrive,
        kb:ConstructionStep_Check ;
    skos:definition """A relationship is constructed, not improvised. Before a relationship is added to a model it passes nine steps, in this order, in the same step as the work:
  1. State the need: Which competency question needs this relation, and which two things does it join?
  2. Choose the category: Which category of the taxonomy says what this relation is, and on which plane? If only unspecific association fits, what does the definition leave unsaid?
  3. State the kind of each end: What kind of class is each end (the taxonomy of class kinds), and does the kind rule of the category allow that pair? A relation between a human being and a device, a class and a table, or a process and a quality is checked here.
  4. Reuse before inventing: Does a standard relation (the Relations Ontology, PROV-O, Dublin Core terms, SKOS, OWL-Time, OWL itself) already say it? Use it; create a new one only when none fits.
  5. Apply a design pattern: Is there a design pattern for it (part-of, participation, n-ary relation, time-indexed relation, mapping)? Follow it.
  6. Declare it fully: What are its domain and range, its characteristics, its facets and its inverse or reciprocal (only if needed)? For a subsumption: do rigidity, identity and unity hold?
  7. Carry every rule as a link: Does any ownership, cardinality, sequence, initiator, starter or finisher live only in a shape, a text or a string? If so, the model needs a link for it (the owner's ruling of 2026-10-20).
  8. Test drive when unsure: If the grouping is unsure, build scratch variants and run the competency question on each before deciding.
  9. Check and record: Run the detection of connectedness (BP-D56) and of missing links (BP-D57); record the category on the relation.

THE TAXONOMY. The categories of relationship are the individuals of kb:RelationshipCategory, placed under one another by skos:broader. Four groups come from the owner's proposal of 2026-10-20 (syntactic, structural, semantic, temporal) and five more are what the literature keeps apart (provenance and derivation, correspondence and identity, epistemic and argumentative, normative, requirement and traceability): syntactic relationship, structural relationship, semantic relationship, temporal relationship, provenance and derivation, correspondence and identity, epistemic and argumentative relationship, normative relationship, requirement and traceability. The semantic group has nine sub-categories (taxonomic, compositional, spatial and topological, characterization and dependence, participation and case role, causal, intentional, similarity and opposition, unspecific association); the syntactic group three, the structural four and the temporal three. Seven facets cut across the categories and say how a relationship is built (plane, level of generality, formal or material, arity, logical characteristics, time indexing, universality). Every category is grounded in sources that were opened and read; none is taken from memory.

THE PLANES. A relationship lies on the representation plane (about the artifact: words, files, versions, citations) or on the domain plane (about the things the model stands for), or on both. The same word can name both: a version of a file is representation, a release that follows a release is domain.

THE FIRST FOUR ARE ENFORCED. The structural category is checked by connectedness (BP-D56); the syntactic, semantic and temporal categories are the three families of the missing-link ceremony (BP-D57). The further groups are not yet detected; they are named so that a relationship that belongs to them is not forced into one of the four.

A RELATION THAT FITS NO CATEGORY but unspecific association is a prompt to look again, not a license: the definition says what the association leaves unsaid. A rule that lives only in a shape (an ownership, a cardinality), and a sequence, initiator, starter or finisher, are relationships of the model and are carried as links; an inverse is made only if it is useful or needed; when a grouping is unsure, scratch variants are built and tested before the choice is made (the owner's ruling of 2026-10-20)."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

