        kb:ClassKind_Proposition,
        kb:ClassKind_Relation,
        kb:ClassKind_Concept,
        kb:ClassKind_Category,
        kb:ClassKind_Organism,
        kb:ClassKind_BodyStructure,
        kb:ClassKind_Cell,
        kb:ClassKind_Tissue,
        kb:ClassKind_Organ,
        kb:ClassKind_Population,
        kb:ClassKind_Ecosystem,
        kb:ClassKind_Specimen,
        kb:ClassKind_ElementaryParticle,
        kb:ClassKind_ChemicalSubstance,
        kb:ClassKind_Element,
        kb:ClassKind_Compound,
        kb:ClassKind_Mixture,
        kb:ClassKind_SpatialRegion,
        kb:ClassKind_TemporalRegion,
        kb:ClassKind_ExtensiveQuality,
        kb:ClassKind_IntensiveQuality,
        kb:ClassKind_LegalPerson,
        kb:ClassKind_Norm,
        kb:ClassKind_LegalDocument,
        kb:ClassKind_MasterData,
        kb:ClassKind_DataElement,
        kb:ClassKind_ValueDomain,
        kb:ClassKind_DataType,
        kb:ClassKind_AbstractDataType,
        kb:ClassKind_MathematicalObject,
        kb:ClassKind_Project,
        kb:ClassKind_DecisionMaking,
        kb:ClassKind_Decision,
        kb:ClassKind_SoftwareTesting,
        kb:ClassKind_TestCase,
        kb:ClassKind_TestSuite,
        kb:ClassKind_TestPlan,
        kb:ClassKind_Metric,
        kb:ClassKind_Measurement,
        kb:ClassKind_Defect,
        kb:ClassKind_SoftwareError,
        kb:ClassKind_Failure,
        kb:ClassKind_Drift,
        kb:ClassFacet_Concreteness,
        kb:ClassFacet_Persistence,
        kb:ClassFacet_Dependence,
        kb:ClassFacet_Materiality,
        kb:ClassFacet_Rigidity,
        kb:ClassFacet_Origin,
        kb:ClassFacet_Sociality,
        kb:ClassFacet_Agency,
        kb:ClassFacet_Composition,
        kb:ClassFacet_Level,
        kb:ClassFacet_Stratum,
        kb:ClassFacet_Kindhood,
        kb:ClassFacet_OrganisationLevel,
        kb:ClassFacet_Additivity,
        kb:KindRule_TaxonomicSame,
        kb:KindRule_CompositionalSame,
        kb:KindRule_ConstitutionCross,
        kb:KindRule_InherenceDependent,
        kb:KindRule_ParticipationEndurantProcess,
        kb:KindRule_ParticipationRange,
        kb:KindRule_SpatialContinuants,
        kb:KindRule_TemporalOrderOccurrents,
        kb:KindRule_ProvenanceEntityActivityAgent,
        kb:KindRule_NormativePartyAsset,
        kb:KindRule_RequirementArtifactSpecification,
        kb:KindRule_EpistemicInformation,
        kb:KindRule_SyntacticExpressions,
        kb:KindRule_CorrespondenceModelElements,
        kb:KindRule_VersioningResources,
        kb:KindRule_MembershipCollective,
        kb:KindRule_SequenceOccurrents,
        kb:KindRule_CausalOccurrents,
        kb:KindRule_ExtensiveQualityBearer,
        kb:KindRule_MeasurementAttribute ;
    skos:definition """Every class of a model has a kind in the taxonomy of class kinds, and a relationship fits the kinds of its two ends. A human being is not a device; a class is not a table; a process is not a quality; a parthood link does not cross from a continuant to a process. Without kinds, a relationship can be declared between any two classes and the model cannot say which pairs make sense.

THE TAXONOMY OF CLASS KINDS. The kinds are the individuals of kb:ClassKind, placed under one another by skos:broader, under the root Entity (84 kinds). Entity splits into concrete entity (continuant, occurrent, presential), abstract entity (set or class, proposition, relation, concept, category), social object and mental object. Medicine adds the organism, the body structure (cell, tissue, organ), the specimen and the substance; physics the elementary particle; chemistry the pure substance (element, compound) and the mixture; the qualities split into extensive and intensive; law the legal person, the norm and the legal document; data standards the master data, the data element, the value domain and the data type; mathematics the mathematical object; software engineering the project, decision-making and the decision, testing (test case, test suite, test plan), the metric and the measurement, the defect, the error, the failure and drift; library science the regions of space and time. A name that can mean an activity or its result (testing and a test, decision-making and a decision) is told apart by its kind: the activity is an occurrent, the result a continuant. The continuants include independent continuants (material entities: physical objects, natural objects, artifacts, devices; agents: human beings, software agents, organizations; aggregates; amounts of matter; immaterial entities such as sites) and dependent continuants (qualities, roles, dispositions, features). Information entities (generically dependent, copiable) carry the software and data kinds: information artifact, document, specification, code, program, software system, software product, software copy, software structural unit (class, method, attribute and the rest of a modelling language), database element (database, table, record, attribute, key) and model element.

THE FACETS. 14 facets cut across the kinds, each with a short list of values (kb:ClassFacet, kb:ClassFacetValue): abstract or concrete, persistence, dependence, materiality, rigidity, natural or made, brute or social, agency, composition, level of classification (Aristotle's said-of and present-in, the particular and the category), stratum of reality (the inorganic, the organic, the psychical, the cultural), natural or nominal kind, level of biological organisation (atom to biosphere), and extensive or intensive (physics and chemistry). A kind carries the values that describe it (kb:hasFacetValue) and none for a facet it does not settle. Mobile or immobile was the owner's example; biology treats sessility as a lack of self-locomotion that changes within a life cycle (a sponge moves as a larva and not as an adult), so it is a state or a capability of an individual and not a kind of class, and the facet is dropped. The same reasoning keeps states of matter (solid, liquid, gas, plasma) out of the kinds.

THE KIND RULES. A kb:KindRule says, for a category of the relationship taxonomy (BP-D58), which kinds it allows at which end (kb:requiresKind, kb:atEnd) and on which facets the two ends must agree (kb:requiresAgreementOn). 20 rules are recorded, each grounded in the sources: subsumption keeps persistence, concreteness and rigidity; parthood stays within one persistence; constitution joins a thing and its base; inherence joins a dependent thing and its bearer; participation joins a continuant and an occurrent; spatial relations join continuants and temporal order joins occurrents; provenance joins entities, activities and agents; a norm binds agents; satisfy and verify join an artifact and a specification; support, attack and citation join information; syntactic relations join expressions; mappings join model elements. Categories with no rule have no restriction that a source states; that is not a license, it is a gap to close.

RECORDING. A class records its kind with kb:hasClassKind. The kind classifies the class; it does not make it a subclass of anything, so a package restructures nothing. The construction method of BP-D58 gains the step 'state the kind of each end', between choosing the category and reusing a standard relation.

LIMITS, STATED. (1) Where a source lists a term and does not define it (the modelling language's structural classes, the relational model's terms, the device), placing it under information artifact or artifact is the method's reading, and the definition says so. (2) Whether natural kinds are independent of the mind is disputed, and taxonomic ranks are largely conventional; the natural-or-nominal facet records a position, not a fact. (3) A test on the method's own 1103 classes by the head noun of the name found a kind for 445 of them, 151 ambiguous (a test is an activity or a case; a method is a software unit or a methodology) and the rest unreviewed. Kinds are recorded so far on 48 classes and categories on 26 properties of the method's vocabulary, only where the name and the definition agree (the ledger is oe-pack structure-debt KIND_CATEGORY_RECORDING_v1_0_0.tsv); the head noun alone was refused for drift (those classes are pathologies of an agent, not changes of data), for failure mode (kinds of failure, not the event) and for testing (methodologies, not the activity). No detector yet finds a relationship whose ends break a kind rule; both are later steps, the second as a family of the missing-link check with a baseline that may only fall."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D60_ANameMeansOneThingAndAThingHasOnePreferredName a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D60: A name means one thing and a thing has one preferred name; synonyms are labels, homonyms are told apart by the preferred label, and related senses are separate things"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D60"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's request of 2026-10-20 (synonyms, homonyms and the lexical concerns of the taxonomies) and the literature research of that date, recorded in RELATIONSHIP_TAXONOMY_RESEARCH_v1_11_0.md."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D58_ARelationshipIsConstructedFromANeedAndACategoryNotImprovised,
        kb:BestPractice_D57_MissingLinksAreDiscoveredDecidedAndSetNeverLeftAsText,
        kb:BestPractice_D59_EveryClassHasAKindAndARelationshipFitsTheKindsOfItsEnds,
        kb:BestPractice_D61_OneMeaningOneTermADuplicateIsMergedNotLinkedAndTheOtherNamesAreLabels,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_LexicalVariant ;
    kb:hasTaxonomyEntry kb:RelationshipCategory_Synonymy,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_NearSynonymy,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Homonymy,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_Polysemy ;
    skos:definition """A name means one thing, and a thing has one preferred name. Synonyms are labels, never repeated definitions; homonyms are told apart by the preferred label; senses that are related but different are separate things with sense-specific labels. The research record RELATIONSHIP_TAXONOMY_RESEARCH_v1_11_0.md (8 sources opened and read on 2026-10-20) gives the grounds; the knowledge base carries the four lexical categories as data (kb:RelationshipCategory: synonymy, near-synonymy, homonymy, polysemy, under lexical variant).

THE RULES.
  L1. One preferred label per thing and language. The label is the rdfs:label (or skos:prefLabel); a thing with two preferred labels in one language is inconsistent (SKOS reference; OBO naming conventions).
  L2. Alternative names are synonyms of the thing and are held on it: skos:altLabel for abbreviations, acronyms, full forms, spelling variants and translations; skos:hiddenLabel for forms that are searched and not shown. The three label kinds are disjoint: the same string is not preferred and alternative at once.
  L3. Two things (two classes, or two properties) of one vocabulary with the same meaning are one thing: they are merged and the other name becomes a label (BP-D61). Two things that overlap without being interchangeable in every sense are linked by skos:closeMatch (symmetric, not transitive); skos:exactMatch is kept for equal things that must live in different vocabularies.
  L4. Two unrelated things must not share a preferred label within one kind of thing (two classes, two properties). A class and a property that share a label are told apart by their kind and are allowed. Homonyms are given separate labels, qualified by the sense.
  L5. Related senses of one form are separate things, each with a sense-specific label and its own definition, and a link that says how they relate; how finely the senses are divided is decided by the competency question and not by the dictionary. A name that can mean an activity or its result (testing and a test) is two things, told apart by their kind (BP-D59).
  L6. A definition that names the term it was consolidated from is a statement of derivation, and the derivation is a link (prov:wasDerivedFrom), not text.

WHAT WAS MEASURED (the method vocabulary, 2026-10-20). Of 1642 labelled classes and properties, 153 labels are shared by more than one thing (317 things); no thing has two preferred labels in one language. Every pair of the same kind with the same label was read. 78 pairs carry the same definition word for word (all between the testing and the reliability vocabulary, left by an earlier consolidation). 38 pairs mean the same in overlapping scope. 18 definitions name the term they were consolidated from. 6 pairs are homonyms, one label for different things: class richness (instances per class, and the share of classes that have instances), architectural coherence score and consistency score (of an ontology, and of the outputs of a language model), and the properties constrains, assesses artifact and produces artifact (each used in two vocabularies for different pairs of things). 1 pair is polysemy (evolution: a process of change, and a lifecycle phase). 1 pair cannot be judged because one side has no definition.

WHAT IS DONE AND WHAT IS NOT. The overlapping pairs are linked by skos:closeMatch and the consolidations by prov:wasDerivedFrom; the pairs of equal meaning were first linked by exact match and on 2026-10-21 merged (BP-D61). The homonyms, the polysemy and the pair with no definition on one side carry, since 2026-10-21, a preferred label that names their sense (class richness as the share of classes with instances, and class richness as instances per class, for example), with the shared label kept as a hidden label; the method vocabulary is the source of its modules, so the label is changed where the term is authored. The ledger (LEXICAL_LEDGER_v1_3_0.tsv) carries each with its reason. The detector for terms of one kind that share a label and are not linked is the duplicate family of the missing-link check (BP-D61, rule S6), with a baseline that may only fall."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .

kb:BestPractice_D61_OneMeaningOneTermADuplicateIsMergedNotLinkedAndTheOtherNamesAreLabels a kb:BestPractice,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BP-D61: One meaning, one term: a duplicate is merged and the other names become labels, not linked as a second class"@en ;
    kb:hasBestPracticeId "BP-D61"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    dcterms:source "The owner's ruling of 2026-10-21 (real equals are single classes with multiple labels, not connected classes, for the efficiency and drift risks) and the analysis recorded in RELATIONSHIP_TAXONOMY_RESEARCH_v1_11_0.md."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:BestPractice_D60_ANameMeansOneThingAndAThingHasOnePreferredName,
        kb:BestPractice_D59_EveryClassHasAKindAndARelationshipFitsTheKindsOfItsEnds,
        kb:BestPractice_D57_MissingLinksAreDiscoveredDecidedAndSetNeverLeftAsText,
        kb:BestPractice_D52_SearchBeforeCreatingArtifact,
        kb:Lesson_L_37_DRYAttributionCompleteness ;
    kb:hasTaxonomyEntry kb:RelationshipCategory_Synonymy,
        kb:RelationshipCategory_NearSynonymy ;
    skos:definition """One meaning, one term. A class or a property that means the same as another in the same vocabulary is not a second term: it is a label. The surviving term carries every name (one preferred label, the others as alternative labels) and the duplicate is removed in the same release, with every reference redirected. Two copies of one meaning cost twice to maintain and drift apart: the testing and the reliability copies of 78 terms carried one definition and different places in the hierarchy. The research record RELATIONSHIP_TAXONOMY_RESEARCH_v1_11_0.md gives the analysis; the rule extends BP-D52 (consolidate before creating), the provenance lesson L-37 (do not repeat), and BP-D60 (a name means one thing).

THE RULES.
  S1. Same meaning is proved, not guessed. Proof is (a) the same definition word for word, or (b) a survivor that is declared the successor: its provenance or its own definition names the term it replaced, or (c) a ruling of the owner on a pair whose definitions describe one concept in the standards the terms cite, recorded in the ledger with that evidence (2026-10-21: the performance tests and the failure type). Overlap of words, the same label, or a declared exact match alone prove nothing.
  S2. Same use. For a property: the same domain and range after the merge (a property that declares none is weaker and folds into the one that does). For a class: where the survivor is the declared successor, its place in the hierarchy stands and the retired term's parents are superseded. Where the use differs and no successor is declared, the two are close and not equal (BP-D60): they stay, linked by derivation or by skos:closeMatch, and the difference is a decision of the owner of the terms.
  S3. The survivor is the declared successor; otherwise the term used more widely in the vocabulary; otherwise the term of the more general vocabulary.
  S4. The merge is one change. The retired term's label is kept as an alternative label where it differs; its examples, further reading and sources move to the survivor; every reference in the T-Box, the A-Box and the shapes names the survivor; a shape that becomes identical to the survivor's is dropped; the survivor keeps prov:wasDerivedFrom to the retired term, so the retirement is a link; the change note of every file says what was merged. A stage never ships half of it.
  S5. skos:exactMatch is for equal things that must live in different vocabularies, for example an alignment to an outside source. Inside one vocabulary a declared exact match between two terms is a defect to be merged; an exact match on a subclass pair whose members differ in scope (agent, artifact, quality metric, severity level) is wrong and is removed; a pair that is also a subclass pair but has the same scope is merged under S1(c); a note that one term calls the other distinct is weighed against the two definitions, and where they describe one concept the terms are merged.
  S6. The detector is the fourth family of the missing-link check (duplicate), run by the publisher and by CI: two terms of one kind (two classes or two properties) in one file set are a high-trust duplicate when they have the same definition word for word (a definition of at least 30 characters), or an exact match is declared between them, or one is derived from the other by a consolidation that left both declared, or they share a preferred label (any case) and nothing links them (no exact or close match, subclass, subproperty, equivalence or derivation). A class and a property may share a label. Each pair is counted once, under the strongest rule. The count may only fall against the recorded baseline; the pairs that are judged not to be duplicates are recorded with a reason in the decisions ledger.

WHAT WAS MEASURED AND DONE (the method vocabulary, 2026-10-21). 78 classes and properties of the reliability vocabulary were merged into their testing successors (same definition, the testing term the declared migration successor, every one with its derivation recorded). 7 terms that a risk term consolidated were merged into it (the same use). 12 further terms that a risk, quality or configuration term consolidated were first kept apart because their domain or range differed; on the owner's ruling of the same day they were merged as well (mitigation, probability, cost, impact, identified risk, severity in three vocabularies, severity rank, priority). Severity and priority belong to many kinds of thing, so their domain is the universal one; the severity written as the words low, medium and high in the reliability vocabulary became the severity level of the risk vocabulary, the scale that class declares, and the 4 quality severity levels were merged into the risk levels. The reliability failure type was merged into the risk failure type (the risk term declares that it consolidated it and both define a classification of failures by temporal and behavioural characteristics, so the note that called the reliability term distinct was not supported). The 4 performance test classes were merged into their testing classes (a declared exact match, also a subclass pair, of equal scope; the placement under the test taxonomy and the measurement subject were kept). 4 declared exact matches were removed (agent, artifact, quality metric and severity level: in each a subclass pair). The duplicate detector then found no duplicate in the method vocabulary (24 before).

LIMITS, STATED. The detector reads each file set alone, so two terms in different file sets are never compared; the duplicates recorded as debt are terms authored in other packages that this package only registers or reads (the registrations of three packages 4: the member and invariant status properties; the ecosystem vocabulary 17) and fall when the owners of those terms rename or merge them; the terms outside the method vocabulary that name a merged term by its old name (the shapes of another package, for example) are not known to this check, and none was found in the repository."""@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-21T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_MethodAuthor .
